HT_DATA.load("detail", {"xAoljeRJ3lU":{"wy":"HN's go-to recommendation for colormap and color-perception questions; commenters call it funny, brief, clear and a favorite talk.","q":{"x":"This hilarious SciPy2015 talk[1] convinced me to switch to the Parula color scheme.","i":17734362,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23427719,"author":"azepoi","month":"2020-06","story":"How to Avoid Equidistant HSV Colors (2011)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presentation on perceptually uniform colormaps","quote":"I have to mention this fantastic presentation on colormaps","text":"I'm impressed by the other mentioned picker, for HCL this time [LINK] This tool is also useful [LINK] I have to mention this fantastic presentation on colormaps [VIDEO] (A Better Default Colormap for Matplotlib, SciPy 2015, Nathaniel Smith and Stéfan van der Walt). It is about perceptually uniform colormaps. [LINK]"},{"id":21273861,"author":"neilpanchal","month":"2019-10","story":"Designing Accessible Color Systems","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presentation on Matplotlib's new perceptually uniform colormaps","quote":"here is an amazing presentation of Matplotlib's new colormaps","text":"I built similar perceptually uniform color library: [LINK] The sad part is that we have all these colors available in the physical world but representing in sRGB (or even Adobe RGB or DCI P3) space takes away all the fun. We have a narrow space to work within and it poses limits to what you can do. Vast majority of monitors in the world are barely capable of representing sRGB space, and even if they are, calibration is another issue. You can employ all kinds of science in representing proper colors but if the end user's calibration is not good, all bets are off. Colors are a problem for interface design (and accessibility), it becomes a real challenge when dealing with quantitative data and build perceptually uniform colormaps with accessibility considerations, here is an amazing presentation of Matplotlib's new colormaps: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20754624,"author":"kbd","month":"2019-08","story":"Turbo, an Improved Rainbow Colormap for Visualization","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presentation where Viridis and Inferno were first presented","quote":"I’d strongly recommend watching this presentation at SciPy 2015 where Viridis and Inferno were first presented","text":"If you have any interest in this topic, I’d strongly recommend watching this presentation at SciPy 2015 where Viridis and Inferno were first presented, when the developers of Matplotlib were coming up with a new default colormap to replace Jet: [VIDEO] I'd love to see Turbo analyzed in the same fashion."},{"id":20754712,"author":"spectramax","month":"2019-08","story":"Turbo, an Improved Rainbow Colormap for Visualization","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains Jet shortcomings that led to Viridis","quote":"These shortcomings were the exact reason why Viridis was created as exemplified in this brilliant talk","text":"> Today we are happy to introduce Turbo, a new colormap that has the desirable properties of Jet while also addressing some of its shortcomings, such as false detail, banding and color blindness ambiguity. What are the desirable properties of Jet? Why not use Viridis or Magma or any of the recent linear colormaps from matplotlib 2.0? These shortcomings were the exact reason why Viridis was created as exemplified in this brilliant talk: [VIDEO] I don't understand the point of Turbo. > Turbo mimics the lightness profile of Jet, going from low to high back down to low, without banding. As such, its lightness slope is generally double that of Viridis, allowing subtle changes to be more easily seen. This is a valuable feature, since it greatly enhances detail when color can be used to disambiguate the low and high ends. Why would anyone want low-to-high-to-low colormap? > Viridis is a linear color map that is generally recommended when false color is needed because it is pleasant to the eye and it fixes most issues with Jet. Inferno has the same linear properties of Viridis, but is higher contrast, making it better for picking out detail. However, some feel that it can be harsh on the eyes. While this isn’t a concern for publishing, it does affect people’s choice when they must spend extended periods examining visualizations. Personal taste vs scientific accuracy - I will take the latter unless we are doing plots for marketing materials. In my view this is not substantiated reason"},{"id":19626142,"author":"Mizza","month":"2019-04","story":"Unveiling the first-ever image of a black hole [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Perceptually uniform colour schemes","quote":"One of my favorite talks ever is on this subject","text":"Grrr, why aren't scientists all using perceptually uniforms color schemes! This would still look awesome in `plasma`. Is there any where I can get the data and do it myself? One of my favorite talks ever is on this subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17734362,"author":"vitalique","month":"2018-08","story":"New Map Scale Is More Readable by People Who Are Color Blind","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hilarious talk convincing viewers to abandon Jet colormap","quote":"This hilarious SciPy2015 talk[1] convinced me to switch to the Parula color scheme.","text":"I am not colorblind, but work with data visualization in Matlab a lot. This hilarious SciPy2015 talk[1] convinced me to switch to the Parula color scheme. There's no way I'm going back to Jet. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49307601,"author":"carpdiem","month":"2026-08","story":"Show HN: Ember – Redshift safe color palettes","stance":"rec","why":"How people work with color maps in practice","quote":"I really like this talk from SciPy 2015 to get a sense of how people work with and talk about color maps in practice","text":"That's a... surprisingly good question. I'm actually a physicist by background first, so I got my start doing waaayyy too many calculations with blackbody and emissions spectra. Then, back in 2010, when I was just getting into startups, I spent two weeks straight at Powell's in Portland reading through their graphic design section. After that, I never got very far away from light or perception... AR/VR -> graphics -> silicon photonics -> physical AI (now). I'm still very much learning, but if you like this stuff and want to learn more yourself there's a couple sources I can recommend: - Interaction of Color, by Josef Albers. Amazing book, but you really want to get a physical copy. - I really like this talk from SciPy 2015 to get a sense of how people work with and talk about color maps in practice: [VIDEO] - This is a really, really thorough (and beautifully illustrated) article on what color spaces are: [LINK] - And last, I wrote a very technical blog post a few years back, if you want to see some math on a bunch of this: [LINK]"},{"id":42735377,"author":"qwertox","month":"2025-01","story":"Issues with color spaces and perceptual brightness","stance":"rec","why":"How Matplotlib got its default colormap","quote":"here's a good video about how Matplotlib ended up having `vividris` as its default colormap","text":"If this submission has put you into a mindset of wanting to know more about colormaps, here's a good video about how Matplotlib ended up having `vividris` as its default colormap: A Better Default Colormap for Matplotlib | SciPy 2015 | Nathaniel Smith and Stéfan van der Walt [VIDEO]"},{"id":37983405,"author":"quietbritishjim","month":"2023-10","story":"Why Japan Has Blue Traffic Lights Instead of Green","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the creators of the viridis colour map","quote":"Here's a talk by the creators","text":"Depending on what exactly your figures were/are, you might benefit from the viridis colour map, available in matplotlib and R (and probably in many other places). It's colourful, so those without colour blindness get a richer display than if you used greyscale, and the colours chosen aren't too affected by most common forms of colour blindness. But for those that really can't see the colours, even if you literally just convert to monochrome, it still works. It also looks nice :-) Here's a talk by the creators: [VIDEO] Here's a little article about it (with some R specifics): [LINK]"},{"id":27697406,"author":"CorrectHorseBat","month":"2021-07","story":"Rainbow Color Map Still Considered Harmful (2007) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows distortion of jet versus viridis via derivatives","quote":"watch the whole video if you have the time","text":"You're increasing the discriminability at the cost of high _local_ distortion, I don't see how that is a good tradeoff. As a comparison, what would you rather have: a non-distorted plot or a plot that is on average about three times as big, but it's heavily distorted. At some places it's smaller than the original and on other places it's 5 times bigger. Would you really choose the bigger figure? That is what jet is. Look at this plot [VIDEO] (watch the whole video if you have the time), ideally you want the derivatives (the 2 plots on the left top) to be constant so not to distort your data. Jet is all over the place while viridis is basically constant (not shown in the slides, but the derivative stays all the way around 120)."},{"id":26499505,"author":"maliker","month":"2021-03","story":"Which color scale to use when visualizing data","stance":"rec","why":"How viridis replaced jet and why colormaps matter","quote":"There’s a great talk [1] on how SciPy chose their new default colormap viridis","text":"There’s a great talk [1] on how SciPy chose their new default colormap viridis to replace the rainbow colored former default “jet”. Turns out that there have been good studies on how the wrong colormap can lead to bad decisions in medicine, and designing a replacement took a lot of careful work. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18219344,"author":"cosmic_quanta","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Good brief technical talk, about 20 minutes","quote":"This talk is made better by the fact that it's rather brief (~20min).","text":"A Better Default Colormap for Matplotlib ( [VIDEO] ) This talk is made better by the fact that it's rather brief (~20min)."},{"id":16796977,"author":"hprotagonist","month":"2018-04","story":"Color: From Hex codes to Eyeballs","stance":"rec","why":"Engrossing talk on designing colormaps for matplotlib","quote":"i watched this pydata talk on designing colormaps for matplotlib that was pretty engrossing","text":"Color is way more fascinating than I had previously thought. A few years ago, i watched this pydata talk on designing colormaps for matplotlib that was pretty engrossing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15572555,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-10","story":"Sorting Algorithms Revisualized","stance":"rec","why":"Explains colour theory simply and colour spaces for colourblindness","quote":"actually spends a good ten minutes on explaining colour theory, and makes it look very simple in the process","text":"There is the cube-helix approach, but I suspect you're thinking about something else. The linked talk about the creation of the viridis colour map[0] actually spends a good ten minutes on explaining colour theory, and makes it look very simple in the process. About ten minutes in it shows the \"perceptual colour space\" of a typical non-colourblind person. A few minutes later it shows the color spaces for people with various types of colourblindness. Perhaps one could project one perceptual space into the other in a way that more-or-less preserves the original information, but essentially with more banding? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11440241,"author":"elsherbini","month":"2016-04","story":"HCL: a color model that actually matches our perceptions (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Details of physical perception and picking colors","quote":"goes into some details about physical perception and what it means for picking colors","text":"This talk from the matplotlib people on making a better color map goes into some details about physical perception and what it means for picking colors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31654620,"author":"dwdz","month":"2022-06","story":"Benchmarking Malloc with Doom 3","stance":"reference","why":"Why to avoid Jet and use viridis","quote":"","text":"Please don't use Jet color scheme. Matplotlib uses excellent viridis by default. There's no reason not to use it. [0][1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25647617,"author":"steerablesafe","month":"2021-01","story":"Ditherpunk: The article I wish I had about monochrome image dithering","stance":"reference","why":"Source for learning about perceptual color spaces","quote":"","text":"> I don't get what you mean, linearity (dL'star') being defined wrt perceptual uniformity, isn't CIELAB 76 linear by definition (to an approximation) ?? And color arithmetic pretty much by definition implies dealing with distances and angles in a perceptually uniform color space, doesn't it ?? No. Linearity is about physical light intensities relating to perceived color. Imagine having two light sources on the same spot that you can turn on or off separately. If you turn the first one on you perceive a certain color, if you turn the other on you perceive an other color and if you turn both on you perceive a third one. It turns out the perceivable colors (under normal viewing conditions) are representable in a three dimensional vector-space (for most people) so that the first two colors add up to the third color for every possible two light sources. Such a linear colorspace is XYZ for example [1]. This has nothing to do with perceptual uniformity. Perceptual uniformity is about the capability of distinguishing near colors. This defines a distance between colors, and there are three dimensional colorspace representations where the Euclidean distance approximate this perceptual distance well. cieLAB is such a colorspace, but AFAIK there are better state of the art colorspaces for the same purpose. I'm not very well versed in this, I learned from them from this video [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] edit: gimp 2.10 now defaults to use a linear colorspace (not perceptually uniform!) fo"},{"id":20008969,"author":"no_identd","month":"2019-05","story":"Please don’t theme our apps","stance":"reference","why":"Colormap generation approach in Matplotlib","quote":"","text":"The color change-inducing-friction complaint seems like an issue with lack of built-in theme sanity checking. There exist numerous partial solutions for this already, I'll link to the four best ones I know, albeit I wish someone would combine their approaches into one unified super-product: 1. [LINK] These palettes will absolutely NEVER clash. But they might have contrast issues. Which brings us to: 2. [LINK] Lyft primarily designed ColorBox.io to always reach WCAG 2.0 contrast ratios. Something I'd claim a ton of UX themes mess up. Unfortunately from what I can tell, it lacks the anti-clash guarantees of #1 3. Much less related, but still important, albeit only for very specific parts of any UX: The color map generation approach in Matplotlib: [VIDEO] (btw.: Seaborn, as mentioned in #1, builds on top of Matplotlib.) For even more color madness, see this recent Hacker News comment by me as well as the other comments linked to there: [LINK]"},{"id":16343367,"author":"memco","month":"2018-02","story":"Show HN: Automatic detection of non-colorblind safe colors","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on information display for Matplotlib","quote":"","text":"Your comment reminded me of the talks on the issue of information display for Matplotlib[0][1]. I think a lot of design is done without awareness of accessibility and this is more broadly a cultural thing than a technical thing. The situation can be improved both by technical means (OS or hardware awareness) as well as by our collective education about what kinds of issues designs should consider. Typically, designs become better when they account for accessibility, but it is oftentimes seen as a hurdle when a designer or developer has not heard about the issues and also is not equipped with tools to handle them. Having good tools is helpful here. The situation seems to be improving, but it will take time. When I was in elementary school, you had to special order large print text books, audio books were available, but required special tapes & players. A portable CCTV could be used to magnify small print. All of this equipment was specialized, expensive and hard to come by for most people. All of this has been replaced by features built into my phone. Special shoutouts to the \"Zoom\" app on iOS, that thing has color filters for people with color sensitivity issues and enhanced text rendering for small print (unfortunately, it's buggy and crashes sometimes), but it is much better than littering my camera roll with random pictures of menus or the bottom of routers just so I can zoom in on some numbers. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":9890555,"author":"icegreentea","month":"2015-07","story":"Digital colours and digital photography","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on finding a new default matplotlib colormap","quote":"I just watched a Scipy conference talk/video about this - it's about finding a new default color map for matplotlib","text":"Funny, I just watched a Scipy conference talk/video about this - it's about finding a new default color map for matplotlib [1] CIE LAB is basically a ~30 year old model based on human perception - basically it takes into account white point adjustment and the non-linear human brightness response, and thats about it. There are newer (and faaar more complicated) models like CIECAM02. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["LKnqECcg6Gw",1],["J44svaQc5WY",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["bKGhvKyjgLY",1],["zeLToGnjIUM",1]]},"7Pq-S557XQU":{"wy":"HN has recommended it for a decade as the standard explainer on automation and unemployment, though skeptics later cite it as an example of overconfident predictions.","q":{"x":"Excellent video on the subject: CGPGrey - Humans Need Not Apply","i":10296165,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":13240805,"author":"noetic_techy","month":"2016-12","story":"The AI Unemployment Anti-FAQ (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Debunks historical analogies about automation.","quote":"I always refer people to this video to explain what it will be like","text":"This article just leans on the same historic analogies that don't hold up to scrutiny. These answers fall way short of the effect of automation and AI will have. You are not replacing the job with another, you are replacing the human brain! I always refer people to this video to explain what it will be like, and it debases almost all the points made in this FAQ: [VIDEO] All these historical analogies fall flat on their face when it comes to an AI being trained to do your exact job. This is not another case of farmers moving to different higher skilled jobs. We are the employed horses of the 1800s about to be made unemployable by the advent of cars."},{"id":10506415,"author":"csl","month":"2015-11","story":"The Most Common Job in Each State 1978-2014","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Strongly recommended video on automation.","quote":"which I strongly recommend, if you haven't already seen it","text":"This reminded me of the \"Humans need not apply\" video, which I strongly recommend, if you haven't already seen it: [VIDEO] Discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":10296165,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2015-09","story":"Amazon Flex","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent video on job automation.","quote":"Excellent video on the subject: CGPGrey - Humans Need Not Apply","text":"And people who refuse to see it are usually blind... The supermarkets I go to have greatly reduced their cashiers. In most cases by at least half, especially in small shops (3 cashiers has become 2-3 machines and one human). As for bakers, they don't just make bread and cake. Excellent video on the subject: CGPGrey - Humans Need Not Apply: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9402969,"author":"polymatter","month":"2015-04","story":"The Machines Are Coming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Good overview of automation and unemployment.","quote":"I highly recommend CGP Greys video","text":"I highly recommend CGP Greys video ( [VIDEO] ) for a good overview of the issue. See [LINK] for similar discussion."},{"id":8739511,"author":"zyx321","month":"2014-12","story":"Artificial Intelligence Isn’t a Threat – Yet","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great video on AI making jobs redundant.","quote":"CGP Grey did a great video on this[1].","text":"Easier said than done. What are they going to retrain to? As AI continues to advance, more and more jobs will necome redundant. CGP Grey did a great video on this[1]. Even if you can't completely replace humans, often times, obe person will be able to do the job that used to take several. Where do those others go? What will we as a society do if for example AI-based Expert Systems make 90% of accountants and office clerks redundant? We can't retrain all of them to become doctors and scientists, can we? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35519804,"author":"jabbany","month":"2023-04","story":"It happened to me today: $80/hr writer replaced with ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Prescient 2014 video on mental labor automation.","quote":"I think the point about automation as a displacement of mental labor is real.","text":"Reminds me of this video about automation of mental labor (and this was made back in 2014, way before any of of the LLM stuff of the modern era): [VIDEO] While not all the example predictions in the video were accurate, I think the point about automation as a displacement of mental labor is real. At the end of the day, a lot of white collar work is really not that \"valuable\" from a production standpoint. And while the easy way is to blame either technology or those it replaces, the crux of the problem is really in society and how individuals make a living. Society needs to be ready for a future where most people will not be able to do meaningfully differentiated work ---where even if you're thrown into higher education, most of the jobs available are still what would be considered as \"entry level\" ones. Also, this is also somewhat an attestation to Moravec's paradox ( [LINK] ), where \"intellectual\" tasks like writing are easier to automate compared to \"actuation\" tasks like fetching food, driving it around, and placing it in the right place (re: Doordash)."},{"id":32263306,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2022-07","story":"The Poor ROI of Autonomy (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at human replacement automation that holds up.","quote":"CGP Grey went in depth on human replacement automation, and almost a decade later it still holds up","text":"Tech makes jobs obsolete, but the new jobs introduced in the last few hundred years only add up to a small part of the overall workforce (programmers excluded). CGP Grey went in depth on human replacement automation, and almost a decade later it still holds up: [VIDEO] The bottom line is that automating a job is obviously a net positive because it frees up a human to do something else elsewhere, while still providing the same added value as before. There will always be something for that human to do, even if not a traditional style job. The gap, as you point out, is making sure that newly created value gets taxed and provided to that freed up person as UBI, so that they can continue to live and consume those products that the machine makes in the first place, otherwise there is no demand for them and capitalism doesn't work. There is no point in having an army of robots making hats if there's nobody to wear them."},{"id":12140338,"author":"dscalisi","month":"2016-07","story":"What If There Just Aren’t Enough Jobs to Go Around?","stance":"rec","why":"Premise that large swaths may become unemployable.","quote":"we need to start thinking about what to do when large swaths of the population find themselves unemployable through no fault of their own","text":"Which skills, for example, are harder to automate? You may have already seen this YouTube video as its a couple years old, but its premise is that we need to start thinking about what to do when large swaths of the population find themselves unemployable through no fault of their own (since over 45% of the workforce holds jobs that are easy targets for automation in the near future). [VIDEO]"},{"id":9131406,"author":"fizixer","month":"2015-03","story":"Coding is not the new literacy","stance":"rec","why":"Views on automation and purpose of education.","quote":"I strongly adhere to the views expressed in the video 'Humans Need Not Apply' by CGPGrey[1]","text":"You seem to be coming from the opposing view of that of the article, that coding 'is' the new literacy. I was more challenging the article's claim that modeling is the new literacy, which I think isn't. As to whether coding is the new literacy or not, I'd say we really need to ask what is the purpose of education. If the purpose of education is enlightenment, then yes, a child should learn how to code, but also how to paint, how to play a musical instrument, and so on and so forth. But then some children have the aptitude for one thing, some for another thing. But I think the purpose of education as a means of making the individual economically viable is becoming more and more futile with increased automation. (In this regard I strongly adhere to the views expressed in the video 'Humans Need Not Apply' by CGPGrey[1], and TED talks by Jeremy Howard[2], Erik Brynjolfsson[3], Andrew McAfee[4]). As to the need for coding so that humans could interact with machines, I think in the long run, this is also going to progress to the point where the machine would be aware of the abilities of the human it's dealing with and make it easy for the human to interact with itself. (EDIT 1: I should also add that interacting with machines is already happening at a massive scale when everyone has a smartphone in their back pocket. And yet when the software goes wrong, the inability of an average user to fix it is not the result of that user not being coding literate, but rather the insurmountabl"},{"id":8505250,"author":"abandonliberty","month":"2014-10","story":"1984 v. Brave New World","stance":"rec","why":"Compelling take on human obsolescence.","quote":"it's quite compelling.","text":"Human obsolescence wasn't on Huxley's mind. Instead of Deltas, Gammas, and Epsilons doing awful labour, they will do none. While CGP Grey has a very consumer-oriented view, it's quite compelling. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8239891,"author":"zanny","month":"2014-08","story":"Hal Finney being cryopreserved now","stance":"rec","why":"Argues much of workforce will be unemployable.","quote":"A huge portion of the workforce will be unemployable in our lifetimes","text":"A huge portion of the workforce will be unemployable in our lifetimes: [VIDEO] By the time the technology comes around the revive the cryopreserved, the rest of technological advancement will change the dynamic of human interaction so much we cannot estimate what the world will be like, but we can assume that with technology advanced enough to repair the damage from the freezing process, you would probably be entering a world of almost any possibility."},{"id":32399553,"author":"gnramires","month":"2022-08","story":"The Reluctant Prophet of Effective Altruism","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for economic obsolescence of humans.","quote":"(humans need not apply[2])","text":"Indeed not only fluid, but also open to criticism. I've been always quite unsure of how to quantify AI risk. My conclusion at the moment is that it's a complex risk. I don't think singularity is plausible in any way or FOOM. Still, the changes to society are absolutely hard to grasp. Humans may face (economical) obsolescence in a few decades[1] (humans need not apply[2]). That puts us in a delicate situation: if humans are economically obsolete, a society that only values economic value (and not consciousness per se ) will have all reasons to slowly but surely get rid of humans (and animals). We need a revolution of meaning and ethical understanding: the understanding of the fundamental value of consciousness, and to structure our lives and all institutions around it (each individual needs to have a good understanding of such ethics and meaning so that society as a whole is robust). I think formalization of ethics (which I'm a proponent of) and progress in metaphysics is going to contribute as well to this end. I think EA is simply part of this new understanding of ourselves and the Universe, a sort of coming out of a dark age -- and there's the opportunity to create a lasting society amazing for everyone. [1] My personal guess-estimate is about 50(-20/+40) years to complete obsolescence, although various researchers have wildly different estimates. [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26482504,"author":"nindalf","month":"2021-03","story":"Moore's Law for Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Horse analogy against new-jobs optimism.","quote":"There's no law of economics that says that better technology makes more better jobs for horses.","text":"> We will discover new jobs–we always do after a technological revolution CGP grey put this well > Imagine a pair of horses in the early 1900s talking about technology. One worries all these new mechanical muscles (cars etc) will make horses unnecessary. The other horse reminds him that everything so far has made their lives easier - remember all that farm work? Remember running coast-to-coast delivering mail? Remember riding into battle? All terrible! These new city jobs are pretty cushy, and with so many humans in the cities there will be more jobs for horses than ever. Even if this car thingy takes off, he might say, there will be new jobs for horses we can't imagine. > But you know what happened. There are still working horses, but nothing like before. The horse population peaked in 1915 - from that point on it was nothing but down. > There's no law of economics that says that better technology makes more better jobs for horses. It sounds shockingly dumb to even say that out loud. But swap horses for humans and suddenly people think it sounds about right. \"Humans need not apply\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":16786229,"author":"cryodesign","month":"2018-04","story":"Do You Trust This Computer? [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier 15-minute documentary on same theme.","quote":"nothing really new since the 'Humans need not apply' 15 min documentary [0]","text":"I was hoping for a bit more in-depth material - allowing the experts to explore their topics a bit more and potentially talking about potential solutions? Where is the call to action for the viewer? What now? It's good for non-technical folks to watch, but nothing really new since the 'Humans need not apply' 15 min documentary [0] [0] [VIDEO] (2014) Edit: added link to humans need not apply"},{"id":12991932,"author":"dfabulich","month":"2016-11","story":"Robots and industrialization in developing countries [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Popular video, contrast with Rules for Rulers.","quote":"Many of the folks here like CGP Grey's video on this topic","text":"Many of the folks here like CGP Grey's video on this topic, \"Humans Need Not Apply\" [VIDEO] But you should watch that in contrast with one of his newest videos, \"The Rules for Rulers\" [LINK] > The more the wealth of a nation comes from the productive citizens of the nation, the more the power gets spread out, and the more the ruler must maintain the quality of life for those citizens. The less, the less. > Now if a stable democracy becomes very poor, or if a resource that dwarfs the productivity of the citizens is found, the odds of this gamble change, and make it more possible for a small group to seize power."},{"id":12992415,"author":"wycx","month":"2016-11","story":"Robots and industrialization in developing countries [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Quotes the horse analogy.","quote":"Better technology makes more better jobs for horses","text":"The relevant quote from the CGP Grey video linked above: \"Better technology makes more better jobs for horses\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":9256554,"author":"marksbrown","month":"2015-03","story":"After Jobs Dry Up, What Then?","stance":"reference","why":"Argues new jobs may not fill the void.","quote":"CGP Grey covered a few of your points in his video 'humans need not apply'[1].","text":"CGP Grey covered a few of your points in his video 'humans need not apply'[1]. The main argument against new jobs filling the void as it were, was the prevalence of the kinds of jobs that most people currently perform and the lack of any industry requiring millions of people in the future. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48824339,"author":"lexandstuff","month":"2026-07","story":"No-One Escapes the Permanent Underclass","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I actually think people from the past would be surprised at how slow AI has progressed in the end.","text":"Ilya Sutskever said in 2017, whilst building OpenAI: > Within the next three years, robotics should be completely solved, AI should solve a long-standing unproven theorem, programming competitions should be won consistently by AIs, and there should be convincing chatbots. There was a viral YouTube video from 2014 called Humans Need Not Apply [1], which made a very strong argument that mass unemployment was right around the corner. I actually think people from the past would be surprised at how slow AI has progressed in the end. Although the domains where it's turned out to be most effective - code, images, videos, music, etc. - would probably surprise them. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41697502,"author":"Chathamization","month":"2024-09","story":"Gavin Newsom vetoes SB 1047","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The AI doomsday folk had an even worse track record over the past decade.","text":"The AI doomsday folk had an even worse track record over the past decade. There was supposed to be mass unemployment of truck drivers years ago. According to CCP Grey's Human's Need Not Apply[1] from 10 years ago, the robot Baxter was supposed to take over many low skilled jobs (Baxter was continued in 2018 after it failed to achieve commercial success). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38320784,"author":"bnralt","month":"2023-11","story":"Rogue superintelligence: Inside the mind of OpenAI's chief scientist","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"These people have a horrible track record when it comes to technology predictions","text":"Not only that, but go back a decade ago and look at what the singularity people (which the AI alignment crowd comes out of) were saying that. When employment was struggling to recover after the great recession, the singularity folks said that it wasn't going to recovers, because tech was replacing humans, and that this was just the beginning of mass unemployment (one of the reasons why UBI got so popular). CGP Grey's popular \"Human's Need Not Apply\" video is a good example of this kind of thought: [VIDEO] Claims that self-driving cars are already here and already better than human drivers, and that the only question is how quickly they replace humans. He argued that Baxter, the general purpose robot, could already copy the tasks of a human worker and do the work for much cheaper. Baxter was discontinued in 2018 because of low interest. These people have a horrible track record when it comes to technology predictions, and it's unnerving that, instead of reflecting on how wrong they've been, they're doubling down and trying to slow technological advancement."}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["FjA0EQPeUGM",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["G0Cp7DrvNLQ",1],["7vsCAM17O-M",1],["2Vrhk5OjBP8",1]]},"-J_xL4IGhJA":{"wy":"HN people recommend the SICP lectures as a deeper, livelier route into the book and a formative course even for seasoned programmers.","q":{"x":"I watched and cannot recommend it enough, even for seasoned programmers","i":26298412,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":34592325,"author":"lab11","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: What's the best lecture series you've ever seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best lecture series, especially the streams section","quote":"It changed my outook on programming by pi degrees","text":"Not even a second of thought: Sussman's & Abelson's SICP [VIDEO] especially the section on streams ( [LINK] ) It changed my outook on programming by pi degrees and I feel it is more needed then ever."},{"id":34070116,"author":"wiseleo","month":"2022-12","story":"24/7 SICP 1986 lectures stream","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent, still relevant course taught by the original SICP authors","quote":"an excellent course that is still very much relevant with very high production quality circa 1986.","text":"It's on-demand on YouTube[1] and an excellent course that is still very much relevant with very high production quality circa 1986. What makes it special is who is teaching it, which is the original authors of SICP. The course thus completely complements their book and is in the same style. The courses from later years are also on YouTube, but they don't feel the same. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26298412,"author":"augusto-moura","month":"2021-03","story":"Not Lisp again (2009)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full MIT course, valuable even for seasoned programmers","quote":"I watched and cannot recommend it enough, even for seasoned programmers","text":"The class mentioned in the article was actually made available under Creative Commons by the MIT OpenCourseWare[1], you can see the entirely of the course in Youtube. I watched and cannot recommend it enough, even for seasoned programmers [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25355278,"author":"k00b","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What new programming languages did you learn in 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows expressiveness from very few operators","quote":"Highly recommend the SICP lectures","text":"The real beauty is in how few operators you need to achieve maximal expressiveness in a programming language. It makes other programming languages seem extra. Highly recommend the SICP lectures [VIDEO]"},{"id":22861327,"author":"floathub","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: How to rediscover the joy of programming?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"First step to learning Lisp and fundamental concepts deeply","quote":"First watch the SICP series","text":"Learn LISP/Scheme and lots of fundamental concepts you probably have a feel for, but in a far more profound way: 1. First watch the SICP series ( [VIDEO] ) 2. Then work through the book ( [LINK] ) If you really do the exercises (an hour here and an hour there), you can feel you brain getting wrapped around many core notions in very illuminating ways."},{"id":48329630,"author":"bmitc","month":"2026-05","story":"Ask HN: Any advice on how to learn good software architecture practices?","stance":"rec","why":"Online SICP lectures as a learning resource","quote":"the latter has online lectures","text":"There are a lot of good books here: [LINK] I second the comment ( [LINK] ) recommending How to Design Programs (HTDP) and Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs (SICP). The former has an edX course ( [LINK] and [LINK] ) and the latter has online lectures ( [VIDEO] ) Head First Architecture ( [LINK] ) is good. Reading about systems thinking would be useful. Learning programming languages that have been well designed is helpful. I suggest Programming Erlang: Software for a Concurrent World and Elixir in Action ."},{"id":27331671,"author":"laen","month":"2021-05","story":"“Computer science is not about computers”","stance":"rec","why":"Abelson's introduction to computer science from 1986","quote":"Interesting to see the lineage of the statement","text":"Here is Harold Abelson discussing the topic in ~1986. His introduction to “Computer Science” has stuck with me for years. Interesting to see the lineage of the statement, it must have stuck with him as it has for me, when he first heard it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22679855,"author":"lymitshn","month":"2020-03","story":"How I became a better programmer (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Gives a feel for the magic of Lisp","quote":"I would suggest either watching SICP video lecs[0] or reading the book.","text":"Maybe more of the hype comes from the fact that its a Lisp. To get a feeling for the \"magic\" of lisp, I would suggest either watching SICP video lecs[0] or reading the book. I started learning lisp, almost the same time I've started learning programming with Java (college). Honestly I was thinking why they don't just teach us that, instead of Java at that time. Also Clojure is created by a fairly respected dude (afaik) Rich Hickey. His talks and Clojure design decision articles on the site are really insightful. Also this talk[1] also pretty good overview of different paradigms and `Clojure way`, imo. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":21783844,"author":"abiro","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Which of our ideas about computation could survive a thousand years?","stance":"rec","why":"Lively presentation of the idea in the first ten minutes","quote":"the first 10 minutes of the introductory SICP lecture contains a livelier presentation of the idea in the quote","text":"I’ve included the halting problem, but omitted the Turing machine, because while ingenious, the latter is simply a hack to make proofs work and not a very good way to think about computation. Similarly, I’ve included distributed representations, but omitted backpropagation since while backprop works very well for a number of problems that are otherwise unsolvable today, it is not a particularly enlightening way to write programs. Also, the first 10 minutes of the introductory SICP lecture contains a livelier presentation of the idea in the quote: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21300514,"author":"chrisa","month":"2019-10","story":"Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs (1996) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Original course videos, easier than reading the book","quote":"I found them a bit easier to watch than reading the book","text":"The original course videos are on youtube too! I found them a bit easier to watch than reading the book: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35099847,"author":"adamddev1","month":"2023-03","story":"Carp lang: statically typed Lisp, no GC","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson on parentheses as a linear form of a syntax tree","quote":"parentheses are just a way to write this two dimensional structure as a linear character string.","text":"As Hal Abelson said back in 1986 in the original SICP lectures [1] while drawing `(+ 3 (* 5 6) 8 2)` and the corresponding syntax tree on the board: >\"We're writing these trees ... and parentheses are just a way to write this two dimensional structure as a linear character string. Of course, when LISP first started and people had teletypes or punchcards or whatever and (the parentheses character structure) was more convinient. But maybe if LISP started today the syntax would look more like (a 2D syntax tree).\" [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33430636,"author":"thrown_22","month":"2022-11","story":"Racketscript","stance":"reference","why":"Live demo of structured Lisp editing from 1986","quote":"Here's a live demo hot from 1986","text":">Maintaining tree on text editors requires careful editing and strict practices, which human brains aren't good at. The \"tree\" will get broken at some point, but, in this school of thought, the recovery strategy is unclear and inconsistent at best. We've had editors that automatically do that since the 70s. Here's a live demo hot from 1986: [VIDEO] >The real tree, at the end of the day, is formed by line breaks and indents, not parenthesis. It isn't. You're not the type of person who lisp is for, which is fine. There are lots of languages suited for people like you. But I'd be curious to know what language you think uses nothing but line breaks and indents to describe its AST."},{"id":33432055,"author":"esjeon","month":"2022-11","story":"Racketscript","stance":"reference","why":"Used as evidence that Lisp writers rely on indentation","quote":"The process in the video still perfectly aligns with my description.","text":"> Here's a live demo hot from 1986: [VIDEO] The process in the video still perfectly aligns with my description. Actually, I reached that conclusion by observing people writing Lisp code. That is, when you're writing Lisp code, that's exactly what people next you would see, no matter what you think. Also, trailing \")\"s are clearly a burden, as people do spend their brain cycles to properly match them. > It isn't. You're not the type of person who lisp is for You sound like you're right in the middle of Lisp fever, which is fine. Everyone goes through that. > But I'd be curious to know what language you think uses nothing but line breaks and indents to describe its AST. You simply didn't get what I was saying. Even when all the parens were stripped off from a Lisp code, you can easily reinsert parens based on line breaks and indents. So people rely more on indentations for correctly putting parens, rather than being cautions w/ every single editing operation."},{"id":32659742,"author":"implements","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Where to find HTML version of SICP?","stance":"reference","why":"1986 SICP lectures on MIT OpenCourseWare","quote":"Actual PDF from MIT","text":"Actual PDF from MIT: (I assume they’re happy to let anyone use it) [LINK] There’s the set of the 1986 lectures on MIT’s Open CourseWare: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30157299,"author":"908B64B197","month":"2022-02","story":"How to recommend books, or, stop recommending SICP","stance":"reference","why":"Companion lectures to SICP, defending its audience","quote":"SICP has been used for decades and is extremely well regarded as an introductory book.","text":"> Written for freshman at MIT, it is ostensibly an entry-level text. But it requires such a level of competence in math and physics, and the prose itself is so dense and archaic, that I couldn't imagine suggesting it to anyone. It’s also a complement to a course, the implications here being that chapters and examples are to be discussed in class and that a certain amount of lab-work has to be performed as well for the concepts to sink-in. But It’s not for everyone. I couldn’t see a bootcamp based on this book for instance. It does assume a fair bit of math and physics knowledge (or a curiosity for such) because it was written with engineering students in mind who, for some of them, were encountering a computer for the first time of their lives. [0] > One guess is intelligence signalling. That it is human nature for a person to suggest a book in an attempt make herself look smart rather than to best assist the person asking for a suggestion. This is clickbait. SICP has been used for decades and is extremely well regarded as an introductory book. Maybe the OP lacked the fundamentals required for approaching it. If the authos has a CS degree, that reflects poorly on the program he completed. > If you love challenging books, you are neither the norm nor the audience of this post. But the MIT freshman this book was written for definitely are. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28540630,"author":"SquishyPanda23","month":"2021-09","story":"Turing Oversold?","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson's point that computer science is not about computers","quote":"computer science isn't about computers any more than biology is about microscopes.","text":"> Turing Machines, on the other hand, were instantly obviously mechanizable. It was clear that one could build a physical machine to run any Turing program without human input. Harold Abelson points out in one of his lectures [0] that computer science isn't about computers any more than biology is about microscopes. From that perspective, it is clear that Turing found an existing discipline of computer science and made major contributions to it. But it doesn't really seem right to say that he invented or founded computer science. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27331073,"author":"soegaard","month":"2021-05","story":"“Computer science is not about computers”","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson on why computer science is a poor name","quote":"explains why Computer Science is a terrible name","text":"The beginning of this lecture (30 seconds in) by Harold Abelson (author of SICP - Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs) explains why Computer Science is a terrible name here. [VIDEO] Not sure about the year."},{"id":25564329,"author":"arexxbifs","month":"2020-12","story":"Tribes of Programming (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson on computer science and computers","quote":"It's not about computers in the same sense that physics is not really about particle accelerators.","text":"Don't know if the \"joke\" originated with Abelson and Sussman but here's Hal Abelson talking about it: [VIDEO] \"It's not about computers in the same sense that physics is not really about particle accelerators.\" I sort of agree, but I guess it depends on what we mean about computer science being about computers. We can do things now that were downright impossible when A&S recorded their Lisp lectures simply because we can think differently about what's reasonable with regards to time and space complexity. And, of course, Abelson himself jokes about everyone wanting a faster computer in a later lecture. Perhaps we're simply confusing CS with software development, which I suppose could be likened to confusing mathematics with accounting."},{"id":21922157,"author":"bluedays","month":"2019-12","story":"A curated list of Computer Engineering resources","stance":"reference","why":"Abelson on computer science not being about computers","quote":"computer science is not really about computers.","text":"It’s funny you should ask that as I was just watching a lecture by Harold Abelson in which he addresses this exact question. [VIDEO] In case you don’t have the time or inclination to watch the video it can basically be summarized by saying the following. When the Egyptians first discovered geometry it was considered crop science as it’s primary application was for determining crop yields and when to plant and harvest. In the same way now that computers science is applied to computers today. But just like Geometry is really about farming, computer science is not really about computers. Professor Abelson goes on to explain that computer science provides a means to determine proofs for abstract mathematics a reasoning, so that we can understand with definitive proof things that until now were only theoretical."},{"id":47572937,"author":"AJRF","month":"2026-03","story":"Stripe is down","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Harold Ableson GIF of him scratching out the word Science, then the word Computer comes to mind.","text":"That's true. The Harold Ableson GIF of him scratching out the word Science, then the word Computer comes to mind. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CXv1j3GbgLk",1],["QNznD9hMEh0",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["wJyUtbn0O5Y",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["2KSahAoOLdU",1],["pOmXiapfCs8",1],["oP3c1h8v2ZQ",1],["vK1DazRK_a0",1],["ii1jcLg-eIQ",1],["NQFqDKRAROI",1]]},"rHIkrotSwcc":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it whenever zero-cost abstraction claims come up, especially for why unique_ptr is not free on some ABIs.","q":{"x":"Chandler presents his point clearly and goes deep enough into the subject.","i":30644518,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30644518,"author":"metalrain","month":"2022-03","story":"Tech talks don’t have to be boring","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear presentation that goes deep into the subject.","quote":"Chandler presents his point clearly and goes deep enough into the subject.","text":"Yes, tech talks don't need to be boring and I agree on many points on the post. But also not just 12 slides, more like hundreds of slides. One example I have is Chandler Carruths talk in CppCon 2019 \"There Are No Zero-cost Abstrations\" [VIDEO] Chandler presents his point clearly and goes deep enough into the subject."},{"id":36570687,"author":"TwentyPosts","month":"2023-07","story":"Scope-based resource management for the Linux kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Explains unique_ptr overhead and ABI constraints.","quote":"everything he says seems entirely solid","text":"When it comes to the overhead issues of unique_ptr, I always think of this video here: [VIDEO] At 26:50 he gets into how this relates to ABI, and how 'fixing' some of this would require a pretty severe ABI change, as well as destructive move semantics, which would require a severe change to how C++ works. I frankly don't understand C++ well enough to fully judge about all of this, but everything he says seems entirely solid, and showcases how unique_ptr is in fact not zero-cost, and can in fact be a significant performance trade-off when you really need that extra bit of performance. > Rust occasionally forces my hand considerably harder (e.g. RC<RefCell<..>> vs std::shared_ptr<>)) in the direction of performance compromise. How so? Is RC<RefCell<T>> more expensive than std::shared_ptr<T>?"},{"id":32978509,"author":"kccqzy","month":"2022-09","story":"C++20, How Hard Could It Be","stance":"rec","why":"Compares assembly showing unique_ptr overhead from ABI.","quote":"You should watch Titus Winters' presentation on this topic","text":"> std::unique_ptr<Foo> doesn't have any overhead over Foo* in code where semantics is the same Not true. And you just refuted yourself by being up the ABI question. You should watch Titus Winters' presentation on this topic where he compiles code using unique_ptr and raw pointers, compares the assembler produced, and explains why unique_ptr has non-zero overhead. The overhead is indeed coming from the ABI. Google has been wanting the next C++ to break ABI but they didn't get enough votes in the standard committee. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28124040,"author":"CountHackulus","month":"2021-08","story":"When zero cost abstractions aren't zero cost","stance":"rec","why":"Makes a convincing point that no abstraction is zero cost.","quote":"he makes a convincing point","text":"There are no zero cost abstractions. (According to Chandler Carruth, and he makes a convincing point). [VIDEO]"},{"id":24749000,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2020-10","story":"The AMD Radeon Graphics Driver Makes Up Roughly 10.5% of the Linux Kernel","stance":"rec","why":"Explains quadratic compile times from generated code at Google.","quote":"It's from this talk, which is about how a large amount of generated protocol buffer code at Google led to a quadratic increase in compile times","text":"The other time that you saw it was also probably me. It's from this talk, which is about how a large amount of generated protocol buffer code at Google led to a quadratic increase in compile times: [VIDEO] . TL;DW: The reasoning is because if you use a distributed build system, then your compile time is gated by the file with the longest compile time (tail latency). The more files you have, the greater chance that one of them takes a long time. When you generate source files, you tend to produce more files than if you didn't. Most users don't use a distributed build system to compile the kernel, so on further thought, in that case compile times probably scale closer to linear with the number of translation units. But wasted cycles are still wasted cycles, and regardless of how exactly compile times scale, you should still consider the cost of longer compile times when you duplicate code. With regards to link time optimization: sophisticated analyses take superlinear complexity: [LINK] . Disclaimer: I work at Google."},{"id":24362266,"author":"mehrdadn","month":"2020-09","story":"The problem with C","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on zero-cost abstraction as idealism, not reality.","quote":"I just wanted to mention this talk about how \"zero-cost abstraction\" is an idealism, not necessarily a reality","text":"With the caveat that I am NOT suggesting this justifies choosing C over C++, I just wanted to mention this talk about how \"zero-cost abstraction\" is an idealism, not necessarily a reality: [VIDEO] That said, I tried to reproduce something similar, and it seems the issue only occurs in my example due to external linkage (adding 'static' fixes it)... but I can't claim this will always resolve the issue: [LINK]"},{"id":22351492,"author":"blux","month":"2020-02","story":"Move, simply","stance":"rec","why":"Expose on why unique_ptr is not zero-cost.","quote":"See for a nice expose on this.","text":"The non-destructiveness of move semantics in C++ is very annoying. It causes for example `std::unique_ptr` to not be a zero-cost abstraction in all cases. See [VIDEO] for a nice expose on this."},{"id":49069863,"author":"RossBencina","month":"2026-07","story":"Building a Fast Lock-Free Queue in Modern C++ from Scratch","stance":"reference","why":"Compiler can't always eliminate unique_ptr overhead.","quote":"Just remember that it is not a zero-cost abstraction","text":"\"For 95% of all code that is correct. std::make_* is easy to use and prevents a lot of mistakes, while having no loss of performance.\" For 95% of code std::unique_ptr has no loss of performance. Perhaps. Just remember that it is not a zero-cost abstraction, the compiler won't always be able to entirely eliminate the overhead: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46448271,"author":"einpoklum","month":"2025-12","story":"The Compiler Is Your Best Friend, Stop Lying to It","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for ABI limits on unique_ptr optimization.","quote":"see Chandler Carrouth's CppCon 2019 talk","text":"> Rust makes it possible to safely manage memory without using a garbage collector, probably one of the biggest pain points of using low-level languages like C and C++. In C++, memory management has not been a pain point for many years, and you basically don't need to do it at all if you don't want to. The standard library takes care of it well enough - with owning containers and smart pointers. > And Rust is famous for its optimizations in the style of \"zero cost abstractions\". No, it isn't that famous for those. The safety and no-UB constraints prevent a lot of that. By the way, C++, which is more famous for them, still struggles in some cases. For example, ABI restrictions prevent passing unique_ptr's via single registers, see: [LINK] (or see Chandler Carrouth's CppCon 2019 talk: \"There are no zero-cost abstractions\" [VIDEO] )"},{"id":39595988,"author":"_aavaa_","month":"2024-03","story":"The Fundamentals of C/C++ Game Programming (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Abstractions have compile-time and human-time costs.","quote":"See for example","text":"What is often meant by that is \"This is a zero run-time cost abstraction\". That's a very different thing from a zero cost abstraction. Even if the abstraction actually has a zero run-time cost, which is absolutely not a give, it still has a compile-time cost and a human-time cost. See for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36573986,"author":"jacoblambda","month":"2023-07","story":"Scope-based resource management for the Linux kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Example of unique_ptr issues cited by another commenter.","quote":"","text":"> So you're (primarily?) referring to \"cognitive overhead\" rather than more usual meaning of overhead as in \"runtime overhead\" I'm referring to both. The issue is that all that complexity makes it very difficult for programmers to write code that the compiler is consistently able to optimise to (or close to) zero overhead. It can be done with enough work and care but for your average C++ developer, they aren't going to be seeing zero overhead for a lot of the things that C++ generally leads us to assume are zero overhead. > Can you given an example? To copy the link that TwentyPosts provided (which is coincidentally also the first thing that came to my mind, albeit at a different timestamp) [VIDEO] . The issues with `unique_ptr` in particular are that: - They force the compiler to generate exception code where maybe there isn't any necessary if you pass them into a function. You can elide this by going through and annotating things that you know can't except with noexcept however most people don't even care about noexcept in the first place and consider it line-noise. - Using move references to unique_ptr instead of pointers adds overhead by forcing the compiler to generate extra memory reads. - Passing by value should be zero cost but it'd require breaking basically every aspect of the C++ ABI to do so. Because of this you still get a double indirection which adds overhead. So your average C++ programmer probably won't do #1 which adds the largest overhead and if you need to"},{"id":36227412,"author":"_aavaa_","month":"2023-06","story":"Swift Ownership Manifesto","stance":"reference","why":"Zero runtime cost can still mean high compile and human cost.","quote":"","text":"But the problem with zero-cost abstractions is that even if the runtime cost is 0, the compile time (and the human time) can become quiet large. E.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35295320,"author":"twic","month":"2023-03","story":"Kobold, a new web UI crate with zero-cost static DOM","stance":"reference","why":"Counterpoint on the zero-cost abstraction term.","quote":"","text":"The term comes from C++, where it does have a fairly rigorous definition: [LINK] Although: [VIDEO] I'm not sure whether Rust people consistently use it with precisely the same sense."},{"id":29757325,"author":"cjfd","month":"2022-01","story":"Writing New System Software","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on tiny overheads that matter to few programmers.","quote":"I have seen a cppcon talk about that.","text":"This appears to be incorrect. I have seen a cppcon talk about that. I think it may have been this one: [VIDEO] . On the other hand, it is a very, very tiny minority of programmers who have to care about an overhead as low as this. Most of us should not waste one microsecond of our thinking time on this."},{"id":28702745,"author":"kinjba11","month":"2021-09","story":"LLVM 13's new pass manager improves Rust's compile times by up to 20%","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on the meaning of zero-cost.","quote":"I'm reminded of this CppCon talk","text":"I wonder if it's time to move past that original meaning, or perhaps invent a new catchy term to describe the problem. I'm reminded of this CppCon talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":24736911,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2020-10","story":"What if you knew how computers work? (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for no cost-free abstractions.","quote":"There are no cost free abstractions","text":"Not sure if this is sarcastic, but... 1) In dynamic languages, it's not possible to detect whether a function is used or not in the general case. For example, consider string accesses on objects. If the compiler is not sophisticated enough to resolve the set of possible strings at compile time (or such analysis would unacceptably increase compile times), then you can't shake out unused methods on that object. [1] 2) For languages like C and C++, the compiler cannot tree shake because it only knows about a single file at a time (translation unit, to be precise). You would have to rely on link-time optimizations to effectively tree shake, but LTO is not well-supported by all toolchains. Tree shaking also has a cost that I mentioned earlier -- it increases compile times. Both LTO and tree shaking in dynamic languages increase compile times superlinearly [citation needed] wrt. the size of your application. As other commenters have mentioned, it's better to avoid including unnecessary libraries in the first place. There are no cost free abstractions, and that applies to tree shaking as well. [VIDEO] [1] For the pedants: yes, I know resolving the possible set of values (stricter than \"all possible values for that type\") for a variable is undecidable in the general case."},{"id":24391535,"author":"Rusky","month":"2020-09","story":"C++20 has been approved","stance":"reference","why":"Shows unique_ptr isn't zero-cost on some ABIs.","quote":"unique_ptr actually isn't zero-cost on some ABIs","text":"This is probably not what was meant, but interestingly unique_ptr actually isn't zero-cost on some ABIs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24316562,"author":"yesbutt11","month":"2020-08","story":"Lithium ranked the highest performance web application platform","stance":"reference","why":"Talk arguing C++ has no zero-cost abstractions.","quote":"I thought there were no zero cost abstractions in C++?","text":"Re: metamap being zero cost I thought there were no zero cost abstractions in C++? [VIDEO] Still, it isn’t RoR or anything JS so it has that going for it."},{"id":23000524,"author":"aw1621107","month":"2020-04","story":"Comparison of C++17, Go, and Java for a next-generation sequencing tool","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why unique_ptr isn't zero-cost: ABI and no destructive move.","quote":"Chandler Carruth talks about this at CppCon 2019","text":"> However, do you have any bench / source for std::unique_ptr ? You are the first one I hear giving critics on it. Chandler Carruth talks about this at CppCon 2019 [0]. From a quick review he says part of the reason std::unique_ptr isn't zero-cost right now is: - Objects passed by value (like std::unique_ptr) are passed on the stack instead of in registers, and changing that will be an ABI break - No destructive move means extra temporaries will be created/retained [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22228282,"author":"leni536","month":"2020-02","story":"ABI – Now or Never [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"The talk on ABI-related overhead.","quote":"","text":"The video: [VIDEO] I wrote up a reddit post for a possible workaround for removing the overhead. It's standard C++, no ABI break is required. It's not without caveats though: [LINK]"}],"r":[["0rCUF-V1esM",1],["97t7Xj_iBv0",1],["QyQvfaW54NU",1],["PpG0z-npFIY",1],["5eL_LcxwwHg",1],["v8IQ-X2HkGE",1]]},"3wfNl2L0Gf8":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a famous, revealing clash between two thinkers, though some note they largely talk past each other.","q":{"x":"For anyone who hasn't watched the actual, full video of the debate, you should do so.","i":9428221,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":49047322,"author":"bbor","month":"2026-07","story":"Claude Opus 5","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Infamous debate with chapter markers to skip to creativity.","quote":"I cannot reccomend the infamous debate b/w Chomsky & Foucault enough","text":"1. Languages are used for much, much more than communication, and there is good reason to think that capacity didn't evolve for communication, but rather for metacognitive problem solving. 2. The idea that Tolkein --one of the most reknowned writers in our entire canon-- did something isn't relevant to the philosophical discussion of creativity and its obvious bounds. Even Tolkein was just painting within the lines set by the linguists he had read and the obscure languages he adored, not to mention the fundamental limits set by our capacity for language in the first place. For anyone who's curious about this kind of thing, I cannot reccomend the infamous debate b/w Chomsky & Foucault enough; it ranges across topics a bit, but chapter markers should help you skip to the core of it (creativity) if you prefer. Video here: [VIDEO] , some old summaries on /r/AskPhilosophy here: [LINK]"},{"id":9428221,"author":"steveklabnik","month":"2015-04","story":"Foucault’s lecture notes from 1970–71 demolish caricatures of his thought (2013)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Full Chomsky-Foucault debate across two languages, fun and revealing.","quote":"For anyone who hasn't watched the actual, full video of the debate, you should do so.","text":"For anyone who hasn't watched the actual, full video of the debate, you should do so. Watching them argue in two languages at the same time is super fun. [VIDEO] Okay, here's what the video is saying, in slightly-longer form. The exchange in the video is basically Chomsky saying \"human nature\" and Foucault laughing. This gets at the heart of the hour-long debate: do humans have some sort of nature? Foucault, and philosophers like him, think that the idea of 'human nature' is complete and utter garbage. So when Chomsky admits that his position boils down to an appeal of human nature, Foucault just laughs at him. It's a great moment that really highlights the whole thing. I love it."},{"id":21152742,"author":"pmoriarty","month":"2019-10","story":"Michel Foucault: Power and Struggle","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting debate though speakers talk past each other.","quote":"Unfortunately, they're mostly speaking past each other, but it's still worth watching.","text":"For anyone who hasn't seen it yet, here's an interesting debate between Foucault and Chomsky: [1] Unfortunately, they're mostly speaking past each other, but it's still worth watching. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":16505847,"author":"marnett","month":"2018-03","story":"The Role of Luck in Life Success","stance":"rec","why":"Enjoyable discussion on acquisition of knowledge and human nature.","quote":"there is a very enjoyable debate/discussion between Foucault and Chomsky on the acquisitions of human knowledge and human nature","text":"I think he is not interested in epistemology. But I always find discussions on the origins or human knowledge just as fascinating as you. Thanks for sharing your thoughts. It sounds like you may have already been exposed to it given the content of your arguments, but there is a very enjoyable debate/discussion between Foucault and Chomsky on the acquisitions of human knowledge and human nature [1] and your position sounds quite similar to the one Chomsky elaborates on. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15272151,"author":"f00_","month":"2017-09","story":"Michel Foucault in Death Valley: A Boom Interview with Simeon Wade","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting debate on human nature.","quote":"this debate with chomsky on human nature is interesting","text":"really wierd, most of my exposure to foucault is indirect. this debate with chomsky on human nature is interesting [VIDEO]"},{"id":13524724,"author":"enqk","month":"2017-01","story":"How Camus and Sartre split up over the question of how to be free","stance":"rec","why":"Shows tonal differences between European and American intellectuals.","quote":"It is quite illuminating to see the exchange for instance between Michel Foucault and Noam Chomsky","text":"It is quite illuminating to see the exchange for instance between Michel Foucault and Noam Chomsky to get a sense of the difference of tone and focus between european and american intellectuals. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43578098,"author":"bbor","month":"2025-04","story":"An image of an archeologist adventurer who wears a hat and uses a bullwhip","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Chomsky's view of creativity.","quote":"Covered in the famous Chomsky v. Foucault debate, for the curious","text":"Disregarding the (common!) assumption that AGI will consist of one monolithic LLM instead of dozens of specialized ones, I think your comment fails to invoke an accurate, consistent picture of creativity/\"truly new\" cognition. To borrow Chomsky's framework: what makes humans unique and special is our ability to produce an infinite range of outputs that nonetheless conform to a set of linguistic rules. When viewed in this light, human creativity necessarily depends on the \"linguistic rules\" part of that; without a framework of meaning to work within, we would just be generating entropy, not meaningful expressions. Obviously this applies most directly to external language, but I hope it's clear how it indirectly applies to internal cognition and--as we're discussing here--visual art. TL;DR: LLMs are definitely creative, otherwise they wouldn't be able to produce semantically-meaningful, context-appropriate language in the first place. For a more empirical argument, just ask yourself how a machine that can generate a poem or illustration depicting [CHARACTER_X] in [PLACE_Y] doing [ACTIVITY_Z] in [STYLE_S] without being creative! [1] Covered in the famous Chomsky v. Foucault debate, for the curious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41549699,"author":"bbor","month":"2024-09","story":"Terence Tao on O1","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You’re asking the right questions, IMO! Chomsky has spent his life trying to answer this question in different forms, and has ultimately arrived to the conclusion that we live in a “Pre-Gallilean” era of cognitive science, where the only answers available to us are developed through the use of intuitive interpretation (like they used to do with ‘the heavens’/space) instead of empirical contradiction (aka science). He does have some answers, such as “human creativity is the ability to create an infinite range of outputs from a finite range of inputs that nonetheless pertain to our motivations/context in some useful way”, but that’s obviously not a very satisfying answer. It tells us a little — I think Tao is gesturing to exactly this when he complains that GPTo1 can only apply and combine mathematical approaches within a sort of closed domain rather than propose radically new ones - but it’s not helpful for an everyday understanding of creativity. IMO :) In his words, from Language and Mind : \"Roughly, where we deal with cognitive structures, either in a mature state of knowledge and belief or in the initial state, we face problems, but not mysteries. When we ask how humans make use of these cognitive structures, how and why they make choices and behave as they do, although there is much that we can say as human beings with intuition and insight, there is little, I believe, that we can say as scientists… What I have called elsewhere 'the creative aspect of language use' remain"},{"id":40833158,"author":"nchmy","month":"2024-06","story":"The Bleak Genius of Michel Foucault","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Foucault came off as amoral late in the debate.","quote":"They had a perfectly pleasant and interesting conversation for the first 75% of the talk","text":"Cooked him? They had a perfectly pleasant and interesting conversation for the first 75% of the talk - both regularly shooshing the clueless moderator. Then at the end it turned more towards morality and Foucault showed himself to be quite clearly completely, illogically, disturbingly amoral - as Chomsky himself later more or less described him (a video which has been linked in other comments) No person with a shred of moral character or intellectual honesty could ever conclude anything remotely close to Foucault winning, let alone \"cooking\" him. edit: It starts building up to the real conflict/debate around maybe 45min, but it really becomes concrete at 54:13. Foucault just finished rambling about \"destroy the bourgeoisie \" and Chomsky says \"surely you believe that your role in the war is a just role\" and then further lays down some very sensible, insightful ideas about justice. Foucault then says something to the effect of \"the proles dont fight for justice, they fight to have power for the first time in history. One wages war to win, not because it is just\". Chomsky disagrees, but remains patient and extends an olive branch to him to say something less insane - suggesting that if such an overthrow would lead to a terroristic police state (e.g. the USSR, north korea etc...) , then I wouldn't want the proles to win. The only justification for such action is if one thinks - rightly or wrongly - that some fundamental human values will be achieved\". Foucault just doubles and tr"},{"id":24793829,"author":"stephc_int13","month":"2020-10","story":"Is Stupidity Expanding? Some Hypotheses","stance":"reference","why":"Used as example of higher-caliber thinkers of the past.","quote":"I tend to think that current (public) thinkers are not as good as they were.","text":"I am not sure. But when I look at something like this: [VIDEO] (Chomsky and Foucault debating at human nature) I tend to think that current (public) thinkers are not as good as they were."},{"id":21197766,"author":"undershirt","month":"2019-10","story":"Collapse OS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Definitely, the Green Revolution et al is a solid basis for optimism, especially with Ehrlich losing his wager on resource scarcity. And I do like the malthusian lineage you described. This empirical optimism is also paradoxically irreverent toward the immutable attrition of complexity. Our creativity has limits, whatever they are just pick something. At the risk of sounding flippant, 200 years of “creative patching” is historically too small a window to say we can continue subverting this “law” with eternal vigilance (I’ve heard this described as “we are running out of tricks”). Maybe I’m oversimplifying when I say we would have to approach the limit of absolute foresight to achieve this, but I think there’s some truth to it. For example, I like these explanations of our rational limits, with regard to managing a complex society: - CHOMSKY[1]: We have in our heads a certain set of possible intellectual structures. In the lucky event that some aspect of reality happens to have the character of one of these structures in our mind, then we have a science. And that doesn’t mean everything is ultimately going to fall within the domain of science. Quite the contrary… personally I believe that the nature of a decent society might fall outside scope of possible human science. - ZIZEK[2]: Hegel says, the owl of Minerva only flies out in the dusk. [owl being the icon of wisdom] So philosophy can only grasp a social order when it’s already in its decay. Particularly unsettling is our r"},{"id":15555293,"author":"mccoyspace","month":"2017-10","story":"The Unfortunate Fallout of Campus Postmodernism","stance":"reference","why":"Example of postmodernism versus rationalism in dialog.","quote":"The Foucault/Chompsky debate","text":"agreed. There are many examples of attempts to put into dialog these different traditions of postmodernism vs humanism/analytic rationality: The Foucault/Chompsky debate: [VIDEO] The Heidegger/Cassirer debate: [LINK]"},{"id":14184707,"author":"openfuture","month":"2017-04","story":"Dreaming Outside Our Heads","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Tim Parks book 'teach us to sit still' is what got me to start a regular meditation practise but only because I enjoyed how hard headed he was and unwilling to accept the obvious positive effect it had on him. This post feels a little bit similar where the other guy is just schooling him and his objections are feeble. Which got me to think, is this the modern equivalence of this [1]? [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9428341,"author":"dethstar","month":"2015-04","story":"Foucault’s lecture notes from 1970–71 demolish caricatures of his thought (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">How are they defining human nature in the context of their debate? That's what Foucault wants to know. [VIDEO] And from my little understanding, because they don't believe there's such thing as a human nature, since society, and other things from our surrounding affect us, and the \"nature\" could change in a different context, which would imply it isn't some kind of force behind us."},{"id":9429803,"author":"thwest","month":"2015-04","story":"Foucault’s lecture notes from 1970–71 demolish caricatures of his thought (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as a rebuttal showing substance in continental philosophy.","quote":"A good rebuttal to this Chomsky quote are the lovely debates he had with Foucault himself.","text":"No one will deny that there are charlatans and poets posing as philosophers in the continental tradition. Tatterdemalion's post even prefaces with that. There are also straight up falsifiers and a endemic of p-value smudgers in the hard sciences. A good rebuttal to this Chomsky quote are the lovely debates he had with Foucault himself. [VIDEO] There is a plethora of writings of substance in the continental tradition. Writings that we should not ignore. 'Discipline and Punish' is one of them. I recommend 'The Dialectic of Enlightenment' by Adorno as your complement to Popper's writings. On another note, I don't generally see the value in antagonisms between paradigms or presentation forms. I find value from the analytical perspective and from critical theory, and being able to dance both dances is illuminating. I don't think the empirical process can reveal the entirety of the human experience. Steadfastly stumping for one paradigm over the other is no more useful than being a vim/emacs zealot. If Deleuze or Foucault don't grok for you, it doesn't mean the authors are _wrong_ or lying to readers to obtain mystic status. Just like someone not caring to study Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory shouldn't write off the entirety of mathematics."}],"r":[["SKtQgFBRUvQ",1],["vvZA9n3e5pc",1],["UGttmR2DTY8",1],["BKP1I7IocYU",1],["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["GoW8Tf7hTGA",1],["S89BHnaxULo",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1],["uRGljemfwUE",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["a1zDuOPkMSw",1]]},"aB8zcAttP1E":{"wy":"HN commenters send people here for a detailed first-hand account from the pilot, and some say it changed their view on the sightings.","q":{"x":"this is a great interview interesting in its own right, even without the UFO part","i":27570598,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28306710,"author":"bostonsre","month":"2021-08","story":"Joe Rogan, confined to Spotify, is losing influence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fravor interview shifted commenter from skeptic to finding UFOs plausible","quote":"I went from \"no friggin way do they exist\" to \"shit... it actually sounds plausible\".","text":"Lex did one [1] with the same pilot that saw the ufo (commander fravor). I went from \"no friggin way do they exist\" to \"shit... it actually sounds plausible\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27570598,"author":"RobertoG","month":"2021-06","story":"There Are Aliens, but Probably Not Here","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great interview with pilot Fravor, interesting even apart from UFO content","quote":"this is a great interview interesting in its own right, even without the UFO part","text":"You should check the interviews (there are several, including a 60 minutes) with the pilot David Fravor (1). There are two pilots and two wizards that saw the same with their own eyes (even I have only see the other pilot confirming it). That's an UFO. That doesn't mean that it's extraterrestrial. To say that it's extraterrestrial at this point, is, in my opinion, so ridiculous like to say that there is nothing to explain (like the author of the article seems to believe). (1) - [VIDEO] (this is a great interview interesting in its own right, even without the UFO part)"},{"id":30703907,"author":"subsubzero","month":"2022-03","story":"Famous Navy UFO video was camera glare, evidence suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed first-hand account of the incident by Commander Fravor","quote":"here is a detailed video of him talking about the incident with Lex Fridman","text":"So actually Commander Fravor saw the object with his own eyes, he is one of the pilots who was sent in to engage the craft after it appeared on radar from the carrier group, here is a detailed video of him talking about the incident with Lex Fridman(AI reasearcher - MIT) on his podcast - [VIDEO]"},{"id":30704426,"author":"cronix","month":"2022-03","story":"Famous Navy UFO video was camera glare, evidence suggests","stance":"rec","why":"Four-hour interview with far more detail than other sources","quote":"Here's a 4 hour interview with the pilot, David Fravor, on Lex Fridman's show where he goes into much more detail","text":"Here's a 60 Minutes piece discussing it: [LINK] Here's a 4 hour interview with the pilot, David Fravor, on Lex Fridman's show where he goes into much more detail than you'll probably read elsewhere: [VIDEO] This wasn't just a single radar. It was an AEGIS system compromised of multiple ships with state of the art radars, multiple plane radars, and visual confirmation by several pilots. They were tracking it for multiple days."},{"id":27530669,"author":"grimgrin","month":"2021-06","story":"Experts Weigh in on Pentagon UFO Report","stance":"rec","why":"Long conversation with eyewitness pilot David Fravor","quote":"Here's a _long_ conversation with one of the pilots with eye visibility that day, David Fravor","text":"Here's a _long_ conversation with one of the pilots with eye visibility that day, David Fravor, though I'm linking you to the start of that specific topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27315835,"author":"tristanj","month":"2021-05","story":"Radar data confirms: USS Omaha was surrounded by swarm of UFOs","stance":"rec","why":"Pilot David Fravor explains the Tic Tac encounter in a long interview","quote":"David Fravor, one of the pilots, explains this in an interview here","text":"That article completely ignores the eyewitness testimony from the navy pilots of the Tic Tac incident where they visually identify that the Tic Tac: 1) can hover a few meters above the ocean without generating rotor downwash 2) can accelerate away at 100+g Both of those rule out any known drone or helicopter. David Fravor, one of the pilots, explains this in an interview here [VIDEO]"},{"id":25808002,"author":"pp19dd","month":"2021-01","story":"US Navy – Tic Tac UFO – High frequency gravitational wave generator","stance":"rec","why":"Pilot describes the event and record keeping.","quote":"Might be worth something to watch part of the Lex Fridman interview with one of the pilots","text":"Might be worth something to watch part of the Lex Fridman interview with one of the pilots, David Fravor. The entire podcast is four hours long and goes over his career and experiences, I'd argue establishes him as a competent naval officer and pilot. The timecode is when he describes the actual event- [VIDEO] In the interview he also describes the record keeping process and basically some of the head scratchers you're bringing about. One other thing the pilot confirms in that interview is the validity (authenticity) of the leaked \"Executive Summary\" report, which, was leaked to media and (as far as I know) not really pursued for authenticity verification by others (PDF): [LINK]"},{"id":24475007,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2020-09","story":"Fighter pilot says UFO he chased in 2004 committed 'act of war'","stance":"rec","why":"Credible first-hand testimony from a fighter pilot.","quote":"I'd recommend this podcast episode","text":"I guess somebody had to cherry pick that line from the 3+ hour long interview, because it makes a good click-bait headline. But there's a lot more to the interview with Fravor than that. I still have over an hour of it left to watch, but I'd already say it's well worth watching. And don't get me wrong: I'm not some \"UFO believer\" or member of the \"UFO community\" or anything. I'm a skeptic by nature and I'm definitely a skeptic when it comes to some of the more radical claims made by ufologist types: the \"extraterrestrial hypothesis\" in particular. But Fravor does come off a very reasonable, intelligent, level-headed, sincere witness to something highly unusual. And he makes a point of distancing himself from claims of, as he puts it, \"little green men\". That said, he also allows that what he saw is not, as far as he knows, possible with any human technology. Net-net, if you enjoy this kind of thing, regardless of whether or not you have any pre-existing biases regarding what UFO's \"really are\", I'd recommend this podcast episode. At the very least, it's a chance to hear first-hand testimony by a very credible witness to something extraordinary. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30146485,"author":"StanislavPetrov","month":"2022-01","story":"Why is Harvard astrophysicist Avi Loeb working with ardent UFO believers?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"We have video from the government that is matched with eye-witness testimony from David Fravor, a pilot who taught at the Navy's Top Gun school. I'm not suggesting this is iron-clad evidence of anything, but nothing about this incident (and others) has been \"debunked\". The first-hand account of one of the Navy's most accredited naval aviators along with matching video and sensor data more credible than some random person on Youtube who \"debunks\" it by saying it was the moon. If the evidence points to a phenomenon that doesn't operate inside the laws of physics as we understand them, then we have to adjust our understanding. Perhaps there are very good, mundane explanations for this and another incidents, but its foolish to simply ignore or downplay the significance of things we can't understand. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27349789,"author":"throwaway29303","month":"2021-06","story":"The UFO Sightings Don't Impress This Physicist","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"For one thing, first-person accounts, which are notoriously inaccurate to begin with, don’t provide enough information for an empirical investigation. Scientists can’t accurately gauge distances or velocity from a pilot’s testimony: “It looked close” or “It was moving really fast” is too vague. What a scientist needs are precise measurements from multiple viewpoints provided by devices that register various wavelengths (visual, infrared, radar). That kind of data might tell us if an object’s motion required engines or materials that we Earthlings don’t possess. It's true that first-person accounts are sometimes inaccurate, but in the Nimitz case, we have, I believe, eight pilots in total who actually saw the object. The first incursion by David Fravor and his co-pilot Alex Dietrich and a second plane; and the second incursion - by other pilots which I don't know the names of - on which there's brief footage of the object[0]. Not only there's first-person accounts there's also devices which registered these objects. Nimitz caught these objects, in fact, they not only caught several of them, the incursions were made in order to check out what was going on. I wish these \"devices can be faulty,\" (knowing that people actually looked at the object in loco ) \"people can be bad first-person accounts\" memes actually died once and for all in this particular case. It feels like people don't actually go out and do their due diligence. I feel people either aren't listening or paying atten"},{"id":27087651,"author":"stevenhuang","month":"2021-05","story":"UFOs shows government competence as either surprisingly high or low","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot's commentary on the incident.","quote":"See commentary by David Fravor, one of the jet pilots in the incident","text":"Those explanations are misguided and thunderfoot is just rehashing (badly) the points raised by Mike West. See commentary by David Fravor, one of the jet pilots in the incident [VIDEO]"},{"id":27087750,"author":"stevenhuang","month":"2021-05","story":"UFOs shows government competence as either surprisingly high or low","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot's commentary on the videos.","quote":"See David Fravor's commentary on the videos.","text":"See David Fravor's commentary on the videos. He's one of the jet pilots from the Nimitz incident. [VIDEO] Specifically in the Go fast video: [VIDEO] The object is shown colder than the ocean; it can't be a bird."},{"id":25808849,"author":"user3939382","month":"2021-01","story":"US Navy – Tic Tac UFO – High frequency gravitational wave generator","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot rebuts skeptic's ideas with equipment knowledge.","quote":"The original observer of the tic tac defeats Mick West's ideas","text":"The original observer of the tic tac defeats Mick West's ideas based on his expert knowledge of the equipment [VIDEO]"},{"id":24768802,"author":"maxharris","month":"2020-10","story":"Navy UFO Videos – FLIR/Gimbal/Gofast Analyses","stance":"reference","why":"Pilot who was there gives his account.","quote":"Here's what one of our Navy pilots that was actually there has to say","text":"Here's what one of our Navy pilots that was actually there has to say, in an interview with Lex Fridman at MIT: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24454964,"author":"pp19dd","month":"2020-09","story":"How UFO culture took over America","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Form whatever idea you will of all this chaff, one thing is indisputable. You yourself can file a FOIA request at the Pentagon requesting \"cockpit videos cleared for release to Louis Elizondo in the Fall (September-October) of 2017\" and you'll get a copy of those videos directly from the horse's mouth. As an ultra-skeptic who is drawn toward primary sources, I've been following the cluster of these stories closely for years with an eye to their provenance. More I thought about all this, I think the whole thing collapses to two life-changing possibilities, rationalizing my way down directly from the videos: 1) No new physics. Cockpit videos - faked by military for a psyop campaign, coordinated with patriot testimonies by credible individuals. Navy patents filed by the US Navy on behalf of Dr. Salvatore Cezar Pais and document leaks added to effect more confusion. There's a precedent for this type and scale of operation going back to the Star Wars program days, where Reagan himself participated in attempting to drain the Soviet Union's R&D budget. If the videos were planted for personal gain by a soon-to-be-laid-off employee, Elizondo, and then engineered into credibility through the FOIA chain of custody - it explains why and how, but doesn't explain everyone else's participation, unless the military chose to exploit the incident and launch a psyop campaign in its wake. If the Navy patents were entirely unrelated to all this, perhaps an innocent case of fraud, incompetent boss"},{"id":25812173,"author":"mr_woozy","month":"2021-01","story":"US Navy – Tic Tac UFO – High frequency gravitational wave generator","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] How the fuck do you honestly utter a sentence like this and expect not to appear like a 15 year old stoner still in High School? I don't even dislike him as a person, I'm just concerned for his mental health at this point. He seems to be really struggling to out sentences together in a coherent fashion."}],"r":[["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["LNjJTgXujno",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["rJjKP8vYjpQ",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["ZTidn2dBYbY",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1],["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["Myq_Hc_39aI",1],["NiRrvxjrJ1U",1],["2wq9x2QcZN0",1],["-q7ZVXOU3kM",1],["eJ3RzGoQC4s",1],["plcc6E-E1uU",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1]]},"JWD1Fpdd4Pc":{"wy":"HN recommends it to Vim users considering Emacs, and several say it persuaded them or their coworkers to switch.","q":{"x":"Aaron gave a great talk about his switch here","i":12104353,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":9939131,"author":"blaenk","month":"2015-07","story":"What's new in Magit 2.x","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reasons for switching from Vim to Emacs match commenter's own.","quote":"TL;DR: Watch this video","text":"TL;DR: Watch this video: the stated reasons for switching are very similar to my own. It wasn't until I watched this video and read the book that I took the plunge to switch to emacs. It was the fact that with emacs I could have vim _plus_ much more and better functionality. [VIDEO] I'll have to write a blog post about it sometime, but basically while I absolutely _love_ the vim 'editing interface', that is, not the GUI, but the modal editing, the bindings, motions, objects, relative line numbers, and so on, everything else aside from that, primarily plugins, always felt like ad-hoc hacks to me. I've contributed to a couple of vim plugins so I've seen it first hand, to the point where plugin authors I've worked with have expressed feelings of wanting to switch to emacs to just make things easier. Indeed, some have, such as the vim-airline author [ [LINK] ]. Whereas plugins in vim oftentimes gave me the impression that they were stopgaps and hacky workarounds to get vim to do something it wasn't meant to do, emacs packages feel very natural, comprehensive, and well designed. Just take this article for example. As awesome as vim-fugitive is for vim, I feel that it's good _for being a vim plugin_, but it has _nothing_ on the equivalent on emacs: magit. Take a look at it's comprehensive documentation: [LINK] Or flycheck's [ [LINK] ] documentation here [ [LINK] ] It seems like everyone puts up with everything else about vim just to have the good parts of vim that are generally ver"},{"id":23070429,"author":"swalladge","month":"2020-05","story":"Emacs' Org-Mode and Syncthing = Perfect (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on switching from vim to emacs with org-mode.","quote":"is an interesting talk on switching from vim to emacs + org-mode.","text":"\"Evil Mode: Or, How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love Emacs\" by Aaron Bieber is an interesting talk on switching from vim to emacs + org-mode. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21642687,"author":"spudlyo","month":"2019-11","story":"Emacs: The Editor for the Next Forty Years [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Perspective from a die-hard Vim user who switched to Emacs.","quote":"I found this video[0] a pretty interesting perspective from a die-hard vim user who switched.","text":"Emacs has recently been seeing a lot of growth from vim users who are migrating to emacs \"distros\" (like spacemacs and doom) that are pre-configured with emacs' vim-emulation. I found this video[0] a pretty interesting perspective from a die-hard vim user who switched. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17685903,"author":"chungy","month":"2018-08","story":"Can I use a non-monospaced font in either Vim or gVim?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why Emacs can behave like Vim with proportional text editing.","quote":"recommend watching this talk for why","text":"It sounds counter-intuitive to use another editor, especially Emacs, but this might actually be the best advice. Emacs is on top of the game for proportional text editing, as well as the ability to make it behave very much (even exactly so) like Vim. I'd recommend watching this talk for why: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12265255,"author":"endgame","month":"2016-08","story":"Intero for Emacs: complete interactive development program for Haskell","stance":"rec","why":"Helps give Emacs a fair trial; converted vim-using coworkers.","quote":"this blog post and lecture might help you give emacs a fair trial","text":"I don't know NixOS, but this blog post and lecture might help you give emacs a fair trial: [LINK] [VIDEO] One of my vim-using coworkers found it. He, along with another colleague, have since found emacs compelling enough to use as their main text editor."},{"id":12104353,"author":"endgame","month":"2016-07","story":"“Give me 15 minutes and I'll change your view of GDB” [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk about switching from Vim; moved several coworkers to Emacs.","quote":"Aaron gave a great talk about his switch here","text":"You might find this article useful: [LINK] Aaron gave a great talk about his switch here: [VIDEO] , which caused several people at work to move off NeoVim and give emacs a go."},{"id":9829505,"author":"kristiandupont","month":"2015-07","story":"Firemacs: Emacs keybindings for FireFox","stance":"rec","why":"Convinced a Vim user to try Emacs with evil mode.","quote":"it really does look like it might be the best of both worlds.","text":"I, as a Vim user myself, recently saw this and decided to give emacs a try. I still have a long way to go but I have to say that it really does look like it might be the best of both worlds. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17289007,"author":"moistoreos","month":"2018-06","story":"Neovim 0.3 released","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I watched this earlier today so it seemed relevant","text":"I have a serious question: If Emacs has a lot of the features people seek in Neovim, why not just use Emacs in evil mode? I watched this earlier today so it seemed relevant: [VIDEO] Side note: I've been using Neovim for months now and I've not tried Emacs. Not because I don't want to but because Vim with the plugins i have works really really well."}],"r":[["gd5uJ7Nlvvo",1],["0g9BcZvQbXU",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["-gPuH1yeZ08",1],["prXuyMCgbTc",1],["W3aieHjyNvw",1]]},"agOdP2Bmieg":{"wy":"HN treats it as a foundational Bret Victor talk that reshaped how people think about software and interfaces, often cited alongside Dynamicland.","q":{"x":"have had a huge impact on the way I think about software","i":16839792,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16839792,"author":"Stwerner","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that shaped the commenter's thinking about software","quote":"have had a huge impact on the way I think about software","text":"Really excited to go through all the other talks listed here I'm not familiar with! Three talks that I tend to reference a lot and have had a huge impact on the way I think about software: The Soul of Software: Avdi Grimm - [LINK] The Humane Representation of Thought: Bret Victor - [VIDEO] How to Program Independent Games: Jonathan Blow - [LINK]"},{"id":34487952,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Has anyone fully attempted Bret Victor's vision?","stance":"rec","why":"One of Victor's most-cited talks","quote":"he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually.","text":"For the people asking who is Bret Victor: When \"What are your favourite tech talks of all time?\" is asked on HN, he gets more mentions than anyone else, usually. Some of his talks: Inventing on Principle [LINK] The Future of Programming [LINK] The Humane Representation of Thought [VIDEO] Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [LINK] Seeing Spaces [LINK] Drawing Dynamic Visualizations [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":28452882,"author":"badassiel","month":"2021-09","story":"Show HN: Web browser to help programmers think clearly","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how tools fail to use our evolved superpowers","quote":"You might find The Humane Representation of Thought[1] by Bret Victor[2] useful","text":"It just so happens I was thinking about spatial org just yesterday, that it would be so nice if I can have 'villages' of tabs on a (geographical) Map. Amazing co-incidence that I woke up to bonsai browser notification! You might find The Humane Representation of Thought[1] by Bret Victor[2] useful, where he demonstrates by example, how existing tools/software (generally speaking) doesn't utilize all the 'superpowers' that we acquired via evolution. --- [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] Edit - grammatical fixes"},{"id":18317856,"author":"skadamat","month":"2018-10","story":"Dynamicland: The Next Big Thing Is a Room","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible overview of humane representations across sensory modalities","quote":"Bret's talk is a great, accessible overview of these ideas from his context","text":"One of the goals of Dynamicland is to foster the creation of new representations that take advantage of + live across more sensory modalities. Bret calls them modes of representation, but there are similar phrases in learning & psychology literature. Most representations are haphazardly created (math notation, periodic table of elements, etc) by (often) singular experimentation. Representations are poorly researched and understood (there's no branch of science dedicated to them). If representations were studied more closely, we could get much better at deliberately creating representations that are more humane. When the scientific method is applied, we can then apply the engineering method to create new things from those principles. Bret's talk is a great, accessible overview of these ideas from his context ( [VIDEO] ). To vastly oversimplify things, one way to improve accessibility is to parallelize representations that live in just 1 or 2 sensory modalities to all of our sensory modalities. This way, math notation could be made accessible to & equally powerful to the blind (as it is to those who can see). I empathize (a little, it would be naive for me to assume I can 100% empathize) with your reaction along the axis of accessibility. What Dynamicland looks like right now isn't what it's going to look like later. Right now, you may see Dynamicland as text boxes but now distributed in a room. In 50 or 100 years, there may be no text AT ALL and you can use the senses availabl"},{"id":15565912,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-10","story":"The HARC First Year Report","stance":"rec","why":"Context for Victor's research from two talks","quote":"Highly recommend Bret's 2 talks for context","text":"Highly recommend Bret's 2 talks for context: [1] Seeing Spaces: [LINK] [2] Humane Representation of Thought: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14509064,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-06","story":"Ask HN: Why does visual programming suck?","stance":"rec","why":"Bret Victor's thinking behind physical computing in the RealTalk project","quote":"Watch his 2 talks that highlight the thinking and inspiration behind the RealTalk project","text":"It's going to be entirely different I feel. Instead of manipulating graphics on a flat screen, focus will probably be on manipulating physical objects on a table / in a room. Watch his 2 talks that highlight the thinking and inspiration behind the RealTalk project: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43382860,"author":"kaycebasques","month":"2025-03","story":"Our interfaces have lost their senses","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks Inventing On Principle: [LINK] A couple other Bret Victor resources I gleaned from other comments: Humane Representation Of Thought: [VIDEO] Dynamicland: [LINK]"},{"id":27684262,"author":"subjectsigma","month":"2021-06","story":"I still use plain text for everything. (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Victor's rant on the inhumanity of keyboards and screens","quote":"Bret Victor's rant about the inhumanity of keyboards and screens","text":"I'll be contrarian and throw my opinion on top of the pile, why not. I've never found most of the arguments about the UNIX philosophy or plaintext super compelling. Some people love to wax poetic about \"composing together small programs\" and \"being able to use one toolset to accomplish many things.\" In my experience this works really great for systems administration (the activity the paradigm was designed around - big surprise) but not very well for other things. Sometimes I want to take a picture. Sometimes I want to query a sqlite database. Sometimes I want Powershell objects. Life is not systems administration, and neither is computing. Don't get me wrong, I have emacs open all day every day at work. But when I hear people talk about using plaintext for everything, I think about Bret Victor's rant about the inhumanity of keyboards and screens: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18555620,"author":"skadamat","month":"2018-11","story":"Math and Analogies","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses representations in the first twenty minutes","quote":"discusses representations quite well in a very similar context / framing.","text":"The first 20 mins of Bret's talk ( [VIDEO] ) discusses representations quite well in a very similar context / framing. The challenge with roman numerals was definitely computing with them and really \"thinking with them\". Abacuses helped, but it was still a poor UX in many ways and it took lots of training & practice to get familiar. Algebraic notation also requires training & practice but has a much quicker learning curve. Almost every literate adult uses equations in some form or another on a weekly basis. Both the medium (pencil & paper) as well as the representation (algebraic notation) were critical to this (as well as other things like education system / literacy, etc). The benefits & disadvantages of different representations is massively understudied and misunderstood. Data visualization, semiotics, and some other fields focus a bit on this but still fall short in many ways."},{"id":18223107,"author":"mitchtbaum","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"reference","why":"Overwhelming talk the commenter had to pause halfway","quote":"they're down right scary and overwhelming, or at least dumbfounding and perplexing","text":"on one level, yes on another, they're down right scary and overwhelming, or at least dumbfounding and perplexing ..thinking specifically about The Humane Representation of Thought [VIDEO] I had to stop it half way through.. :-/ see also Seeing Spaces [LINK] and Media for Thinking the Unthinkable [LINK]"},{"id":18316831,"author":"skadamat","month":"2018-10","story":"Dynamicland: The Next Big Thing Is a Room","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I had the pleasure of visiting Dynamicland and hanging out with Peter Norvig, Nicky Case and many others. There's a few things that stuck out for me: - The community! Most people I interacted with were pretty inter-disciplinary. Some came from an education heavy background, others from a more programming language background. Some from interface design, others from physical installation or video game background (and everything in between). Everyone had their own degree of skepticism but were excited by the discussions that were happening (a sign of a good research culture!). - The representations : What excites me the most about Dynamicland is that it's an environment for fostering unique representations of ideas. The cultural forces & ideas (social programming, remixing, visible state / code at all times, 3d environment, etc) baked into the space encourage the experimentation & creation of new ways of representing complex ideas. Some scattered examples here - [LINK] - Removal of artificial barriers : When programming on a computer these days, there's so many barriers to doing simple things. The amount of code that exists to do virtual actions in a virtual world is MASSIVE, and acts as a huge barrier. This is something Bret talks about at the end of his interview on Postlight ( [LINK] ). Because code / programs in Dynamicland are embodied in the room, you don't need code to move a dialog box or a slider around. You just moved it yourself. This is super powerful and it means yo"}],"r":[["klTjiXjqHrQ",2],["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["PFc379hu--8",1]]},"huEtJw7pfLk":{"wy":"Commenters repeatedly call it a favorite and a mind-blowing introduction to jazz.","q":{"x":"which served as one of my most memorable early introductions to jazz","i":27541446,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39600319,"author":"LanternLight83","month":"2024-03","story":"How I listen to music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Miles Davis and LCD Soundsystem duet with an affectionate mouse cursor.","quote":"one my favorite videos","text":"This reminds me, somewhat tangentially, of one my favorite videos, the Miles Davis / LCDS YT Duet w/ the oddly affectionate mouse cursor [VIDEO]"},{"id":32919804,"author":"spartanatreyu","month":"2022-09","story":"React I love you, but you're bringing me down","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mashup that improves on the original song.","quote":"One of those rare exceptions, where overlaying another song improves the original","text":"How did you manage to share the song without also sharing that amazing mashup? One of those rare exceptions, where overlaying another song improves the original: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28517435,"author":"gtpedrosa","month":"2021-09","story":"RaveDJ – AI Music Mashup Maker","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Miles Davis and LCD Soundsystem mashup duet.","quote":"Blew my mind back than as it still does today","text":"Can't help but remember LCD Soundsystem + Miles Davis duet from 2011: [VIDEO] Blew my mind back than as it still does today"},{"id":27541446,"author":"mustacheemperor","month":"2021-06","story":"A Beginner’s Guide to Miles Davis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Miles Davis improvising over LCD Soundsystem as a jazz introduction.","quote":"which served as one of my most memorable early introductions to jazz","text":"This reminded me of this 10 year old YouTube video that creates a \"duet\" between that soundtrack and LCD Soundsystem's New York I Love You But You're Bringing Me Down, which served as one of my most memorable early introductions to jazz: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qd_hEja6bzE",1],["rCMh7srOqvw",1],["nfDoml-Db64",1],["OyfBQmvr2Hc",1],["jgOTZe07eHA",1]]},"0yKf8TrLUOw":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it whenever Excel, spreadsheets or functional programming come up, as a fun and serious take on spreadsheets as real programming.","q":{"x":"Felienne has a great talk on functional programming in Excel","i":23054638,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23054638,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2020-05","story":"History of Logo","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great talk on functional programming in Excel","quote":"Felienne has a great talk on functional programming in Excel","text":"OT, but Felienne has a great talk on functional programming in Excel (thanks for reminding me of her name): [VIDEO]"},{"id":38877743,"author":"robertknight","month":"2024-01","story":"CellLVM: A proof-of-concept LLVM to Excel spreadsheet compiler","stance":"rec","why":"Talks on Excel as a functional programming language","quote":"I recommend Felienne Hermans' talks on Excel as a functional programming language","text":"Related to this, I recommend Felienne Hermans' talks on Excel as a functional programming language - [VIDEO] ."},{"id":26127788,"author":"ggrrhh_ta","month":"2021-02","story":"The worst of the two worlds: Excel meets Outlook","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting, fun talk on Excel as a programming language","quote":"I found it interesting, fun and great.","text":"This is the talk that I first saw that builds on that: [VIDEO] I found it interesting, fun and great. Still, I would stick to using Excel for the personal analysis of data and not as the language/tool on which software is developed: data & programming logic intermixed, the default way to index cells, the difference between normal cells and array cells, and tables, etc., the fact that some things are just not possible to do fully programmatically without creating your own VB functions and therefore asking recipients of what looks like data to accept running arbitrary code in their machines, etc."},{"id":24076251,"author":"brohee","month":"2020-08","story":"Scientists rename human genes to stop MS Excel from misreading them as dates","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on pure functional programming in Excel","quote":"You may enjoy","text":"You may enjoy \"Pure Functional Programming in Excel\" by Felienne Hermans presented at GOTO 2016 [VIDEO]"},{"id":15928317,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2017-12","story":"Excel team considering Python as scripting language: asking for feedback","stance":"rec","why":"Hermans' work on Excel as a functional language","quote":"Check out Felienne Hermans' work if you haven't already","text":"Excel itself is functional and reactive already, wouldn't adding an FRP language on top of that be a bit superfluous? Check out Felienne Hermans' work if you haven't already: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":43333227,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2025-03","story":"New Zealand's $16B health dept managed finances with single Excel spreadsheet","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing the world runs on spreadsheets","quote":"Felienne Hermans is proven right about the world running on spreadsheets","text":"And once again Felienne Hermans is proven right about the world running on spreadsheets and that we should take them a lot more seriously for that reason alone. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26669646,"author":"molsongolden","month":"2021-04","story":"Spreadsheet is a software development paradigm","stance":"reference","why":"Cited alongside Hermans' spreadsheet research","quote":"Also see the research of Felienne Hermans and her talk","text":"Also see the research of Felienne Hermans and her talk “Pure Functional Programming in Excel.”[1] [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["GtQdIYUtAHg",1],["ip641WmY4pA",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["ouvi-fwrfIY",1]]},"6h3RJhoqgK8":{"wy":"Recommended to freelancers and consultants as practical advice on contracts and getting paid.","q":{"x":"1 of my favorite videos on the internet about this is [1].","i":13424551,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13424551,"author":"agibsonccc","month":"2017-01","story":"RethinkDB Postmortem","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk about business models and getting paid","quote":"1 of my favorite videos on the internet about this is [1].","text":"I'll attempt to add to this. I might be stating things that are a bit controversial here. 1 thing we are always transparent with users about is how our business model works. 1 of my favorite videos on the internet about this is [1]. We are very much based on closed source for making revenue. This has actually helped with our community a lot. We are basically focused on closed source solutions for specific verticals. The only way to get clients to pay is to make something more efficient or don't open source/core it. For open source companies there is always a trade off of community vs customers. You have to find a hard line and stick to it. Community is great, but most of those people won't want to pay you. You can try to convert them, but that takes a lot of time and isn't the best path to market. We strive to provide a great base line experience to our users, but a few of the things we have found in practice that helps: 1. Features have customer names on them 2. There's a mass of community interest in a specific feature You have to be careful about where time is spent. It's very easy to mistake \"users\" for \"customers\". I wish slava and co the best of luck and really appreciate their post mortem. 1 thing we've learned as an infra company is \"be as close to the app as possible\". In our case the bulk of what we focus on is banking/telco anomaly detection. It's a straight forward revenue making product that can benefit from deep learning. The great thing is we can grow in to oth"},{"id":13846491,"author":"redbeard0x0a","month":"2017-03","story":"Ask HN: How do I start my own consulting firm?","stance":"rec","why":"Essential viewing before starting a consulting firm","quote":"Watch this video","text":"There are a lot of things that you need to do to start your own consulting firm. One of those things to do needs to be: * Watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13066178,"author":"kefka","month":"2016-11","story":"Don’t Sign That Non-Disclosure Agreement Yet","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant talk on contracts and payment","quote":"Very relevant video","text":"There's your first problem. You had a shitty contract that isn't paying you for billable hours. If you quit paying me, or on net 30, you will pay for the work I've done. And if you don't, I'll take your ass to court. \"Fuck you, pay me.\" Very relevant video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13066998,"author":"vlod","month":"2016-11","story":"Don’t Sign That Non-Disclosure Agreement Yet","stance":"rec","why":"Source talk for the quoted line","quote":"I would however, recommend the whole vid.","text":"It's from: Mike Monteiro - \"Fuck you pay me\" talk. Here's a link to the relevant bit in the video: [VIDEO] I would however, recommend the whole vid."},{"id":9616017,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2015-05","story":"Ask HN: Getting stressed out moonlighting","stance":"rec","why":"Advice on contracts and scope creep","quote":"See his comments on contracts, starting generally at 7m 20s","text":"Mike Monteiro - \"Fuck you pay me\" [VIDEO] See his comments on contracts, starting generally at 7m 20s Scope creep is included. Renegotiate. It's a new project."},{"id":6802849,"author":"CodeMage","month":"2013-11","story":"An understated job advertisement","stance":"rec","why":"Mike Monteiro talk on getting paid","quote":"I would like to suggest watching the following talk by Mike Monteiro","text":"Oh, okay. I guess that makes it all right, then. I would like to suggest watching the following talk by Mike Monteiro: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13846407,"author":"finkin1","month":"2017-03","story":"Ask HN: How do I start my own consulting firm?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in advice about starting a consulting firm","quote":"","text":"I manage a 2-person remote design and development team that seems similar to yours. We all got started doing individual freelance work, and then we raised some money as a team to pursue building our own software product. When we ran out of money, we went back to individual freelancing. We decided to actually start a consulting firm when we landed a big client that required combining all of our skills. We still actually operate with individual S-Corps which contract with our consulting firm LLC, which we own equally. Sometimes we still do individual freelance work outside of our firm. You may want to talk to a CPA about the best way to set things up for your particular goals. - Look for projects everywhere. Even if someone is looking for an individual freelancer, there's no harm in presenting yourself as a team and trying to get the gig anyway. - Local, remote, doesn't matter. The quality of the project is what matters most in my experience. Burnout matters even more when your team is small and everyone is relying on getting income from a project. - Definitely reach out to other agencies, particularly local ones. Tell them you can help with overload and custom coding problems they might not have the in-house talent to tackle. - I've never had a mentor/coach, so I can't really say if it's worth it. - You should probably start by doing the outreach yourselves. Try subscribing to some paid curated lead generation services for freelancers/agencies. For a couple hundred dollars a m"}],"r":[["RrHnLXMTOWM",1],["fXW-QjBsruE",1],["HXAW2GseICs",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["AItTqnTsVjA",1],["wlR5gYd6um0",1],["hlrv7DIHllE",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["VnvGwFegbC8",1],["jh8supIUj6c",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["eG0KrT6pBPk",1],["DoeNbZlxfUM",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1]]},"NRoUC9P1PmA":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the definitive explanation of ZFS design straight from its inventors, with war stories about data integrity.","q":{"x":"I would recommend anybody interested in filesystems to watch Jeff Bonwick (ZFS inventor) explain the design of ZFS","i":11982302,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":38362637,"author":"jorangreef","month":"2023-11","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk by ZFS creators that inspired TigerBeetle","quote":"I love this talk by Jeff Bonwick and Bill Moore","text":"ZFS was an inspiration for TigerBeetle. I love this talk by Jeff Bonwick and Bill Moore: “ZFS: The Last Word in File Systems” [VIDEO]"},{"id":21180141,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2019-10","story":"Ubuntu installer now supports ZFS on the boot partition","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how different ZFS is from other filesystems","quote":"Watching Part 1 at least is worth it for anyone curious","text":"> Is ZFS any good? The presentation \"ZFS: The Last Word in File Systems\" from its debut in 2008 is a good introduction. Part 1 (of 3) probably best explains how different it is from just about anything else out there (even now, in 2019): * [VIDEO] Part 2 show some extra niceties / features, and Part 3 is a demo and Q&A. Watching Part 1 at least is worth it for anyone curious; 2 and 3 are optional."},{"id":11982302,"author":"tnorgaard","month":"2016-06","story":"A ZFS developer’s analysis of Apple’s new APFS file system","stance":"strong_rec","why":"ZFS inventor explains design, with war stories on checksumming","quote":"I would recommend anybody interested in filesystems to watch Jeff Bonwick (ZFS inventor) explain the design of ZFS","text":"I would recommend anybody interested in filesystems to watch Jeff Bonwick (ZFS inventor) explain the design of ZFS: [VIDEO] . They have a few very nice war stories explaining why they found it useful to have the user data checksummed as well."},{"id":46470313,"author":"solarengineer","month":"2026-01","story":"FreeBSD: Home NAS, part 1 – configuring ZFS mirror (RAID1)","stance":"rec","why":"ZFS talk series, listed among good resources","quote":"Some good stuff here","text":"ZFS: The last word in File Systems: Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] ZFS In the trenches: [LINK] Some good stuff here: [LINK]"},{"id":26901345,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-04","story":"Btrfs on Zoned Block Devices","stance":"reference","why":"ZFS creators on avoiding the write hole via COW","quote":"See this 2008 SNIA presentation from Bonwick and Moore, the creators of ZFS talking about not having a write hole","text":"> initial issues You mean like the current advice not to use anything except mirroring and striping (RAID-0/1/10)? > Parity may be inconsistent after a crash (the \"write hole\"). The problem born when after \"an unclean shutdown\" a disk failure happens. But these are two distinct failures. These together break the BTRFS raid5 redundancy. If you run a scrub process after \"an unclean shutdown\" (with no disk failure in between) those data which match their checksum can still be read out while the mismatched data are lost forever. * [LINK] I've been using ZFS since it came out on Solaris 10 over a decade ago and it was specifically designed not to have a write hole due to its COW/ACID nature. See this 2008 SNIA presentation from Bonwick and Moore, the creators of ZFS talking about not having a write hole: * ACID/COW: [VIDEO] * Integrity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24375225,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2020-09","story":"Preserving data integrity: A ZFS-inspired storage system","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by ZFS creators, timestamped to architecture layers","quote":"A potato-quality video from 2008 with Moore and Bonwick, the creators","text":"Well, ZFS isn't exactly monolithic if you look under the hood: it has the ZPL (files, directories), DMU (objects, transactions on those objects), SPA (actual disk I/O). A potato-quality video from 2008 with Moore and Bonwick, the creators (timestamped to relevant section): * [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"gEKSpZPByD0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the clearest long-form explanation of the everything-is-fields view for non-physicists.","q":{"x":"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists.","i":31289871,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":12676428,"author":"Noseshine","month":"2016-10","story":"A Natural Law for Rotating Galaxies: What Does This Mean for Dark Matter?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what particles are; essential background","quote":"(really worth it! Watch at 1.5 times speed)","text":"> Every point of Universe contains infinite number of small, even infinite small, particles. What \"particles\"? [VIDEO] (really worth it! Watch at 1.5 times speed) and [LINK] (reading the discussion is useless though without understanding at least what is presented in the above lecture)"},{"id":31289871,"author":"joncp","month":"2022-05","story":"Where do space, time and gravity come from?","stance":"rec","why":"Carroll makes quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists","quote":"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists.","text":"Sean Carroll is a master of making quantum mechanics approachable to non-physicists. Here are a few of his talks: - Fermilab 2013: [VIDEO] - The Royal Institution 2014: [LINK] - The Royal Institution 2020: [LINK]"},{"id":22459946,"author":"bvinc","month":"2020-03","story":"Quantum researchers able to split one photon into three","stance":"rec","why":"Carroll explains particles as vibrations in fields","quote":"Here's a video of Sean Carroll talking about it, roughly 28:00-33:00.","text":"There are many different ways of looking at quantum mechanics, so here is one way of answering your confusion. Probably the standard way of looking at quantum mechanics right now is quantum field theory. In quantum field theory, you give up the notion of \"particles\" and instead view them as vibrations in fields. Once you do that, it makes more sense that these \"particles\" can turn into other particles, be absorbed or emitted by other particles, or split. Here's a video of Sean Carroll talking about it, roughly 28:00-33:00. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22373206,"author":"brian-armstrong","month":"2020-02","story":"Double Slits with Single Atoms","stance":"rec","why":"Long lecture on the everything-is-fields idea","quote":"This lecture is long but goes into the everything is fields idea","text":"I believe \"everything is fields\" is indeed a common interpretation. Particles are an unnecessary component we invent because of our familiarity with \"stuff\" This lecture is long but goes into the everything is fields idea [VIDEO]"},{"id":12377595,"author":"okket","month":"2016-08","story":"There are no particles, there are only fields (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining Sean Carroll lecture on particles and fields","quote":"which can be very entertaining like this one from 2013","text":"Sean M. Carrol always mentions this fact when he talks about QFT, which can be very entertaining like this one from 2013 \"Particles, Fields and The Future of Physics\" [VIDEO] (Audio starts at 19 sec, Lecture starts at 2:00)"}],"r":[["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1],["5hVmeOCJjOU",1],["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["lyqt6u5_sHA",1],["wtV5ev6813I",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1]]},"jAhiMCSvtCc":{"wy":"HN commenters hold it up as a top-tier long-form process video, recommended whenever the Great Wave or Hokusai comes up.","q":{"x":"The series is up there with Clickspring's skeleton clock in my pantheon of \"greatest long-form process videos","i":47093171,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47093171,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2026-02","story":"The Rediscovery of 103 Hokusai Lost Sketches (2021)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ranks among the greatest long-form process videos.","quote":"The series is up there with Clickspring's skeleton clock in my pantheon of \"greatest long-form process videos","text":"I bought one of his prints of The Great Wave off Kanagawa. It's stunning. The series is up there with Clickspring's skeleton clock in my pantheon of \"greatest long-form process videos\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22735957,"author":"rwmj","month":"2020-03","story":"Dutch museum says van Gogh painting stolen in overnight raid","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long series on reproducing the Great Wave as a print.","quote":"There's a fantastic (and long) series on Youtube about this","text":"I think there's a genuine reason for them to be worth more since they are closer to the original painting. There's a fantastic (and long) series on Youtube about this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22033545,"author":"Obsnold","month":"2020-01","story":"The 'Great Wave' has mystified art lovers for generations (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"David Bull's series reproducing the Great Wave.","quote":"I really suggest checking out David Bulls channel on YouTube.","text":"If anyone is interested in Japanese wood block prints I really suggest checking out David Bulls channel on YouTube. He did a whole series on his own copy of the Great Wave. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26994978,"author":"Cerium","month":"2021-04","story":"The 'Great Wave' has mystified art lovers for generations (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Printer David Bull documents his 2015 reproduction of the Great Wave.","quote":"In 2015 he did a reproduction of the Great Wave and documented the progress online","text":"You may find David Bull's youtube content interesting. He is a Japanese woodblock printer living in Asakusa, Tokyo, where he runs a print shop. In 2015 he did a reproduction of the Great Wave and documented the progress online: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["7jPKEyM44GU",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["B8Y146v8HxE",1],["F04dGK1_wYA",1]]},"y71lli8MS8s":{"wy":"HN programmers admire it as a clean, clever demonstration of emulator design and as the standout of a favorite programming YouTuber.","q":{"x":"Here's a very clean and clever NES emulator in C++ by Bisqwit of TAS fame","i":9249745,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":19026585,"author":"petercooper","month":"2019-01","story":"Let’s Create a Speech Synthesizer in C++17 [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic video among favorite programming YouTuber's work.","quote":"My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch.","text":"Agreed. My favourite programming YouTuber by a long stretch. If anyone is unfamiliar with him and wants to watch some of the \"classics\" I'd recommend these: His \"cracking videogame passwords\" series: [LINK] Creating a Doom-style 3D engine in C: [LINK] Creating a Rogue-like game in C++11: [LINK] Creating a PIC16 emulator and NES music player: [LINK] Creating a NES emulator: [VIDEO] Creating a DOS VGA raytracer: [LINK]"},{"id":33380058,"author":"molticrystal","month":"2022-10","story":"Smolnes: A NES emulator in less than 5000 significant bytes of C++","stance":"rec","why":"Tool-assisted coding journey of making an NES emulator.","quote":"there is a nice video series short in minutes(time)","text":"Those who enjoy this might also enjoy \"Creating a NES emulator in C++11\" [0] [1] [2] while not short in bytes of code(space) there is a nice video series short in minutes(time). Bisqwit takes you on a 30 minute tool assisted coding journey of making an NES emulator and also provides a 15 minute followup Q&A video explaining different aspects. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":12703000,"author":"petercooper","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite YouTube channel for developers?","stance":"rec","why":"Channel favorite that creates a NES emulator.","quote":"is by far my favorite and I've spent many hours watching him","text":"[LINK] is by far my favorite and I've spent many hours watching him. He does things like create a Doom-style engine from scratch: [LINK] .. create a NES emulator: [VIDEO] .. work back from a C++17 example to show why new C++ standards are needed: [LINK] .. and even building a Tetris clone in GW-BASIC: [LINK] .. Right now, he's doing a series on cracking 80s videogame passwords: [LINK] Sirajology - [LINK] - is another interesting one. He moves a bit too quickly for me, but does things like explain machine learning in 5 minutes or how to generate music with systems like Tensorflow."},{"id":9249745,"author":"atesti","month":"2015-03","story":"Nes emulator written in go","stance":"rec","why":"Clean NES emulator coded at tool-assisted speed.","quote":"Here's a very clean and clever NES emulator in C++ by Bisqwit of TAS fame","text":"Here's a very clean and clever NES emulator in C++ by Bisqwit of TAS fame: [VIDEO] He presents the code in the style of a tool assisted run, typing it in in high speed. I never saw templates used like that to create the unrolled CPU dispatcher"},{"id":7609730,"author":"petercooper","month":"2014-04","story":"Ask HN: Your favorite YouTube channels?","stance":"rec","why":"Coding videos like building a NES emulator.","quote":"always blows my mind when he does his coding videos","text":"[LINK] record high quality videos at many programming conferences each year and then share the recordings on YouTube. So much to enjoy here, especially if you're open source leaning, like most of the events they do. O'Reilly puts up lots of good stuff at [LINK] although the webinar recording quality leaves a lot to be desired. A real random set of tech topics though and often something worth watching. Entrepreneur - [LINK] - usually puts up lots of short videos with a business tip in or something. Sometimes longer interviews. Usually worthwhile if a little superficial at times. The guy who founded Something Awful has a ridiculously addictive channel - [LINK] - he generally plays horrifically poorly produced indie games with his sidekick Shmorky and I could listen to their absurdist banter all day. It's a bit of a mish mash but [LINK] always blows my mind when he does his coding videos such as coding a NES emulator in C++11: [VIDEO] [LINK] is awesome if you're into supercars. He's currently doing a tour of European supercar events and factories putting up a video each day. Far Lands or Bust - [LINK] - is a guy who started to walk towards the 'far lands' in a Minecraft maps years ago. He's something like 10% of the way but is still plodding along recording his progress. This is a real pilgrimage with all the highs and lows that entails. MrThaiBox123 - [LINK] - is a British IT expert who seems to have an endless supply of cash to buy gadgets, phones, and amazing computer setups."},{"id":18513984,"author":"0xcde4c3db","month":"2018-11","story":"Goboy: Multi-Platform Nintendo Game Boy Color Emulator Written in Go","stance":"reference","why":"Tool-assisted speedrun of writing an accurate NES emulator.","quote":"There's even a sort of tool-assisted speedrun of writing an accurate NES emulator","text":"The main advantage to NES emulation is that it's a very mature area of study. High-quality documentation is much more readily available than for other systems, and there's more public discussion of the specific problems that NES emulator authors encounter (both primarily on nesdev.com [1] [2]). There's even a sort of tool-assisted speedrun of writing an accurate NES emulator, if you're into that sort of thing [3] [4]. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":10089546,"author":"iofj","month":"2015-08","story":"The Strange Appeal of Watching Coders Code","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I love having streams of coding open while working.","text":"I love having streams of coding open while working. It makes me type faster (I think). Sound is off though. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6933003,"author":"grapeshot","month":"2013-12","story":"Show HN: I made an easy-to-understand 6502 emulator in pure C","stance":"reference","why":"Template-generated opcode dispatch for 6502.","quote":"Here's a very apropos example of this technique as applied to the 6502.","text":"It's possible to do something like that in C. In C++, you can even do some template magic to create all the appropriate functions for each opcode based on something like the original instruction decode ROM. Brilliant and short code, but very difficult to write in the first place. Here's a very apropos example of this technique as applied to the 6502. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6556702,"author":"GhotiFish","month":"2013-10","story":"A quick look at the Redis source code","stance":"reference","why":"Bisqwit's compile-time instruction table technique.","quote":"he brings in the instruction table at 1:30","text":"Yes. It is certainly more pithy. ACTUALLY! It reminds me of a technique Bisqwit used when he made his emulator. He used strings to define the behavior of certain instructions, the strings were actually interpreted at compile time. Though I think this is a C++ specific trick. [VIDEO] he brings in the instruction table at 1:30"}],"r":[["LRhbcDzbGSU",1],["N8elxpSu9pw",1],["63vqob-MljQ",1],["eRkgK4jfi6M",1],["HQYsFshbkYw",1],["gPlpphT7n9s",1]]},"JHGkaShoyNs":{"wy":"HN developers recommend it as the talk that changed how they model systems, and as a clear intro to event sourcing's motivations.","q":{"x":"forever changed how I think about modeling systems, preserving history","i":31783857,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31783857,"author":"ssalka","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best dev tool pitches of all time?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear walkthrough of problems event sourcing solves and new use cases.","quote":"forever changed how I think about modeling systems, preserving history","text":"Not about dev tools per se, but this talk by Greg Young on event sourcing & CQRS forever changed how I think about modeling systems, preserving history, and supporting multiple read models/versioning. Really clear walkthrough of the types of problems that benefit from an event sourced system, how event sourcing addresses them, and exploration of new use cases it enables. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13129378,"author":"stock_toaster","month":"2016-12","story":"Talks that changed the way I think about programming","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Greg Young's talk on event sourcing.","quote":"really made an impact on me","text":"The following talk about event sourcing (not listed in the article)•, really made an impact on me: Event Sourcing - Greg Young [VIDEO] •: Not that I really expected it to be I guess."},{"id":19074689,"author":"rhizome31","month":"2019-02","story":"Event Sourcing is Hard","stance":"rec","why":"Fun introduction to event sourcing and its motivations.","quote":"For a fun introduction to this concept and the motivations behind it","text":"For a fun introduction to this concept and the motivations behind it, you may watch Greg Young's talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":18706704,"author":"matthewaveryusa","month":"2018-12","story":"Wolkenkit 3.0 – a CQRS and event-sourcing framework for JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Greg Young explains event sourcing, useful for pitching it.","quote":"Greg Young's presentation on event-sourcing is pretty great","text":"I agree with you it's tough to sell CQRS, it's like trying to explain to someone that their business should use a database in one line. For me event-sourcing is a historical layer below your existing database that allows you to replay the sequence of events that got your database to its current state. If I had to businessify it, maybe it would be 'Wolkenkit is a framework that allows you to save and replay your entire business's history so you never miss a key insight again.' From a technical perspective, engineers can focus on the product and organically build and destroy the data structures around a changing business instead of trying to find the perfect data-structures that will inevitably be incorrect as the business grows and evolves. You get regression tests for free, and you get backups for free which may be nice-to-haves when the business is small, but absolute requirements once you have real customers paying real money. Not sure how I would squeeze that in an elevator pitch though. Greg Young's presentation on event-sourcing is pretty great, you may find some inspiration there: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15186603,"author":"bottlenecked","month":"2017-09","story":"Show HN: Learn what DDD, CQRS, and event-sourcing are all about","stance":"reference","why":"Greg Young cautions about applying event sourcing only where it fits.","quote":"Do not tell me you've built an event sourced system","text":"Greg Young also cautions \"Do not tell me you've built an event sourced system\"* (or something along those lines) meaning that you should be careful to apply the pattern only to the part(s) of the system that makes sense (due to the increased complexity of ES) * I think it's in this talk [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XVbuk3jizGM",1],["qH6sSOr-yk8",1]]},"LbH1ZDImaI8":{"wy":"HN commenters send people here as the best accessible starting point for understanding why Raptor and fuel choice matter for Starship.","q":{"x":"A great place to start is this amazing video by The Everyday Astronaut","i":30347369,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30347369,"author":"DisjointedHunt","month":"2022-02","story":"Starship is threatening NASA’s moon contractors","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how fuel choice shapes rocket design, including Mars refueling","quote":"A great place to start is this amazing video by The Everyday Astronaut","text":"Because the assumptions made for one class of payload don't apply to another class of payload with vastly different mission specifications. A great place to start is this amazing video by The Everyday Astronaut: [VIDEO] Notice how the type of fuel used itself is a consideration for the type of rockets we may send to Mars if we're considering In-Situ refueling."},{"id":21628521,"author":"DennisP","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: How would you bootstrap a space company?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what makes the Raptor engine revolutionary","quote":"Here's a great video on what makes the new SpaceX engine revolutionary","text":"NASA's big project for launch is a disposable rocket using Space Shuttle technology. It will cost at least a billion dollars per launch. SpaceX's project is a fully reusable rocket, with rapid turnaround because it uses methane fuel in the world's first clean-burning full-flow engine. They expect to be able to launch three times per day. On top of that the rocket is built of cheap stainless steel instead of carbon fiber. With that amount of reuse, each launch basically pays for fuel and ground support, each of which costs about a million dollars per launch. Given their payload of at least 100 tonnes, that's $20/kg. They've already done a single-engine hop test, and hope to reach orbit within six months. Here's a great video on what makes the new SpaceX engine revolutionary: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33620423,"author":"kiratp","month":"2022-11","story":"NASA – Artemis I Liftoff","stance":"rec","why":"Further learning on Raptor's breakthroughs","quote":"If you want to learn more","text":"SpaceX is making significant breakthroughs that you can appreciate if you understand the tech. > As of 2019, only three full-flow staged combustion rocket engines had ever progressed sufficiently to be tested on test stands; the Soviet Energomash RD-270 project in the 1960s, the US government-funded Aerojet Rocketdyne Integrated powerhead demonstration project in the mid-2000s,[6] and SpaceX's flight capable Raptor engine first test-fired in February 2019. [LINK] If you want to learn more [VIDEO]"},{"id":33620460,"author":"kiratp","month":"2022-11","story":"NASA – Artemis I Liftoff","stance":"rec","why":"Further learning on Raptor's breakthroughs","quote":"If you want to learn more","text":"Raptor is a breakthrough engine. If NASA with nation state resources and 23B is 6 years late, let’s cut SpaceX some slack for building a profitable launch business with multiple successful vehicles while also being very close with Starship. > As of 2019, only three full-flow staged combustion rocket engines had ever progressed sufficiently to be tested on test stands; the Soviet Energomash RD-270 project in the 1960s, the US government-funded Aerojet Rocketdyne Integrated powerhead demonstration project in the mid-2000s,[6] and SpaceX's flight capable Raptor engine first test-fired in February 2019. [LINK] If you want to learn more [VIDEO]"},{"id":23499805,"author":"martythemaniak","month":"2020-06","story":"We could see an orbital launch of SpaceX’s Starship this year","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth look at the Raptor engine","quote":"Here's an in-depth video about Raptor","text":"Starship is a pretty radical redesign with the main goal being reusability. Everything about it is with reusability and and thus scale in mind. A new engine was needed because while the Merlin is a great engine, it could never be reused the thousands of times Raptor is designed to be reused. You can think of engine design as the simultaneous, interdependent optimization across 10+ dimensions, but as an example of one of dimensions - Raptor uses Methane (rather than Kerosene) because it burns cleaner (does not leave residue) and can be manufactured on Mars more easily. Here's an in-depth video about Raptor: [VIDEO] The rest of the rocket is similar, in that it is done with manufacturability, reusability and scale in mind. The Falcon design could never stretch to these demands."},{"id":26017434,"author":"gridspy","month":"2021-02","story":"SpaceX SN9 Explodes on Landing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Raptor's full-flow staged combustion achievement","quote":"","text":"Both you and I agree that aerospace companies should do large scale vehicle simulations, which they do. In addition SpaceX does complex fluid dynamics simulations to design and test their engines. That's likely part of why they have successfully made the \"holy grail\" of Full-Flow staged combustion work in the Raptor. [0] None of the failures so far could have been detected through simulation since they were complex integration issues. At some point the cost of simulation is vastly larger than the cost of just creating a test article. Components like drogue chutes, explosive bolts, failsafe valves and abort systems are typically validated both analytically (by reviewing designs) and are often hard to fully validate by testing. Since validating components is so expensive, it leads companies that rely on analyitical validation (like Boeing) to become overly attached to previously analysed and working components because analysis is so time consuming and expensive. You end up with modern rockets built with space-shuttle engines as components (SLS) and old jets with new engines tacked on (747 Max). When you talk about explosive deconstruction of a test article after something new was learnt you fail to realise the learning (not the landing) was the goal. If SN9 had landed correctly, it is very likely they would have deconstructed it anyway. They just deconstructed SN12, 13 and 14 to make way for SN15 which has a very improved design. 0: [VIDEO] \"Is SpaceX's Raptor engine the king o"}],"r":[["m1kHsd3Ocxc",1],["Hl1zEzVUV7w",1],["PqPGOhXoprU",1]]},"Uwl3-jBNEd4":{"wy":"HN points to it for a clean first-order framework for the rent versus buy decision.","q":{"x":"This guy breaks down the analysis very cleanly to a first-order approximation, using 2019 figures","i":48286730,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":48283630,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-05","story":"The Cost of Owning a Home","stance":"rec","why":"Handy 5% rule of thumb for rent versus buy","quote":"The 2019 video goes over a handy \"5% rule of thumb","text":"Ben Felix, a Canadian portfolio manager that does personal finance videos and podcast, has been arguing for years that rent versus buy is often a wash financially, and that you should make the decision for non-financial resasons. * recent 2026 video: [LINK] * 2025a: [LINK] * 2025b: [LINK] * 2021: [LINK] * 2019: [VIDEO] The 2019 video goes over a handy \"5% rule of thumb\": start with the purchase price of home, take 5% of that: if your rent is less than that, better numbers-wise to continue renting (and investing the difference); if your rent is more, probably better numbers-wise to buy/own. The company he works for created a tool to examine the numbers: * [LINK]"},{"id":48286730,"author":"Paracompact","month":"2026-05","story":"The Cost of Owning a Home","stance":"rec","why":"Clean first-order analysis of buying versus renting","quote":"This guy breaks down the analysis very cleanly to a first-order approximation, using 2019 figures","text":"The borrowed money isn't free from interest. There's no numerical sense in borrowing money at 5%+ interest to fund an investment appreciating at 3%. Some schemes might advantage it further (such as leveraging your mortgage with your retirement funds) but then we might as well also discuss further disadvantages compared to renting, such as upkeep and property taxes. Back around 2020 when mortgage rates very briefly dipped below 3%, there could have been an argument. But such is no longer the case and not likely to return soon. This guy breaks down the analysis very cleanly to a first-order approximation, using 2019 figures: [VIDEO] > It's a choice between $1m in housing appreciating at 3% or $100k in stocks appreciating at 9%. Just to drive the point home: It would be $100k in stocks appreciating at 9% and monthly rent subtracted, or $1m in housing appreciating at 3% and a $900k debt growing at 5%+ interest."},{"id":31531692,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-05","story":"Why the government took home prices out of its main inflation index","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Felix's 5% rule for deciding when buying makes sense","quote":"If you want to know when it makes financial sense, the \"5% Rule\" by Ben Felix is a decent place to start","text":"> You don't need to purchase a home - but historically - if you did - you've been much better off financially. [citation needed] Per the The Credit Suisse Global Investment Returns Yearbook 2018 , the global return for real estate from 1900 to 2017 was 1.3%, while stocks returns 5.2%: * [LINK] Rent is often cheaper than mortgage payments, and is very more often cheaper than mortgage payments plus the cost of maintaining a home. If you take the difference and investment you can have just as must equity in a few decades. Preet Banerjee goes over the math in this ten minute video: * [LINK] He rents: * [LINK] If you want to know when it makes financial sense, the \"5% Rule\" by Ben Felix is a decent place to start: * [LINK] * [VIDEO] Until recently he was a renter, but purchased a house 1-2 years ago. Not exactly happy with the decision though: * [LINK]"},{"id":29534977,"author":"throwaway9139","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: How do I create a social movement to change the world?","stance":"rec","why":"Why renting can beat buying financially","quote":"Check out","text":"Hi, Australian here living in Sydney. I'm a very happy renter. Renting in Sydney annually is way under 5% of the cost of owning a house. Even if I could afford to buy a house in Sydney, I wouldn't dream of it. It's much more economically sensible to rent. The money I save in opportunity costs from renting is huge. I can invest the difference between rent and mortgage repayments and don't need to be concerned with maintenance and other costs of owning. Check out [VIDEO] for more info."},{"id":26432650,"author":"Cicero22","month":"2021-03","story":"Renting Is Terrible. Owning Is Worse","stance":"rec","why":"Arguments that renting versus buying is not clear-cut","quote":"here's a video I think makes some good arguments about why it's not such a clear decision.","text":"I feel like this article could do a better job comparing renting and buying a home, so here's a video I think makes some good arguments about why it's not such a clear decision. [VIDEO] I'd be interested in seeing analysis of how this 3rd option would historically compare to renting and home ownership."},{"id":22134217,"author":"chii","month":"2020-01","story":"How housing became the world’s biggest asset class","stance":"rec","why":"Explains unrecoverable costs when comparing renting and buying","quote":"This video explains the concept really well","text":"This video explains the concept really well: [VIDEO] but TLDW; the unrecoverable cost of rent is just the rent money, and a lot of people compare it with a mortgage incorrectly. A mortgage is not 100% unrecoverable, but some portions of it is unrecoverable (the interest). The other unrecoverable cost of owning is the initial downpayment. The 3% capital cost is the combination of the above interest payment and the lost income opportunity from the downpayment. Whether you buy out right with cash, or get a mortgage, you still end up paying a price either way (via interest, or via lost opportunity to deploy the cash downpayment)."},{"id":39376827,"author":"miga89","month":"2024-02","story":"Over 50% of the population of Germany does not own their home","stance":"reference","why":"Rule of thumb: rent below 5% of property value","quote":"if your yearly rent is less than 5%","text":"When doing these rent vs buy comparisons people often neglect the opportunity cost of investing the down payment and any difference between rent and mortgage (if it exists) Rule of thumb according to [0] is that, assuming historical returns, if your yearly rent is less than 5% (1% maintenance + 1% property tax + 3% capital cost vs investing) of the property value it's better* to rent. [0] [VIDEO] *local tax treatment might play a role **paying a mortgage requires less discipline than consistently investing during volatile markets ***Purely from a financial, non-emotional standpoint ****you might not be able to rent the house you want to buy"},{"id":31528476,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2022-05","story":"Inflation is differential and restructuring (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I find it interesting that house prices are kept out of inflation. > The reason, as I understand it, is that shelter costs are kinda funny in how they are added to this statistic. Housing prices are not considered in the CPI (\"cost of living\") because houses are mostly an asset: > House prices are an interesting case. Houses are considered capital investment by the [US] BLS. So, when the value of your home increases that's a good thing as you didn't consume the house. In other words, you don't need to replace the house. Consumption goods are different in that you need to replace the thing you bought. Inflation is very bad for consumption goods because it costs you more to replace that thing each time you need it (food, for instance). * [LINK] > The BLS views housing as a mostly “investment” item as opposed to a consumption item. So, for instance, when you consume a hot dog and have to replace it then the cost of replacement is a direct reflection on your well-being. A $1 hot dog that costs $2 one year later is a material change in living standards, all else equal, since the hot dog is an asset that you literally consume. A house is much more complex. […] > Of course, anyone who owns a house knows that it’s not that simple. You do basically consume your house over time. For instance, my home has appreciated substantially since I purchased it just 5 years ago and underwent a hellish remodel. At that time the cost of replacement was roughly $300 per square foot. But in the ens"},{"id":25584033,"author":"carabiner","month":"2020-12","story":"Should I Buy a House?","stance":"reference","why":"The 5% rule on ongoing homeownership costs","quote":"see the 5% rule","text":"The thing is you still \"throw money away\" in owning a home through maintenance, see the 5% rule: [VIDEO] . There are large, unrecoverable costs in upkeep of a home. You will have to replace the roof in 15-20 years, just to have it not go to crap. That's $20k with no actual improvement to the home. Plumbing, outdoor siding, and so on. These would normally be covered by rent."}],"r":[["BCmz-fgp24E",2],["8wlld3Z9wRc",2],["ZuvK-od647c",1],["JRuNA2eK7w0",1],["rFqL9CkNxXw",1],["QPKKQnijnsM",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["nvK8fua6O64",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["YGLNyHd2w10",1],["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1]]},"ezVk1ahRF78":{"wy":"Recommended whenever population growth comes up, as a data-driven correction to common assumptions.","q":{"x":"Then watch this video to understand the real trends in population growth.","i":21061107,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":39279581,"author":"leipert","month":"2024-02","story":"The world is awful. The world is much better. The world can be much better","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Number of kids linked to child mortality and healthcare access.","quote":"Just can recommend this great talk","text":"Just can recommend this great talk: [VIDEO] Amount of kids is strongly linked to child mortality and access to health care access."},{"id":40316473,"author":"WA","month":"2024-05","story":"The world has probably passed peak pollution","stance":"rec","why":"Hans Rosling on population.","quote":"Check out this good old Hans Rosling video","text":"It's not. We need a sustainable size of population. Unlimited population growth is not sustainable. It doesn't mean that we are going to die out. It just means that there are as many newborns as old people dying. Check out this good old Hans Rosling video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21061107,"author":"familysized","month":"2019-09","story":"From Underwear to Cars, India’s Economy Is Fraying","stance":"rec","why":"Real trends in population growth.","quote":"Then watch this video to understand the real trends in population growth.","text":"> You are right, what nature actually cares greatly, is about exponential population growth with no regard for future sustainability. Perfect, I was waiting for you to say this. Now I have the chance to educate you (which should have been done by your \"superior western education\" but anyway). Read these articles and you will find that exponential growth is a myth. We are already close to replacement levels. The population is high in India and China only because of historic reasons - they are one of the most fertile lands on the planet. [LINK] [LINK] Then watch this video to understand the real trends in population growth. [VIDEO] Great! You finally rid yourself of these myths."},{"id":6658623,"author":"sdoering","month":"2013-11","story":"An interview with Bill Gates","stance":"rec","why":"Shows projected population limits and poverty reduction.","quote":"Hans Rosling showed it quite well in a TED talk he gave","text":"Funny thing is, we \"know\" relatively good, what the maximum of earths population will be and why. And we know (also relatively good) how fast we will lift people from poverty, given the actual and historical numbers. Hans Rosling showed it quite well in a TED talk he gave: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10774786,"author":"classicsnoot","month":"2015-12","story":"Nations Approve Landmark Climate Accord in Paris","stance":"reference","why":"Violence and poverty linked to high birthrates.","quote":"Areas with extreme violence, high poverty, and uncertain futures tend to have high birthrates","text":"Areas with extreme violence, high poverty, and uncertain futures tend to have high birthrates according to this Swede [1] FWIW [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RmIgJ64z6Y4",1],["2LyzBoHo5EI",1],["pL3Yzjk5R4M",1],["AGCmPLWZKd8",1],["7FeqF1-Z1g0",1]]},"B8Y146v8HxE":{"wy":"Recommended as an absorbing masterpiece of craftsmanship, often raised in threads about watches and clockmaking.","q":{"x":"It's also a masterpiece, just in a different medium","i":31268562,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31268562,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2022-05","story":"Mechanical Watch","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Masterpiece skeleton clock build series","quote":"It's also a masterpiece, just in a different medium","text":"This is lovely! While there's a lot of watch content on YouTube, I'm amazed that no one has called out Clickspring's skeleton clock build. It's also a masterpiece, just in a different medium: Full build playlist: [LINK] Direct link to the first episode: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15113418,"author":"KGIII","month":"2017-08","story":"One of the most complex wristwatches ever made","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Absorbing clockmaking series","quote":"It will eat up your time and it takes your complete attention.","text":"For those wondering about Clickspring, here is a link: [VIDEO] I advise you not to click on it. It will eat up your time and it takes your complete attention."},{"id":42441706,"author":"xdennis","month":"2024-12","story":"Making a watch from scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Playlist building a desk clock over roughly 1000 hours","quote":"Specifically this playlist where he builds a desk clock in an estimated 1000 hours","text":"Specifically this playlist where he builds a desk clock in an estimated 1000 hours: [VIDEO] The last video in the series summaries everything in 12 minutes: [LINK]"}],"r":[["jAhiMCSvtCc",1]]},"2EWejmkKlxs":{"wy":"Recommended as the best presentation on how modern CPUs run code, and why speculative execution matters for performance.","q":{"x":"The best presentation I've seen about CPU performance related to pipelining, branch prediction, and speculative execution","i":47757051,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47757051,"author":"inetknght","month":"2026-04","story":"Visualizing CPU Pipelining (2024)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best presentation on pipelining, branch prediction and speculative execution","quote":"The best presentation I've seen about CPU performance related to pipelining, branch prediction, and speculative execution","text":"The best presentation I've seen about CPU performance related to pipelining, branch prediction, and speculative execution was Chandler Carruth's \"Going Nowhere Faster\" presentation at CppCon 2017 [0]. I do recommend watching the whole presentation, but if you watch nothing else then just watch the 5 minutes or so from the linked timestamp. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18462716,"author":"pkaye","month":"2018-11","story":"Researchers discover seven new Meltdown and Spectre attacks","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why speculative execution is critical for performance","quote":"Chandler Carruth has a good talk with an example of why speculative execution is critical for performance.","text":"Chandler Carruth has a good talk with an example of why speculative execution is critical for performance. [VIDEO] It starts around 36m13s time frame."},{"id":15936228,"author":"inetknght","month":"2017-12","story":"Intel i7 loop performance anomaly (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on CPU performance and optimization","quote":"Tangential video worth watching","text":"Disclaimer: I am not an expert and have not measured. This is armchair theory. But, I would argue two things. First, the former appears to have at least one unaligned arithmetic: > 400538: mov 0x200b01(%rip),%rdx # 601040 <counter> ...while the latter's equivalent instruction is 4-byte aligned: > 40057d: mov 0x200abc(%rip),%rdx # 601040 <counter> So, I would argue that's the biggest source of _speedup_ in the second case. However, I'm really interested in whether that's true since I don't see a memory fence; so the memory should be in L0 cache for both cases; I have trouble believing that an unaligned access can be so much slower with the data in cache. As for the `callq` to `repz retq`, I would venture a guess that the CPU's able to identify that there are no data dependencies there and the data's never even stored; I'd argue that it probably never even gets executed because the instruction should fit in instruction cache and branch prediction cache and all. Arguably. Like I said, I'm not an expert. I'd say run it through Intel's code analyzer tool. [LINK] Tangential video worth watching: [VIDEO] Edit: actually, thinking about it, it's not unaligned access , it's unaligned math . I don't think that should affect performance at all? Fun."},{"id":38634204,"author":"mathiasgredal","month":"2023-12","story":"Ubuntu 24.04 LTS will enable frame pointers by default","stance":"reference","why":"Compiler talk prompting thoughts on registers and out-of-order execution","quote":"after watching a talk by a compiler guy","text":"Sure, but wouldn’t it make sense to extend the instruction set to allow the compiler to use these registers instead of reserving them for speculative / out-of-order execution? It was just a thought i had after watching a talk by a compiler guy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17392509,"author":"jasode","month":"2018-06","story":"DevTube: Searchable index of developer videos","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">The YouTube inteface says \"auto-generated\" Ok, I never noticed that. I just read the captions and assumed obvious misspellings were auto-generated. For example in this video[1], the caption text is \"L1D cache misses\" but he's actually saying \"L1-dcache misses\" . (The Linux terminal screen he's showing does display \"L1-dcache\" .) Even though that video is not labeled as \"auto-generated\", I assumed it was because of the bad caption. Based on your info, I guess CppCon uses humans like Mechanical Turk or other non-domain typists to manually add the captions. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xbBdIG--b58",1],["ARYP83yNAWk",1],["ZQFzMfHIxng",1],["B90_SNNbcoU",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["bY2FlayomlE",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1],["isBYV6QWDIo",1],["OOeBJ8mDr8Q",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["5Ge2RcvDlgw",1]]},"8COaMKbNrX0":{"wy":"HN links it in threads about athlete body types, sports technology and record-breaking as a relevant, counter-intuitive explanation.","m":[{"id":7847529,"author":"nichochar","month":"2014-06","story":"NFL players: height and weight over time ","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Argues athletes are not simply getting bigger and faster.","quote":"reminds me of a great TED talk about this","text":"Fasinating, reminds me of a great TED talk about this: \"Are athletes really getting bigger, faster, stronger?\" [VIDEO] tl;dr: no"},{"id":22271773,"author":"aledalgrande","month":"2020-02","story":"How the Nike Vaporfly War Was Lost","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant to technology driving records in sport.","quote":"I suggest you watch this TEDx for reference","text":"I can attest that running on a thick plated pair of shoes like the Zoom Fly SP I had is exhilarating, especially after training in other \"normal\" shoes. I see how the competition should measure athleticism and not a sponsor/athlete unit, but all recent records have been broken because of technology, let's be honest. I suggest you watch this TEDx for reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21507553,"author":"code_sloth","month":"2019-11","story":"I Was the Fastest Girl in America, Until I Joined Nike","stance":"rec","why":"Pro athletes reflect selection of specialized bodies for each sport.","quote":"Relevant TED talk","text":"Professional sports today are the more result of artificial selection of people that have specialized bodies for their sport. Relevant TED talk: [VIDEO]"}]},"PFc379hu--8":{"wy":"HN recommends it for Kay's blunt critique of today's computing and standard CS curricula.","q":{"x":"Alan Kay, almost impolitely, tears into today's computing while talking at CMU.","i":15850101,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":39652734,"author":"blessedonekobo","month":"2024-03","story":"Alan Kay's talk at UCLA – Feb 2024 [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite Kay talk, mostly unscripted.","quote":"This probably my favorite because it was mostly unscripted","text":"This probably my favorite because it was mostly unscripted Alan Kay : July 2007 : A Conversation with CMU Faculty & Students [VIDEO]"},{"id":15850101,"author":"jstewartmobile","month":"2017-12","story":"Comparison of GreenArrays Chips with TI Micropower Controllers (2010) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Kay sharply criticizes today's computing.","quote":"Alan Kay, almost impolitely, tears into today's computing while talking at CMU.","text":"Funny you should say that. Reminds me of this video[0] where Alan Kay, almost impolitely, tears into today's computing while talking at CMU. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15542824,"author":"skadamat","month":"2017-10","story":"The Future of Programming (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Technology-focused discussion at CMU.","quote":"I think you'll like the discussion he had at CMU a few years back","text":"I think you'll like the discussion he had at CMU a few years back, which focused a lot on the technology side of things [VIDEO]"},{"id":31310206,"author":"tomcat27","month":"2022-05","story":"A Case for an Open Source CS Curriculum [pdf] (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Kay argues against standard CS curriculum.","quote":"Alan Kay (Turing Prize 2004) argues against current standard CS curriculum.","text":"I'm glad an insider is thinking about this. But why do I have a feeling that it's written with an idea to make faculty life easy, not necessarily for a great student learning outcome, although that's what they say. Alan Kay (Turing Prize 2004) argues against current standard CS curriculum. [VIDEO] Anyway few unrelated thoughts. --- The undergrad CS courses mostly do not deviate in content.The variance is in quality. \"pick good professor over interesting course\" paid off for me in university. Factors such as these matter: course pace, instructor and TA accessibility, course slides and other material quality, lecture interesting or boring etc. --- Isn't it surprising that one of the most watched courses is by an YouTube tutor with a credential no american university will ever take seriously for hiring even an adjunct teaching faculty? See Intro to Algorithms by Abdul Bari on YouTube"},{"id":30947386,"author":"michael_j_ward","month":"2022-04","story":"We’ve got a science opportunity overload: Launching the Wolfram Institute","stance":"reference","why":"Kay on errors in formal CS education.","quote":"Alan Kay has part of the answer in this talk","text":"Alan Kay has part of the answer in this talk [0] where elucidates the errors taken by formal Comp Sci education. He claims there was a battle in the university between testable curriculum centered around Algorithms and builder curriculums based around hardware and distributed systems, and says it's a shame that algorithms won out. [0] Alan Kay : July 2007 : A Conversation with CMU Faculty & Students [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["TH9VCN6UkyQ",1],["OkmNXy7er84",1],["XFDM1ip5HdU",1],["If61baWF4GE",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["FvmTSpJU-Xc",1],["p2LZLYcu_JY",1],["N9c7_8Gp7gI",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"bmsV1TuESrc":{"wy":"HN commenters offer it as a sharp, funny answer to 'PC gone mad' and anti-woke arguments in discussions of language policy.","q":{"x":"there's an excellent 9-minute bit of stand-up comedy from Stewart Lee about political correctness you might want to watch","i":34062875,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34062875,"author":"stdbrouw","month":"2022-12","story":"Stanford's “Elimination of Harmful Language” Initiative","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Nine-minute stand-up on why politically correct language isn't gone mad.","quote":"there's an excellent 9-minute bit of stand-up comedy from Stewart Lee about political correctness you might want to watch","text":"Obviously there's much to laugh at in this initiative and a legitimate worry about thought being policed. That said, if you are open to changing your mind, there's an excellent 9-minute bit of stand-up comedy from Stewart Lee about political correctness you might want to watch [1] where he discusses why for example it's not \"political correctness gone mad\" to refer to a prostitute as a sex worker [2]. I do think the general impulse to try and create a kinder language is worthwhile, even if in practice it can spiral out of control due to virtue signaling and people with too much time on their hands. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27549247,"author":"geden","month":"2021-06","story":"What's Happened to the National Trust","stance":"rec","why":"Stewart Lee on wokeness and 'PC gone mad'.","quote":"Stewart Lee, another British institution, telling you all you need to know about ‘wokeness’, 10+ years ago.","text":"Stewart Lee, another British institution, telling you all you need to know about ‘wokeness’, 10+ years ago. [VIDEO] It’s extra charged in the UK because ‘PC gone mad’, please don’t show me inconvenient truth types, map neatly onto Brexit supporters."},{"id":11940925,"author":"zorpner","month":"2016-06","story":"Trump","stance":"rec","why":"Smart, amusing stand-up bit about political correctness.","quote":"Stewart Lee's smart (and amusing!) bit on the idea of \"political correctness","text":"He's right that we're drowning in political correctness... Trump's casual racism, misogyny, and conspiracy theories are without precedent... That's some impressive cognitive dissonance, sama. Those are two aspects of the same thought happening in Trump's mind. EDIT: Stewart Lee's smart (and amusing!) bit on the idea of \"political correctness\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":10266875,"author":"Apocryphon","month":"2015-09","story":"The Importance of Donald Trump","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The problem with critiquing political correctness is that there's no definite definition of what \"PC\" is. A lot of what's criticized as PC is merely leftist activism and liberal policies taken to extremes. Is multiculturalism and diversity PC? (and what exactly do those even mean in terms of specific policies?) Are sexual harassment lawsuits? Is affirmative action? Tort cases? The War on Christmas? The war against the War on Christmas? Is media censorship in favor of Israel, or media censorship in favor of Palestinians, PC? Public safety regulations ( [VIDEO] )? Why is being anti-Trump considered pro-PC? What specific statements do you consider disrespectful? I'm sure there are equivalent criticisms of Obama on HN, at least in terms of civil liberties as it relates to hackers (anti-NSA surveillance, pro-Net Neutrality, anti-drone). They can be found. How can something be even called \"politically correct\" when one lives in a society with multiple parties and political viewpoints? Is online shame culture inherently PC? Why is Cecil the Lion considered PC, while the online (and other media) campaign against Planned Parenthood, or ACORN, not considered PC? All include using the internet to create viral outrage. If you want to be against online shaming, sure be against it. If you're against excessive actions done in the name of leftist or liberal beliefs, then by all means be against them and be specific. I would be in favor of either of that. But don't try to call it under some n"},{"id":8625169,"author":"andy_ppp","month":"2014-11","story":"Uber Executive Suggests Digging Up Dirt on Journalists","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's not PC it's called the free market? If I don't like Uber I have the choice not to use them. Anyway, watch this :-D [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["x_JCBmY9NGM",1]]},"hdUbIlwHRkY":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on language purity and standardization for its clear take that languages are fluid and that national languages are constructed.","q":{"x":"Here's my favorite talk on the subject given by Chomsky, highly recommend a listen","i":38780817,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38780817,"author":"surfpel","month":"2023-12","story":"History-making SpaceX booster mostly destroyed in post-flight topple","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Chomsky talk on how language is flexible, evolving and individual","quote":"Here's my favorite talk on the subject given by Chomsky, highly recommend a listen","text":"\"Learning\" is also a noun [1] - which can be plural, so I'd say the creator of those comments has some learnings to make. More generally, though, being a grammar nazi is silly. Dictionaries are defined by the common use of language and language is quite flexible, evolving, unique to individuals, and even used to be much more geographically continuous than it is today.[2] 1 - [LINK] 2 - Here's my favorite talk on the subject given by Chomsky, highly recommend a listen (especially to the poster of those dead comments): [VIDEO]"},{"id":40651989,"author":"jcul","month":"2024-06","story":"Noam Chomsky 'no longer able to talk' after 'medical event'","stance":"rec","why":"Old Chomsky interview with a poignant final question","quote":"I love this old interview.","text":"I love this old interview. If you skip to the very last question at 26:50, it is a little bit poignant, considering this news. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31567010,"author":"random314","month":"2022-05","story":"France bans English gaming tech jargon in push to preserve language purity","stance":"rec","why":"Chomsky interview on language purity and national language being silly","quote":"Noam chomsky has an informative interview.","text":"Yes, the concept of nationalism and national language is a modern phenomenon. It is helpful in having subjects treat their governments as correct and moral by default and develop a distrust of their neighbors. Noam chomsky has an informative interview. He seems to think the concept of guardianship of a \"pure\" language is conceptually silly [VIDEO]"},{"id":35053070,"author":"random314","month":"2023-03","story":"Nalanda: The university that changed the world","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky on language and state-imposed standard languages","quote":"The nation state is a modern invention and this involves myth building about the country going back to ancient times.","text":"> If this is your sort of goalpost, then even relatively homogenous countries like Germany and Italy shouldn't really exist today, given that they only coalesced in the late 19th century. The strength of modern state propaganda is such that people have developed all kinds of crazy notions about history. The nation state is a modern invention and this involves myth building about the country going back to ancient times. The French spoken in southern France had much more in common with northern Italian. Its just common sense that it would be so. Modern French was forced down it's subjects just like Urdu in Pakistan, Standardized Mandarin in China and ongoing attempts for Hindu in India. The state has been elevated to the status of a mythical quasi religious entity. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28835857,"author":"speedcoder","month":"2021-10","story":"Peter Norvig Joins Stanford HAI","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky's view on the concept of language","quote":"Does language itself come from inventing a way to express what one wants to express?","text":"Hmm. Does language itself come from inventing a way to express what one wants to express? What would Chomsky say? [VIDEO]"}]},"jQIHqkudgNY":{"wy":"Recommended as an engaging, well-researched look at the history of gambling machines and how games borrow their psychology.","q":{"x":"it's a genuinely interesting exploration on the murky history of video games and gambling","i":34062985,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34062985,"author":"jterrys","month":"2022-12","story":"A real history of video games – Pay to Win","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Interesting exploration of murky history of video games and gambling.","quote":"it's a genuinely interesting exploration on the murky history of video games and gambling","text":"Though the video is fairly long, it's a genuinely interesting exploration on the murky history of video games and gambling. This is part two of a video series, I highly recommend watching both. Part 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41272444,"author":"maxbond","month":"2024-08","story":"A New Theory of Distraction (2015)","stance":"rec","why":"Video essay on slot machines and the machine state.","quote":"I watched an interesting video essay about slot machines recently[1]","text":"This is tangential but I watched an interesting video essay about slot machines recently[1], based on the book Addiction by Design by Natasha Dow Schüll, and one of the surprising things Schüll's research turned up in interviews with slot machine gamblers was that for some winning was jarring and not really satisfying. It took them out of the trance-like \"machine state,\" and their goal was not to win but to linger in the machine state. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38240772,"author":"AlexandrB","month":"2023-11","story":"Casino-like apps have drained people of millions","stance":"rec","why":"History and appeal of gambling machines.","quote":"I recently saw a good video[1] covering the history and appeal of gambling machines.","text":"I recently saw a good video[1] covering the history and appeal of gambling machines. My takeaway is that the primary reason people play these is not because they think they're going to win money. This especially true of pachinko where \"cashing out\" is a convoluted process of trading balls for prizes for cash. > If they never paid out, people would never go and the casinos would not exist This is self evidently false based on the linked article. Random chance games that have no possibility of ever having a net payout attract tons of players and make a lot of money. I think the problem is analyzing this with the economic \"rational consumer\" model of human behavior. Gambling is not rational but extremely popular because people are not rational. The whole industry is a trap that feeds users dopamine rewards in exchange for money. Once you're in, the payouts have little to do with it. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aY985qzn7oI",1],["lC4YLMLar5I",1],["eMmNy11Mn7g",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["4PHT-zBxKQQ",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1],["EiZhdpLXZ8Q",1],["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1]]},"BwGsr3SzCZc":{"wy":"Recommended as a non-technical, concrete case study of how metadata and phone records expose covert operations.","q":{"x":"The whole talk is worth watching.","i":17288634,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":21389816,"author":"theossuary","month":"2019-10","story":"Mass cellphone surveillance experiment in Spain","stance":"rec","why":"How the CIA rendition of Abu Omar was uncovered via phone data","quote":"There was a great talk about some of this from black hat","text":"It also seems like phones have their own tracking ids that they report, it's not just the sim card. There was a great talk about some of this from black hat; how the CIA renditioned Abu Omar out of Italy and how they were found out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17288634,"author":"shpx","month":"2018-06","story":"Sweden Tries to Halt Its March to Total Cashlessness","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on cell location data catching CIA agents in Italy","quote":"The whole talk is worth watching.","text":"Your phone's location can be silently triangulated based on what cellphone towers can see your phone and the strength of the signal to them. This is being done and you can buy the data from LocationSmart. This was discussed on HN a few weeks ago [0]. This data was used in Italy to catch CIA agents after they extrajudicially extradited (so to speak) a terrorist[1] in 2003 [VIDEO] . The whole talk is worth watching. The cost of storing this forever is negligible and going down. The government would be foolish or (in my opinion irrationally) principled not to hold on to this for the next 1,000 years. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":11416336,"author":"Robadob","month":"2016-04","story":"Why metadata is bad – 3 minute video your Mom will understand","stance":"rec","why":"Non-technical overview of how metadata exposed a 2003 CIA operation in Italy","quote":"It's by a non-tech journalist, so it's also largely non-technical and provides a very interesting overview of what metadata can expose.","text":"Longer video (25 minutes) of a talk from BlackHat 2013, about how metadata was used to expose an illegal(?) 2003 CIA operation carried out in Italy. It's by a non-tech journalist, so it's also largely non-technical and provides a very interesting overview of what metadata can expose. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18001647,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2018-09","story":"A German Couple Winds Up on U.S. No-Fly List","stance":"reference","why":"Black Hat talk on the Milan kidnapping","quote":"Black Hat talk","text":"Going to be hard and would be ineffective. For one, America still has several military bases in Germany alone e.g. Ramstein, which was used by the CIA in the Milan kidnapping [0][1]. [0] [LINK] [1] Black Hat talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16565341,"author":"closeparen","month":"2018-03","story":"Feds Bust CEO Allegedly Selling Custom BlackBerry Phones to Sinaloa Drug Cartel","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how phone serial numbers linked a CIA cell to the embassy","quote":"this is how Italian investigators were able to prove the link between a CIA cell and the American embassy.","text":"Both the SIM and the phone's serial number are available to the network. An analyst looking to correlate your activities can do so trivially. In fact, this is how Italian investigators were able to prove the link between a CIA cell and the American embassy. No one used the same SIM to talk to both groups, but they did use the same phone. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["X-iSQQgOd1A",1],["DkMwYxfSYNQ",1],["sI1C9DyIi_8",1],["DxL2HoqLbyA",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["fCd6B5HRaZ8",1]]},"Uv1UfLPK7_Q":{"wy":"Recommended in note-taking and personal wiki threads for its enthusiasm about granular, densely linked notes and TiddlyWiki.","q":{"x":"great talk by its creator Jeremy Ruston and the late creator of Erlang, Joe Armstrong","i":24825096,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":29182629,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2021-11","story":"Launch HN: Dendron (YC W21) – Structured note-taking for developers and teams","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong on granular, densely linked notes versus page-at-a-time wikis","quote":"Joe Armstrong (Erlang) gave at least one talk about it, available on YouTube","text":"Things which are deeply intertwingled. Joe Armstrong (Erlang) gave at least one talk about it, available on YouTube: [VIDEO] The key points were that Wikipedia style wikis force you into a page-at-a-time model. And TiddlyWiki uses more like a post-it-note sized amount of information. Wikipedia has a few links, TiddlyWiki items would have dense links to other notes. Then the experience of reading something would be like a 'choose your own adventure' book - click on a link and you don't get whisked away to a new page with all the context lost, you get the next note appearing below. It's building on the work of Ted Nelson and the early HyperText dreams from before the Web. As you click and explore, you build up a longer page of just the ideas and content you want, and don't have to write huge topic-wide essays and browse huge topic-wide pages all in one go. He called it \"the correct level of granularity, deeply intertwingled\". There were probably other important points I've forgotten. Relevantly for Dendron, the slide I've clicked onto in the YouTube has a quote: \" Hierarchical and sequential structures are usually forced and artificial. People keep pretending they can make things hierarchical, categorizable and sequential when they can't \" - Ted Nelson, 1974."},{"id":24825096,"author":"vector_spaces","month":"2020-10","story":"This page is a truly naked, brutalist HTML quine","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk by TiddlyWiki's creator and Joe Armstrong","quote":"great talk by its creator Jeremy Ruston and the late creator of Erlang, Joe Armstrong","text":"If you're interested in quines, see also TiddlyWiki[0] (and specifically this[1] great talk by its creator Jeremy Ruston and the late creator of Erlang, Joe Armstrong) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22009102,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2020-01","story":"Managing my personal knowledge base","stance":"rec","why":"Armstrong and Ruston enthuse about TiddlyWiki","quote":"there's a video of the Tiddly Wiki creator and him enthusing about it here","text":"Joe Armstrong of Erlang fame was a fan of Tiddly Wiki, and there's a video of the Tiddly Wiki creator and him enthusing about it here [VIDEO]"},{"id":40772621,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2024-06","story":"Show HN: Eidos – Offline alternative to Notion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"he met up with the creator to give a talk together about tiddlywiki","text":"Joe Armstrong of Erlang fame was a big fan of tiddlywiki, and he met up with the creator to give a talk together about tiddlywiki: [VIDEO] “Joe Armstrong & Jeremy Ruston - Intertwingling the Tiddlywiki with Erlang | Code Mesh LDN 18”"},{"id":25175930,"author":"a_c","month":"2020-11","story":"Show HN: A simple way to make HTML websites","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on what a single HTML file can achieve","quote":"A talk given by joe armstrong (author of erlang)","text":"While not javascript free, suggest one to look at [LINK] for what a single html file can achieve. A talk given by joe armstrong (author of erlang) [VIDEO] on it"}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"noQcWra6sbU":{"wy":"Recommended as a funny, engaging look at how Perl's quirks create real security vulnerabilities.","q":{"x":"try this great Perl presentation from Netanel Rubin","i":17995234,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":31218308,"author":"BonoboIO","month":"2022-04","story":"Experience Report: 6 months of Go","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"Best bash of a programming language","text":"Bit of offtopic: Best bash of a programming language „The Perl Jam: Exploiting a 20 Year-old Vulnerability“ [VIDEO] And part 2 „The Perl Jam 2 The Camel Strikes Back“ [LINK]"},{"id":17995234,"author":"lathiat","month":"2018-09","story":"Show HN: Wat","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, engaging Perl security talk exploiting a 20-year-old vulnerability.","quote":"try this great Perl presentation from Netanel Rubin","text":"If you loved that video, try this great Perl presentation from Netanel Rubin: [VIDEO] And it's follow-up cousin \"The Camel Strikes Back\" [LINK] (He says wat alot and it's hilarious)"},{"id":35652141,"author":"Kostarrr","month":"2023-04","story":"Why Perl?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Friends dont let friends write perl.","text":"Friends dont let friends write perl. Source: The legendary \"Perl Jam\" talk from the chaos communication congress. * [VIDEO] * [LINK] Also I find the \"ubiquity\" argument very side loped. What I could agree with is \"small/self-contained install\" (e.g. small single executable available for many platforms). But not sure how well perl fares there."}],"r":[["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["rNu8XDBSn10",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1]]},"0fpDlAEQio4":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a sampling of truly different programming paradigms beyond mainstream OOP.","q":{"x":"Check out this fantastic talk by Scott Wlaschin to get a sampling of truly different language paradigms","i":22401834,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22401834,"author":"yawaramin","month":"2020-02","story":"The Zen of Go","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sampling of truly different language paradigms and problem approaches.","quote":"Check out this fantastic talk by Scott Wlaschin to get a sampling of truly different language paradigms","text":"> I've learned many languages including C, Objective C, Java, Python, Ruby, JS, and some Haskell. You've learned basically the same language over and over again: Algol-family imperative-OO-mishmash. Haskell is probably the most unique out of all of them, and I'm inclined to think you haven't gone too far with it. Perhaps you know SQL though and recognize how a relational (roughly, set-oriented) language can work. My point is that I understand that Go offers you some compelling advantages. We're just saying that other languages offer some pretty amazing things too, and they are even better designed (as languages) than Go. Check out this fantastic talk by Scott Wlaschin to get a sampling of truly different language paradigms and how they approach problems: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20321703,"author":"souenzzo","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: Why do new(ish) programming languages eschew OOP features?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on languages with different paradigms.","quote":"I think we need languages that bring new paradigms.","text":"Rust, Go, Julia, Nim are all cruly braced, procedural, mutable, object oriented languages. I think we need languages that bring new paradigms. All that you quoted fail in this respect. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32649287,"author":"hazelnut-tree","month":"2022-08","story":"50 years Smalltalk anniversary celebration at Computer History Museum","stance":"reference","why":"Includes a Smalltalk demo at the 44 minute mark.","quote":"Here's a Smalltalk demo from the talk","text":"I posted a talk on HN recently about four programming languages from the 1970s - Smalltalk was one of the languages discussed. Smalltalk has a object-oriented programming model (OOP) with notable differences to the more familiar OOP model from languages like Java and C#. Smalltalk also has an windowing IDE (eschews separate text files), a consistent model, and minimal syntax (just 3 keywords). It's a very interesting language. Here's a Smalltalk demo from the talk: Four Languages from Forty Years Ago (2018) (44min mark): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IOiZatlZtGU",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["E8T_4zqWmq8",1]]},"HNziOoXDBeg":{"wy":"HN commenters point to Kitboga as an entertaining, righteous example of wasting scammers' time.","q":{"x":"One of my favourite videos so far involved “driving to a store” to get google play codes","i":25526595,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":25526595,"author":"jackweirdy","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What are some channels that make you glad YouTube exists?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Scammer baited with fake Google Play store while screen-sharing","quote":"One of my favourite videos so far involved “driving to a store” to get google play codes","text":"I have sunk many hours this year into watching Scott Brown Carpentry - a New Zealand Carpenter who is incredibly talented at video editing as well as carpentry [LINK] I’ve also enjoyed watching Kitboga - a twitch / YouTube personality who wastes phone scammers time in intricate ways, like building fake bank websites to lure them in if they use screen sharing. One of my favourite videos so far involved “driving to a store” to get google play codes, then redeeming them on a fake google play store while the scammer watched on screen share: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34150392,"author":"matthewmorgan","month":"2022-12","story":"Americans lost $10B; caused by illegal Indian call centres in 2022","stance":"rec","why":"Youtuber who wastes scammers' time","quote":"Kitboga is a youtuber who winds scammers up and wastes their time, and is pretty righteous","text":"My grandfather stopped answering his landline telephone because of these scumbags. Kitboga is a youtuber who winds scammers up and wastes their time, and is pretty righteous [VIDEO]"},{"id":43998293,"author":"modeless","month":"2025-05","story":"Coinbase says hackers bribed staff to steal customer data, demanding $20M ransom","stance":"reference","why":"Kitboga mentioned as ideal recipient for scam calls","quote":"Wish I could forward them to Kitboga[1].","text":"I have been receiving regular spear phishing calls from these guys, or someone who bought the leaked data, with classic tactics like claiming that I need to confirm a potentially fraudulent transaction. They speak perfect English with an American accent, sound very friendly, and have knowledge of your account balance. Thankfully on the first call I realized it was a scam right away, and Google's call screening feature takes good care of the rest. Wish I could forward them to Kitboga[1]. I guess they didn't have as much luck as they wanted scamming Coinbase's customers, and once they had their fun they decided to try extorting Coinbase themselves. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"NK1zlz67MjU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a window into Moore's minimalist CPU design and the GA144 chip, despite the slow pace.","q":{"x":"I was blown away by this 2011 video of Charles Moore talking about his CPU design and running code","i":12432722,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12432722,"author":"mmel","month":"2016-09","story":"Plan9 from?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moore demos his multi-node CPU generating video signals live","quote":"I was blown away by this 2011 video of Charles Moore talking about his CPU design and running code","text":"I was blown away by this 2011 video of Charles Moore talking about his CPU design and running code, talking about how the different nodes are creating the frequencies to produce the images you see on the projector - [VIDEO]"},{"id":15253477,"author":"exikyut","month":"2017-09","story":"What Makes AI and GPUs So Compatible?","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long, slow-paced overview of the GA144 chip","quote":"a (1hr long, somewhat slow-paced) presentation that provides a good overview of the GA144","text":"The following two points are highly tangential and don't squarely answer your question; I'm just mentioning them as things to file away in case they turn out to be relevant. - [LINK] shows an experimental new CPU architecture which appears to be single-core but demonstrates interesting power savings and speedups by eschewing standard on-chip caching and building a smarter compiler. TL;DR closed-source compiler, but I think the people making the design are trying to make the architecture design/registers/overview/etc as open as practical/viable with free documentation etc. I'm not sure if there's a simulator for it. I'm very sure they're already taking on commercial customers, and getting access to it is likely to be not overly nontrivial. (I realize you're not likely to need one just yet, but \"can't access at all\" makes for something much less interesting to file away.) - Forth, Inc. makes the GA144, a 144-core microcontroller that natively runs Forth (and will NOT comfortably run transpiled <any other language here>). At one point a production company was making evaluation boards. [VIDEO] is a (1hr long, somewhat slow-paced) presentation that provides a good overview of the GA144. I half wonder whether I'm looking at a chip designed by people who don't understand SMP when I watch this, but I also think that it's possible that the brutal simplicity that Forth (and Chuck Moore) applies to everything means that I'm really staring at the raw complexity of SMP here. I've read tha"},{"id":6667997,"author":"astrobe_","month":"2013-11","story":"Chuck Moore's Creations","stance":"reference","why":"Chuck Moore talk on the GreenArrays chip","quote":"There's some on youtube in the manufacturer's channel, Greenarray.","text":"There's some on youtube in the manufacturer's channel, Greenarray. [VIDEO] Also maybe try the designer's site, Chuck Moore. Google Colorforth."}],"r":[["2-oBNh0UkQc",1]]},"R3ZJKN_5M44":{"wy":"HN posts it as a favorite comic summary of debt avoidance and spending discipline, and as a joke in buy-now-pay-later threads.","q":{"x":"This is my favorite video that explains it all","i":34077996,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34077996,"author":"FrontierPsych","month":"2022-12","story":"The Cost of Being Poor: Why It Costs So Much to Be Poor in America","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sketch summing up credit card and spending discipline.","quote":"This is my favorite video that explains it all","text":"I understand what you're saying. However, I pretty much learned all there was to learn about them in 15 minutes when I got my first one. I read about them. I'm not the smartest person in the world, by far. If I can easily learn now, then anyone can learn this stuff on their own, in 15 minutes. It is NOT complicated. Getting information is exceptionally easy, and a LOT easier than when I got my first one. I knew from when I got my first credit card that I had to pay off the balance in full, every month. That using a credit card should be the exact same as using cash. You never buy anything unless you actually have the cash first. Then you can use the credit card and pay it off at the end of the month. It's so easy to understand. It doesn't matter about credit cards companies and their obfuscation. I didn't learn it from credit card companies. I just learned it because it was easy to learn. How difficult is it? Don't use a credit care unless you have the cash first . You pay it off in full and you don't pay interest, whatever level it is - .01% or 40% - it does not matter what the interest rates are, unless you don't pay on time. And the problem is that people don't care. They think emotionally, and don't give a crap about future ramifications. This is my favorite video that explains it all: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11320889,"author":"ezequiel-garzon","month":"2016-03","story":"Golden Rules for Making Money (1880)","stance":"rec","why":"SNL infomercial about avoiding debt.","quote":"SNL made a wonderful infomercial in 2006 about point 3: avoid debt.","text":"SNL made a wonderful infomercial in 2006 about point 3: avoid debt. Depending on your location you should be able to watch it in [1] or [2]. During my years in the US it always puzzled me that, upon reaching my credit limit, it would not just increase without any interaction, but sometimes double! American \"plastic\", as hailed in \"The Graduate\", was a significant lesson for me. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34523317,"author":"lkbm","month":"2023-01","story":"In Their 20s, Struggling to Save and Tired of Being Lectured About It","stance":"reference","why":"Shows financial prudence lectures long predate Gen Z.","quote":"","text":"> “I feel like the older generation is constantly pushing you to do stuff like they did when they were in their 20s, but it’s not even comparable to when they were in their 20s,” To be clear, much of the older generation didn't do this in their early 20. It's been a building concern for many decades. And they've been lectured about it forever, same as 20-somethings are today. It's not clear what \"older generation\" here here, but Millennials and Gen-X got stuff like SNL's 2006: \"Don’t Buy Stuff You Cannot Afford\" [VIDEO] This isn't \"Gen Z is poor; Boomers/Gen X/Millennials were rich\". This is \"I'm poor, and know some older people who aren't\". There's no shortage of people in their twenties making well into six figures. > Ms. German-Tanner said 20-somethings were often encouraged to take financial steps like build emergency funds, save for retirement and pay off debt. Well...yeah. It's good advice. It might be hard, but it's been hard for a sizeable chunk of the population for many, many decades. That's why there's constant push for it—without this emphasis, people just borrow and spend. True in the 70s, true in the 90s, and true today."},{"id":49140569,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2026-08","story":"From MIT: AI financial advice is surprisingly good","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of that Saturday Night Live skit from decades ago","text":"While I agree with you about the level of financial illiteracy in the general population, I don't really see what AI has to add for the vast majority of the population is simple. Basic financial advice is not hard (save regularly, invest in low cost index funds, don't take on CC debt, etc.), but a lot of it goes against most human nature, especially around delayed gratification. People have known for decades that \"diet and exercise\" are very important for good health, yet we still have an obesity epidemic. It reminds me of that Saturday Night Live skit from decades ago, \"Don't Buy Stuff You Cannot Afford\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":39490844,"author":"Terr_","month":"2024-02","story":"Quality is a hard sell in big tech","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It reminded me of that “Eat Less, Move More” SNL skit Or another skit \"Don't buy stuff you cannot afford.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":35343774,"author":"hn_throwaway_99","month":"2023-03","story":"Apple introduces Apple Pay Later","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Saturday Night Live had it spot on years ago","text":"Ahh, more \"innovation\" from the tech industry. So much BS that is easily a net negative to society. Saturday Night Live had it spot on years ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34368385,"author":"__s","month":"2023-01","story":"The 'Buy Now, Pay Later' Bubble Is About to Burst","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"which advertises a self help financial book whose contents are","text":"See the SNL skit: Don't Buy Stuff [VIDEO] which advertises a self help financial book whose contents are \"If you don't have the money, don't buy it\""}]},"Z0oFJ2jbkDI":{"wy":"Recommended as an example of earth-sheltered housing and as part of a channel HN loves for architecture and efficiency.","q":{"x":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes","i":20246851,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20246851,"author":"cwkoss","month":"2019-06","story":"Swedish Couple Builds Greenhouse Around Home","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Earth-cooled container home among favorites from a prolific architecture channel.","quote":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes","text":"I can't recommend Kirsten Dirksen's channel enough to people interested in architecture, energy efficiency, tiny homes, 'green' construction, etc. Literally hundreds of hours of high-quality content - I'm really curious how she is able to find all of these interesting people who are doing such cool things. Channel: [LINK] Some other favorites: Baubotanik shapes living tree branches into building facades - [LINK] Boeing retiree finds meaning inventing micro homes & high speed trikes - [LINK] Extreme transformer home in Hong Kong: Gary Chang's 24 rooms in 1 - [LINK] Earth-cooled, shipping container underground CA home for 30K - [VIDEO] Yokohama narrow tiny house \"breathes\" & attracts local nature - [LINK]"},{"id":17689573,"author":"jay-anderson","month":"2018-08","story":"An office building in Seattle that doesn't have air conditioning","stance":"rec","why":"Example of an earth-cooled underground home.","quote":"Earth sheltered homes seems like a great way to do that","text":"I live in the desert as well. I'd love to have a house with better insulation to spend less on AC. Earth sheltered homes seems like a great way to do that (though there are other problems - e.g. moisture, heavier roof). [VIDEO] , [LINK] - here's a couple examples (she has visited others as well. Short of that I'd settle for a basement. Someday perhaps."},{"id":29722422,"author":"gremlinsinc","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Tiny Home for $5k USD?","stance":"reference","why":"Similar shipping container home example.","quote":"","text":"I think they had someone w/ some engineering skill do it, so it was structurally sound and all... I'm not sure if this is the exact same one I saw, but it's similar enough... [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WB2-2j9e4co",1],["4Z75A_JMBx4",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1]]},"sKOk4Y4inVY":{"wy":"HN users recommend Moglen as a brilliant speaker and a top introduction to free software philosophy, and cite this talk in privacy and crypto-wars discussions.","q":{"x":"none seems to beat those from Stallman's lawyer, Eben Moglen.","i":9239473,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9239473,"author":"belorn","month":"2015-03","story":"Dear Dr. Stallman: An Open Letter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best talk to introduce someone to free software philosophy","quote":"none seems to beat those from Stallman's lawyer, Eben Moglen.","text":"When it comes to well formulated talks about free software, none seems to beat those from Stallman's lawyer, Eben Moglen. He knows very well how to speak to a crowd, and tends to use local references and languages to explain concepts (professor of law and history of law). Stallman has excellent skills in seeing problems in software before they get noticed by everyone else, but when I want to introduce someone to the philosophy of free software I would rather put on a talk like Freedom of Thought Requires Free Media from 2012 or the now 11 year old talk Die Gedanken Sind Frei from 2004. 1: [VIDEO] 2: [LINK]"},{"id":4817219,"author":"brabram","month":"2012-11","story":"Megaupload Assisted FBI vs NinjaVideo, But Evidence Then Used Against Them","stance":"rec","why":"Eben Moglen is a brilliant speaker","quote":"Just take a look at Eben Moglen[0], he is a very brilliant speaker and a brilliant man in general.","text":"Just take a look at Eben Moglen[0], he is a very brilliant speaker and a brilliant man in general. Or Jérémie Zimmermann[1] for the European side. Or Benjamin Bayart for the French side. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] (in french)"},{"id":32392280,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2022-08","story":"Doomed to repeat history? Lessons from the crypto wars of the 1990s (2015) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen anecdote on losing battle against public key encryption","quote":"","text":"From an old comment of mine on the topic: [VIDEO] [1] 1. \"In 1995, there was a debate at Harvard Law School – four of us discussing the future of public key encryption and its control. I was on the side, I suppose, of freedom. It’s where I try to be. With me at that debate was a man called Daniel Weitzner who now works in the White House making Internet policy for the Obama administration. On the other side was the then Deputy Attorney General of the United States and a lawyer in private practice named Stewart Baker who had been chief council to the National Security Agency, our listeners, and who was then in private life helping businesses to deal with the listeners. He then became, later on, the deputy for policy planning in the Department of Homeland Security in the United States and has much to do with what happened in our network after 2001. At any rate, the four of us spent two pleasant hours debating the right to encrypt and at the end there was a little dinner party at the Harvard faculty club, and at the end, after all the food had been taken away and just the port and the walnuts were left on the table, Stuart said, “All right, among us now that we are all in private, just us girls, I’ll let our hair down.” He didn’t have much hair even then, but he let it down. “We are not going to prosecute your client, Mr. Zimmermann,\" he said. “Public key encryption will become available. We fought a long, losing battle against it, but it was just a delaying tactic.” And then he"},{"id":28105138,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2021-08","story":"Why I Wrote PGP (1999)","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen anecdote on losing battle against public key encryption","quote":"","text":"From an old comment of mine on the topic: [VIDEO] [1] 1. \"In 1995, there was a debate at Harvard Law School – four of us discussing the future of public key encryption and its control. I was on the side, I suppose, of freedom. It’s where I try to be. With me at that debate was a man called Daniel Weitzner who now works in the White House making Internet policy for the Obama administration. On the other side was the then Deputy Attorney General of the United States and a lawyer in private practice named Stewart Baker who had been chief council to the National Security Agency, our listeners, and who was then in private life helping businesses to deal with the listeners. He then became, later on, the deputy for policy planning in the Department of Homeland Security in the United States and has much to do with what happened in our network after 2001. At any rate, the four of us spent two pleasant hours debating the right to encrypt and at the end there was a little dinner party at the Harvard faculty club, and at the end, after all the food had been taken away and just the port and the walnuts were left on the table, Stuart said, “All right, among us now that we are all in private, just us girls, I’ll let our hair down.” He didn’t have much hair even then, but he let it down. “We are not going to prosecute your client, Mr. Zimmermann,\" he said. “Public key encryption will become available. We fought a long, losing battle against it, but it was just a delaying tactic.” And then he"},{"id":25098136,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2020-11","story":"A court ruling in Austria could censor the internet worldwide","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen talk supporting arguments for online anonymity","quote":"","text":"> but (...) truly people need to be responsible for what they post online. I strongly disagree. Anonymity is an important and integral part of the internet and the free speech it enables. Moving towards this sort of thing will only be used for abuse and censorship. It saddens me how much this has become an accepted position, even on hn, and I'm quite tired of all the justifications for giving up important rights, anonymity is closely related to privacy for example, just because of some scarey boogeyman of the day. Anonymity offers the ability to criticize entities (governments, corporations, people), to communicate in ways more freely than otherwise, to discuss personal issues without fear, to have a presence online without stalkers or other abusers attack, it also avoids the most common rhetorical abuse I see, which is overreliance on argument from authority by separating the identity of the person making the argument from the argument itself, and I could go on. I could go on... Do you really think the vast amount of negatives that come with your proposal would be worth the tradeoff? [VIDEO] [1] 1. \"In 1995, there was a debate at Harvard Law School – four of us discussing the future of public key encryption and its control. I was on the side, I suppose, of freedom. It’s where I try to be. With me at that debate was a man called Daniel Weitzner who now works in the White House making Internet policy for the Obama administration. On the other side was the then Deputy Attorney "},{"id":21846537,"author":"arminiusreturns","month":"2019-12","story":"Show HN: HN Card, Hacker News Profile Overlay","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen talk on privacy and self-censorship","quote":"I tend to always think about this Eben Moglen talk (listen from timestamp for 4 or 5 mins)","text":"I tend to agree. I know you are referring to karma but it also applies to comments. A few times I've found myself doing the same and then chastise myself for it. I think making comment histories private might be a potential solution, some are moving for reddit to do this. I am honestly torn, as I progress in my career and life, as a sort of contrarian type, I get more and more worried some background check algo is going to find my presence on platforms, troll my history, and find some random comment from 5 years ago that might be controversial and blackball me for it, unbeknownst to me. On the other hand, I hate the idea that we increasingly self censor as we all become more aware of this. This is why I still think anonymity is important on the internet, and lean towards burning accounts every couple of years on commenting platforms. This is also why I still value what others might call cesspools that are the chans, and of course onion sites et al. I tend to always think about this Eben Moglen talk (listen from timestamp for 4 or 5 mins) : [VIDEO]"},{"id":13985907,"author":"arca_vorago","month":"2017-03","story":"VPNs are not the solution to a policy problem","stance":"reference","why":"Moglen on the 90s crypto wars","quote":"","text":"> We have police, laws, community rules, all of these things to protect our houses and personal security. If you leave the door unlocked, robbing it is still a crime. Likewise, if you walk around on an unsafe neighbourhood and get robbed, it would be ludicrous to hear \"well, the city warned you that part is unsafe, so the police isn't going to investigate\" The irony of this statement is that this actually happens quite often in certain east of the track neighborhoods, especially when the victim is a minority. It goes to show that this attitude, while I don't agree with it, isn't so far from the reality as you might think. Coming from out west, this is one of the cultural reasons I am pro-gun. The police are just there to draw the chalk line around your body, it is your responsibility to defend yourself, your loved ones, and your home. Always remember that the constitution was created to protect, not establish rights, rights that you have independent of the constitution itself, and of these rights, the right to self defense is one. The second amendment is simply about defense against tyranny. Even if you got rid of the second amendment I still have the right to bear arms. Which makes me wonder, how well could the right to self defense argument be applied to encryption? It's almost like everyone forgot about the 90's crypto wars, but it makes me think of something Eben Moglen said about the 90's crypto wars being just a temporary setback to TPTB; [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["5PmHRSeA2c8",1]]},"Ev373c7wSRg":{"wy":"Posted as relevant satire in threads on wokeness and identity politics, with some saying it is barely an exaggeration.","m":[{"id":28414432,"author":"vimy","month":"2021-09","story":"How did American “wokeness” jump from elite schools to everyday life?","stance":"rec","why":"Satirical skit commenter says reflects what wokes actually say.","quote":"Nothing in this video is an exaggeration.","text":"Yes, 100 %. I listen to what wokes say and do. [VIDEO] Nothing in this video is an exaggeration. They literary say these things."},{"id":26395504,"author":"aeoleonn","month":"2021-03","story":"Four times I felt discriminated against for being a female developer","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous take on wokes and racists agreeing.","quote":"Check out Ryan Long for a humorous example","text":"What’s funny to me is that people are only complaining about diversity in the US and Europe. Although I have seen a few articles about class based diversity in Latin America and India. Maybe I am missing something but I don’t see complaints about diversity in Arabia, Persia, African Regions, Asian regions. Yet there are historic multi-ethnic conflicts in most of not all of these areas. Take the Hausa, Fula, Igbo, and Yoruba of Nigeria for example, as an example. Ultimately, dividing people and calling it “diversity” resembles a divide and conquer tactic for reducing the cohesion of a society, making it easier to control (via narratives and reducing my inter-group trust compared to some central, approved authority), in my view. Check out Ryan Long for a humorous example: “When Racists and Woke Agree On Everything”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42689035,"author":"llm_nerd","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">It's funny, I can't think of one. Dwarf, midget, retarded, idiot, oriental, cripple, gypsy are just a start. Facticity doesn't actually seem to be on your side. >As you see it Woke to me is when people are doing performative moralizing that benefits absolutely no one, all to achieve moral supremacy, and often to the ends of white shame (only ever targeting whites, particularly white males). The \"cargo cult\" article that might still be on the front page is the perfect example. It's is absolutely true that there are right wing groups that think a woman existing in media is \"woke\". A woman not having adequately large breasts in a video game is \"woke\". But on the flip side, there was a \"woke\" push that meant that every lead character everywhere had to be female, even better a minority female, even better a lesbian minority female, maybe with a handicap. Every commercial had to feature a mixed race or same sex couple, etc. When that becomes obvious and apparent to everyone, people do naturally start to suspect and see ghosts everywhere. It is an incredibly obvious outcome of pushing something to the points of parody. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26860634,"author":"busymom0","month":"2021-04","story":"Word usage guidance and alternative terms","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> They then assign different treatments/rights to the different segregations It's what Yuri Bezmenov refereed to as useful idiots. Divide and conquer has been used by those in power to rule over the constituents for centuries. They realized that the best way to prevent people from looking at the real enemy (establishment) was to keep them infighting and the best way to do that is to divide people into as many oppressor oppressed buckets as possible and let them fight each other. So now we have people not just fighting about rich vs poor, now it's based on race, sex, sexual preference, ethnicity etc. While we keep fighting each other, the corporations and establishment elites keep getting richer and more powerful, meanwhile our lives keep getting more and more miserable. This identity politics is also used by corporations and politicians to prevent and deflect blame. Want your $2000 stimulus? Want a minimum wage? Want wall Street, Robinhood accountability? Want universal health care? Want an end to forever wars? You won't get any of that and if you question it, you are being sexist for attacking the first secretary of the treasury (paid off by Wall Street and $800G by Robinhood hedge fund), first black secretary of defence (Raytheon board seat), first trans health minister (took out her own mother while forcing covid patients into nursing homes). It is evil yet genius identity politics and people are too afraid to speak up. \"Racism is not dead. But it is on life-support, kept "},{"id":26436697,"author":"f154hfds","month":"2021-03","story":"You can't censor away extremism or any other problem","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The youtube algorithm suggested this to me the other day, posting it here because it's pretty relevant.","text":"Disclaimer: I don't watch this channel, can't vouch for other vids. The youtube algorithm suggested this to me the other day, posting it here because it's pretty relevant. When Wokes and Racists actually agree on everything: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29582377,"author":"gorwell","month":"2021-12","story":"Power imbalances and sex","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What would you call it when someone is obsessed with race and views skin color as paramount? Squeezing everything through that lens and making judgements on that basis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28415388,"author":"busymom0","month":"2021-09","story":"How did American “wokeness” jump from elite schools to everyday life?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant comedy skit","text":"I am brown and I can’t believe what the western countries are doing with going back to racial segregation days. Instead of teaching everyone to work hard snd accomplish hard things, they are being taught that they can’t achieve hard things because the boogeyman is stopping them. Victimhood doesn’t help kids. Even the most successful black and brown people like Lebron James who achieved what they did via working hard know this but they don’t want to teach that to the kids. It’s mind boggling. Now a days there’s literally no difference between a woke and a racist. Both believe in judging people by their skin color instead of the content of their character. Relevant comedy skit: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26072612,"author":"jimbokun","month":"2021-02","story":"John McWhorter: The Neoracists","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this","text":"> Show interest in multiculturalism. But do not culturally appropriate. What is not your culture is not for you, and you may not try it or do it. But—if you aren’t nevertheless interested in it, you are a racist. Reminds me of this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24725314,"author":"tuna-piano","month":"2020-10","story":"Justice Department sues Yale for illegal practices in admissions","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant (satirical) video","text":"Shocked me when I first heard of it! Liberals and racists agree on a fair number of policies regarding race these days. Relevant (satirical) video: [VIDEO]"}]},"GcfLZSL7YGw":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an accessible pop-science introduction to entropy and the physics of life, including Jeremy England's work.","q":{"x":"it's a very good video on this topic by PBS","i":38672012,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38672012,"author":"donny2018","month":"2023-12","story":"NASA study finds life-sparking energy source and molecule at Enceladus","stance":"rec","why":"Very good basic explanation of life and entropy.","quote":"it's a very good video on this topic by PBS","text":">but life organizes Yes, and that's the interesting part. In the right circumstances, more entropy doesn't mean less complexity. Matter can become more complex and less entropy locally if that results in increase of total entropy in the system. This can occur, for example, if we have constant flow of concentrated energy on some local areas of the environment that continuously wants to spread out. Because the system as a whole \"wants\" to maximize its entropy as fast as it can, and it statistically \"searches\" for the shortest paths possible. This may result in natural occurrence of more and more complex molecules that dissipate heat more and more efficiently. It's like the planet's atmospheric processes, for example. Where differences in heat concentration don't just spread out in linear gradients but can form complex structures like vortexes, hurricanes or even stable geometric hexagons (like the famous hexagon storm on Saturn), etc. >My view has always been that life fights against entropy. Yes, it \"fights\" against entropy to exist, but it exists solely by processing low entropy into high entropy. Maybe that's why it even exists in the first place? This part is better addressed in Jeremy England's work, for more basic explanations you can refer to videos like this: [VIDEO] (it's a very good video on this topic by PBS)"},{"id":28725411,"author":"efokschaner","month":"2021-10","story":"Anil Seth Finds Consciousness in Life’s Push Against Entropy","stance":"rec","why":"Pop-science treatment of the subject with references.","quote":"This subject is discussed in the pop sci work of PBS spacetime","text":"This subject is discussed in the pop sci work of PBS spacetime [VIDEO] with reference to articles and books if you're looking for more"},{"id":22850953,"author":"julienchastang","month":"2020-04","story":"Entropy and Life","stance":"reference","why":"Where the commenter discovered Jeremy England's work.","quote":"I recently discovered the work of Jeremy England [1] on this topic on a PBS Space Time YouTube video","text":"I recently discovered the work of Jeremy England [1] on this topic on a PBS Space Time YouTube video [2] dedicated to the origins of life. England's work dates back a few years (2012) and seems like it was an exciting advancement in the field but was also controversial. Does anyone here know the status of this line of research? [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"JpqnNCx7wVY":{"wy":"HN commenters offer it as a good example of what Perl 6 does well and as a way to get started with the language.","q":{"x":"I recommend a look at a couple of videos to get started too","i":11409814,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11409814,"author":"pplu","month":"2016-04","story":"Wow, Perl 6 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Video to get started with Perl 6.","quote":"I recommend a look at a couple of videos to get started too","text":"You can go to [LINK] , download it, and play with it now :) I recommend a look at a couple of videos to get started too: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":10169414,"author":"keeperofdakeys","month":"2015-09","story":"Perl 6 hands-on tutorial – A small introduction to a big language [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Example of good things coming out of Perl 6.","quote":"There are also definitely some good things coming out of perl 6","text":"As someone who is relatively new to perl, I think perl 6 is definitely a positive direction. It removes lots of inconsistencies, while providing a clean base for future expansion. For something like the sigils, what's more important - to know that a variable is a scalar, or that it's some kind of hash/array? The second would definitely be less confusing when learning the language. There are also definitely some good things coming out of perl 6, like this [VIDEO]"},{"id":10342757,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2015-10","story":"Larry Wall Unveils Perl 6.0.0","stance":"reference","why":"Additional Perl 6 talk offered alongside another link.","quote":"","text":"The typing and grammar mechanism aren't common, it may influence other languages. Also, Perl managed to survive even without mainstream relevance so the community will probably keep enjoying it. ps: I found this talk pretty convincing about pragmatism [LINK] also: [VIDEO]"}]},"Q8oCilY4szc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an entertaining and thorough overview of Boeing's decline whenever the company's safety problems are in the news.","q":{"x":"There is a very nice episode of Last Week Tonight by John Oliver diving into it","i":41343297,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41343297,"author":"cube2222","month":"2024-08","story":"NASA announces Boeing Starliner crew will return on SpaceX Crew-9","stance":"rec","why":"Deep dive into Boeing's troubles.","quote":"There is a very nice episode of Last Week Tonight by John Oliver diving into it","text":"There is a very nice episode of Last Week Tonight by John Oliver diving into it, accessible on YouTube[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39661373,"author":"stevenwoo","month":"2024-03","story":"U.S. is said to open criminal inquiry into Boeing","stance":"rec","why":"Entertaining segment covering the Boeing issues.","quote":"It’s aimed at entertainment but the latest Last Week Tonight covered this and is on YouTube.","text":"It’s aimed at entertainment but the latest Last Week Tonight covered this and is on YouTube. Hope this links works from my phone [VIDEO]"},{"id":40011128,"author":"belter","month":"2024-04","story":"Another Boeing whistleblower says he faced retaliation for reporting 'shortcuts'","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Kayak let's you select: [VIDEO] Also historical flight data like on [LINK] Will let you go back hundreds of flights, and find the airplane the airline uses most of the time, for that flight."},{"id":39673881,"author":"Bender","month":"2024-03","story":"Boeing whistleblower found dead in US","stance":"reference","why":"Widely viewed segment where employees say they won't fly Boeing.","quote":"","text":"I hope the company had no involvement in this. It would be bit futile given such a large audience has seen their employees state they won't fly in the aircraft they build. [1] 3,716,935 views 4 days ago and Blancolirio's assorted incident reports [2] [1] - [VIDEO] [video][32 mins][john oliver] [2] - [LINK]"},{"id":39792784,"author":"gigel82","month":"2024-03","story":"U.S. sues Apple, accusing it of maintaining an iPhone monopoly","stance":"reference","why":"Cited to argue for more regulation.","quote":"what we need is a hell of a lot more regulation, not less","text":"> Apple should be another Boeing, a company that exists to embody its regulation That is pretty funny because you're right, Apple is becoming another Boeing - a profit-above-all-else corporate asshole and just like Boeing what we need is a hell of a lot more regulation, not less: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ZQc9-seU-5k",1],["YKuRkGkf5HU",1]]},"Qaq9vlfoGnA":{"wy":"Cited in anti-piracy discussions as a clear explanation of clever retro copy-protection tricks, though one commenter disputes a claim about Leander.","q":{"x":"I thought the two methods described in this video were both good","i":16301753,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":28814356,"author":"LaserDiscMan","month":"2021-10","story":"The Coding Secrets Hidden in “Sonic the Hedgehog” [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite among Burton's videos, on copy protection via destroyed floppy sectors.","quote":"Another one of my favorite ones describes copy protection via intentionally destroyed floppy sectors","text":"For those who don't know, Burton explains this in depth here: [LINK] Another one of my favorite ones describes copy protection via intentionally destroyed floppy sectors: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16301753,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2018-02","story":"Gaming's most fiendish anti-piracy tricks (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Two good anti-piracy methods explained.","quote":"I thought the two methods described in this video were both good","text":"I thought the two methods described in this video were both good: [VIDEO] Also, the method described here, even though it didn't result in a financially successful game, was pretty clever (pirating the game meant your game company would be defeated by pirates): [LINK]"},{"id":16302301,"author":"mizaru","month":"2018-02","story":"Gaming's most fiendish anti-piracy tricks (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Jon Burton's account of Leander's disk-damaging protection.","quote":"According to Jon Burton, something like this was actually done for the Amiga game Leander","text":"According to Jon Burton, something like this was actually done for the Amiga game Leander, although not with the intent to damage hardware: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40470305,"author":"amiga386","month":"2024-05","story":"The Space Quest II Master Disk Blunder","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I have the original release of Leander. There are no holes in the disks.","text":"I can give you an example of someone claiming there were holes in the disk, Jon Burton, but they're a goddamn liar : [VIDEO] I have the original release of Leander. There are no holes in the disks. The code on the disk doesn't write anything besides hiscores. There is a protection routine exactly where he says there is, however what it does is check for a long track . It waits for the index pin, reads lots of data from the track, then looks to find two sync marks in the data it read, and they're at least a certain distance away from each other. No lasers, no holes, no writing. Standard long track protection. Here's the whole routine: [LINK] Here's a page that more accurately describes floppy disk protection methods (and also explains what a long track is): [LINK]"}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1],["ZZs2HUW9tDA",1]]},"YUU7_BthBWM":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a fantastic developer commentary on how Doom's first episode was made.","q":{"x":"in this fantastic developer commentary he did for the game's first episode with IGN","i":16789284,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16789284,"author":"doomlaser","month":"2018-04","story":"Why I’m Using the Cerny Method of Game Development","stance":"rec","why":"Romero's developer commentary on Doom's first episode and e1m1.","quote":"in this fantastic developer commentary he did for the game's first episode with IGN","text":"Yeah, John Romero explains that the famous e1m1 was the last level he designed for the original Doom, in this fantastic developer commentary he did for the game's first episode with IGN, for the game's 20th anniversary: [VIDEO] 'the classic horseshoe design' - show inaccessible teasers of later areas of the stage near the beginning to drive the player forward"},{"id":15760740,"author":"jonathankoren","month":"2017-11","story":"When is it OK to remake a classic game?","stance":"rec","why":"Playing Doom with John Romero commentary.","quote":"I did enjoy watching","text":"The idea of a re-releasing a classic game with running commentary is interesting, but I can't imagine that there's much of an appeal for commentary during the gameplay. Conversely, I did enjoy watching \"We Play Doom With John Romero\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":10911505,"author":"some-guy","month":"2016-01","story":"John Romero has released his first Doom level in over two decades","stance":"reference","why":"Romero's walkthrough of Doom's first episode.","quote":"","text":"All of the Daikatana and Ion Storm jokes aside, what Romero was able to achieve from a level-design perspective in 1993 with Doom, given that such an engine had never existed before, was pretty mind-boggling. I wish I had a chance to take game design classes with him now that he's a professor at my alma-mater (UC Santa Cruz), especially after seeing his walkthrough of Knee-Deep In The Dead on YouTube recently[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["PLyEO0jNt6M",1]]},"dQGnwKBxAKk":{"wy":"Recommended as a high-level, in-depth take on SMIC's 7nm progress and Asian chip manufacturing.","q":{"x":"Probably one of the best high level insights into tech mfg in Asia.","i":39927369,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39927369,"author":"zer00eyz","month":"2024-04","story":"Intel discloses $7B operating loss for chip-making unit","stance":"rec","why":"High-level insight into tech manufacturing in Asia and SMIC","quote":"Probably one of the best high level insights into tech mfg in Asia.","text":">> Western FDI constituted negligible part of PRC domestic investment as % of GDP... US made it geopolitically difficult to invest in PRC Foxconn leaving at apples behest (and the Foxconn owner getting vocal with the prc in a rare move). The banking exoudus from Hong Kong (now deep into its 2nd year). And the impact to the GDP... its a literal house of cards with the failure of ever grand with country garden hot on its heels... Meanwhile the retail apocalypse has hit china. High end luxury brands. Out. Grocery chains, shutting down. Finally the iron bowl, the local government workers are getting pay cut. (because land sales are a shitty source of funding)... Post pandemic there was going to be a realignment of global supply chains to be multi source and closer to home. Chinas covid zero policy just accelerated a decisions already made. >> Every component has domestic ventures to replicate. These have been facing up for decades. Without EUV they have no path forward. There is no EUV without Zeiss and ASML. Meanwhile unless your TSMC with their knowledge your going to have dismal yeilds. [VIDEO] Probably one of the best high level insights into tech mfg in Asia. >>PRC hard tech sectors have exploded in employment and compensation. Broad unemployhment 5% which means new graduates get jobs eventually. There's new youth unemployment stats to discount students in tertiary like west, it's fine at ~20%. Youth? [LINK] Tech sector: [LINK] [LINK] The decline of Chinese tech from stem to"},{"id":32307182,"author":"chrisjc","month":"2022-08","story":"TSMC Completes Construction of 5nm Fab 21 in Arizona","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of SMIC's 7nm progress and how far behind it is","quote":"While stating that SMIC might still be around 4-6 years behind the competition, I was completely shocked to hear it was only that.","text":"Asianometry released a video yesterday related to this. [VIDEO] While stating that SMIC might still be around 4-6 years behind the competition, I was completely shocked to hear it was only that. The next few years are going to be some very interesting times."},{"id":32691838,"author":"igravious","month":"2022-09","story":"US Government Bans Export of Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs to China and Russia","stance":"reference","why":"In-depth detail on SMIC demonstrating 7nm production","quote":"SMIC demonstrating 7nm production ( as detailed in depth in this video by Asianometry","text":"Nah. Your interpretation is undoubtedly correct. I've been on HN 12 years now and I've become used to the increase in Western chauvinism over the recent years – mostly all of it led by US sentiment. The implication above is that the US is part of the civilized world (colloquially called \"The West\") and that China (and presumably Russia, and Iran, and Venezuela, and Cuba, et al) are part of the uncivilized world. See, for instance, on this page where Hume speaks of civilized versus uncivilized forms of government: “This chapter examines Hume's conception of government. It considers three forms of government that Hume distinguishes: barbarous monarchy, civilized monarchy, and free government (with its two subdivisions, limited monarchy and republic).” So civilized is contrasted with barbarous . [LINK] You'd think with SMIC demonstrating 7nm production ( as detailed in depth in this video by Asianometry: [VIDEO] ) you'd think that the US had gotten the hint that the tech cat is out of the bag – or the tech genie has escaped the lamp or whatever metaphor you prefer … A comparison of SMIC's 7nm process versus the competition: [LINK] My prediction is that SMIC joins TSMC, Intel, Global Foundries, and Samsung at the top tier of semiconductor manufacturing by 2030, along with an ecosystem of Chinese design and manufacturing companies. In doing so the West's stranglehold on other companies like Huawei will be over. If Russia can survive the punitive Western sanctions until then it'll "},{"id":32645444,"author":"fakethenews2022","month":"2022-08","story":"SMIC reaches 7nm without access to western equipment and technologies","stance":"reference","why":"Cited alongside claims about TSMC talent moving to SMIC","quote":"","text":"Supposedly a high level TSMC executive moved to SMIC in 2017 and became co-CEO. So did 200 TSMC engineers. There was a lawsuit a few years ago that TSMC won but it before all this. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["RSTNhvDGbYI",1]]},"ir0FAa8P2MU":{"wy":"It is recommended in threads on the F-35 and defense procurement as a funny, mostly factual look at how requirements balloon, with some warnings about its accuracy.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended. It's available for free on youtube.","i":32056829,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37564814,"author":"TylerE","month":"2023-09","story":"Marine Corps orders safety stand-down of all aircraft after F-35 disappearance","stance":"rec","why":"HBO film on failed military procurement, mostly based on fact","quote":"HBO did a great made-for-TV movie on this called the \"Pentagon Wars","text":"I don't know why the military can't learn from a string of failed systems going back to at least the '80s. 1. Make Tough Requirements 2. Get Prototype 3. Prototype attracts some interest 4. Now the brass additionally wants capabilities X, Y, Z 5. Prototype now costs twice as much and is more failure prone, and actually less mission-capable due to all the bolt-ons 6. Program costs goes up due to all the changes 7. We can't afford this! 8. Cut unit acquisitions by 50% 9. Wait? Our unit cost just nearly doubled because the real costs were all in design/R&D? 10. Now we REALLY can't afford this. 11. Cut orders by another 75% 12. So finally approximately 10% of the original order actually gets built, at a total program cost 2 or 3x what it was supposed to be, and a unit cost that's massively huge. 13. Program fails in the mid-to-long term due to maintenance costs and falling spares inventory. HBO did a great made-for-TV movie on this called the \"Pentagon Wars\", which while fictionalized is only about 20% actually made up: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32056829,"author":"viggity","month":"2022-07","story":"Lockheed Martin Prepar 3D","stance":"rec","why":"Comedy film about the Bradley procurement saga","quote":"Highly recommended. It's available for free on youtube.","text":"there is an old made-for-HBO movie called The Pentagon Wars... I presume it is accurate enough, although it was turned into a comedy so I'm sure they took some artistic license with it. Highly recommended. It's available for free on youtube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34885431,"author":"meltyness","month":"2023-02","story":"OODA Loop","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is not untrue, but a more applicable interpretation of this conceptual framework might go as follows: In an AAR[0] operators explain risks, causes, and limitations of the system. This provides a formal structured language for describing how, precisely, supporting systems can be improved and how they impact all specific aspects of operator interaction. Since the operator is viewed simply as a networked control-system in this case, the cognitive path from structure, supporting physical system, and then physical quantities needing improvement is unmistakably clear, as is required in successful planning, acquisitions, operations, and maintenance.[1] Essentially it makes subjective experience concrete, and is prophylactic against bikeshedding/blame-gaming. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26593016,"author":"black6","month":"2021-03","story":"The F-35 may be unsalvageable","stance":"reference","why":"Satirical film on Pentagon procurement","quote":"The Pentagon Wars is available for viewing on YouTube.","text":"This has all happened before, and it will all happen again. The Pentagon Wars is available for viewing on YouTube. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37564874,"author":"throw0101b","month":"2023-09","story":"Marine Corps orders safety stand-down of all aircraft after F-35 disappearance","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I would not take it, or the book it was based on, completely at face value","text":"> HBO did a great made-for-TV movie on this called the \"Pentagon Wars\", which while fictionalized is only about 20% actually made up: [VIDEO] Great in that it is entertaining, but I would not take it, or the book it was based on, completely at face value: > The Pentagon Wars was made to be a comedy first and an adaptation of the Pentagon Wars book second. It was never meant to be an accurate portrayal of the Bradley program and it shows. You're getting Hollywood taking an an axe-grinding, half-true memoir as fact and then playing it for laughs. * [LINK] See also perhaps: > Although the Bradley destroyed many enemy tanks and experienced fairly low rates of casualty in the First Gulf War and the Iraq War, the vehicle’s recent battlefield performance was not acquired through efficient planning and project management. The origins of the modern Bradley span decades of troubled development; the involvement of many stakeholders; inflexible and questionable requirements; a failure of contractors to make design tradeoffs for fear of losing capability; billions of dollars in R&D costs; and numerous Congressional interventions, including House Armed Service Committee hearings investigating Bradley testing protocol.1 The Bradley has been described at different times, by military officers and historians, as a weapon that means “all things to all people,” a “quintessential hybrid,” and “a proverbial camel...that does nothing well”.2 * [LINK]"}],"r":[["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1]]},"isBYV6QWDIo":{"wy":"HN uses it as the source on India's early fab attempts, in threads about India's semiconductor ambitions.","q":{"x":"India had sound policies it was quite ahead of time when it created SCL, check this video for more","i":43867245,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43867245,"author":"newusertoday","month":"2025-05","story":"Zoho halts $700M semiconductor plan","stance":"rec","why":"Shows India had sound early semiconductor policy but weak execution","quote":"India had sound policies it was quite ahead of time when it created SCL, check this video for more","text":"india does not lacks it, its not price competitive as compared to china which has better supply chain and economy of scale. India and other countries are not going in fab for jobs but for self-reliance. Going for high-value semicon later is similar to the logic of removing poverty first before investing in space tech. It doesn't work that way. India had sound policies it was quite ahead of time when it created SCL, check this video for more [VIDEO] it does lacks execution though and had some bad luck. India does have 130nm plants right now its obsolete node but works fine for low volume needs of its govt."},{"id":30129263,"author":"pkaye","month":"2022-01","story":"India Semiconductor Mission","stance":"rec","why":"Covers India's early fab attempt, built before Taiwan had one","quote":"Never knew they had built a fab before Taiwan had one.","text":"There is a YouTube video about India's attempt at semiconductor fabs. Never knew they had built a fab before Taiwan had one. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40658802,"author":"teitoklien","month":"2024-06","story":"Intel is trucking a 916k-pound 'Super Load' across Ohio to its new fab","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for sabotage at India's SCL fab in the 1980s","quote":"","text":"most definitely protected, and very expensive insurance too. > gunfire in transit Not just gunfire, they need to protect it from - Climate Activists, blocking road and sabotaging the instrument, like they destroy old paintings at museums and art galleries to bring awareness via headlines [1] - Foreign Adversary Intentionally Sabotaging Equipment to prevent America from leading in Semiconductor ( Had happened to India’s SCL silicon lab when they were catching up with market in 1980s ) [2] - And ofcourse Gun Slingers too [1]( [LINK] ) [2]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":30127105,"author":"sinyug","month":"2022-01","story":"India Semiconductor Mission","stance":"reference","why":"Documents India's failed 1980s semiconductor attempt","quote":"The initial attempt failed in spectacular fashion back in the 1980s.","text":"The initial attempt failed in spectacular fashion back in the 1980s.[1] Given that the same incompetent bureaucracy still runs the country, this is never going to happen. [1] India's Semiconductor Failure ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":28833740,"author":"baybal2","month":"2021-10","story":"Sony to join TSMC on new $7B chip plant in Japan","stance":"reference","why":"Source for India's earlier government-backed fab attempts","quote":"","text":">India's first forays into semiconductor fabrication in the 80s and 90s were likewise enthusiastically supported by the government [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2EWejmkKlxs",1],["bSkpMdDe4g4",1],["OOeBJ8mDr8Q",1],["gwf5mAlI7Ug",1],["5Ge2RcvDlgw",1]]},"nLjFTHTgEVU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an interesting look at the psychology research behind bias in hiring and performance reviews.","q":{"x":"Google has a training video that talks through some interesting psychology research that ties to hiring / performance reviews","i":13321627,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13321627,"author":"garysieling","month":"2017-01","story":"Harvey Mudd College Attempting to Address Tech Diversity Issues","stance":"rec","why":"Psychology research on unconscious bias in hiring and performance reviews.","quote":"Google has a training video that talks through some interesting psychology research that ties to hiring / performance reviews","text":"For the people who are interested in this topic, Google has a training video that talks through some interesting psychology research that ties to hiring / performance reviews - [VIDEO] I haven't been able to locate many similar good talks, so if anyone can point me to speakers / talks I'd appreciate it."},{"id":9087951,"author":"africandawg","month":"2015-02","story":"Female Founders Conference – Live Stream [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on removing bias in the workplace","quote":"Here's an interesting talk titled Unconscious Bias @ Work | Google Ventures.","text":"Maybe it'd be helpful to not discriminate against other genders and have other gender specific conferences too. Maybe we can divide the groups by race too. You know, so people are comfortable. Maybe I am too simple but I do no understand segregation. Neither do I understand \"reverse\" discrimination; for the lack of a better choice of words. Anytime you have something going on where if you reverse the \"minority\" with \"majority\" labels and the result sounds bad, I feel like the original was bad too. For down voters: Please feel free to leave a comment explaining your stands. Also, people who are interested in the ideas of removing bias, scientifically backed arguments exist. The touchy-feely-ride-the-trend ones are just more talked about. It's like picking the color of a bike shed. Here's an interesting talk titled Unconscious Bias @ Work | Google Ventures. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17257007,"author":"denzil_correa","month":"2018-06","story":"Alphabet shareholders reject diversity proposal backed by employees","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source on unconscious bias.","quote":"","text":"You might also want to look into \"Disparate Impact\" [0] and \"Unconscious Bias\" [1]. > For example, if XYZ Company hired 50 percent of the men applying for work in a predominantly male occupation while hiring only 20 percent of the female applicants, one could look at the ratio of those two hiring rates to judge whether there might be a discrimination problem. The ratio of 20:50 means that the rate of hiring for female applicants is only 40 percent of the rate of hiring for male applicants. That is, 20 divided by 50 equals 0.40, which is equivalent to 40 percent. Clearly, 40 percent is well below the 80 percent that was arbitrarily set as an acceptable difference in hiring rates. Therefore, in this example, XYZ Company could have been called upon to prove that there was a legitimate reason for hiring men at a rate so much higher than the rate of hiring women. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"xEDZQtAHpX8":{"wy":"Recommended as the best introduction to modern SQL standard features that textbooks skip.","q":{"x":"the whole talk is good","i":34207893,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34207893,"author":"scythmic_waves","month":"2023-01","story":"Easy, alternative soft deletion: `deleted_record_insert`","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on interesting SQL standard additions since 92.","quote":"the whole talk is good","text":"I happened to fall down this rabbit hole a few days ago because of another HN thread. In it, a commenter mentioned [1] a talk by Markus Winand where he goes over some of the more interesting additions to the SQL standard since 92, including versioned tables [2] (I've linked to the timestamp, but the whole talk is good). I got real excited about that feature because I could think of a few tables at work that could use it. Sadly, PostgreSQL doesn't implement it [3] (I can't permalink to the feature but you can search for \"T180\", \"System-versioned tables\", \"T181\", or \"Application-time period tables\"). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":19150310,"author":"mongol","month":"2019-02","story":"SQL: One of the most valuable skills","stance":"rec","why":"Summarizes the modern foundation of SQL databases.","quote":"I recommend you look at one of Markus Winand's presentations","text":"I recommend you look at one of Markus Winand's presentations, such as this one : [VIDEO] I felt it summarized well the modern foundation of SQL databases."},{"id":19298682,"author":"jamie_ca","month":"2019-03","story":"Ask HN: What made you change your mind about a programming language/paradigm?","stance":"reference","why":"Talk covers SQL:2011 system versioning support across databases.","quote":"part of SQL 2011 called System Versioning","text":"That feature's \"coming\" - part of SQL 2011 called System Versioning, currently supported in DB2 and SQL Server, but available in MariaDB 10.3 Beta according to this talk: [VIDEO]"}]},"3UnB1fomvAw":{"wy":"HN cites it as a showcase of memory-corruption exploits achievable using only normal controller inputs, often alongside other arbitrary-code-execution speedruns.","q":{"x":"One of my favourites","i":6633011,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6633011,"author":"Shish2k","month":"2013-10","story":"Discoveries Ten Years Later in Zelda Speedrun","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Glitches corrupt memory using only normal Game Boy inputs.","quote":"One of my favourites","text":"One of my favourites; not technically a speed run, but a festival of glitches being used to corrupt memory in a very specific way, using only the regular gameboy inputs :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":6826124,"author":"comex","month":"2013-12","story":"Writing a Program in Pokemon Blue","stance":"reference","why":"Example of exploits used in tool-assisted speedruns.","quote":"These sorts of \"exploits\" have also been used in Pokemon and other games for extremely fast tool-assisted speedruns","text":"These sorts of \"exploits\" have also been used in Pokemon and other games for extremely fast tool-assisted speedruns: [VIDEO] (Pokemon Yellow) [LINK] (Super Mario World) [LINK] (EarthBound)"},{"id":6826728,"author":"chilldream","month":"2013-12","story":"Writing a Program in Pokemon Blue","stance":"reference","why":"Pokemon Yellow arbitrary code hack for Pi day.","quote":"It's neat how many glitches people have found in these particular games.","text":"It's neat how many glitches people have found in these particular games. A couple other Pokemon \"arbitrary code\" hacks (in the Yellow version): [VIDEO] (Pi day celebration) [LINK] (proof-of-concept for a clojure-to-pokemon-to-assembly compiler) A \"normal\" playthrough in which the author shows off various glitches (most without any kind of cheat device): [LINK]"},{"id":5432911,"author":"mlex","month":"2013-03","story":"Super Mario World \"Completed\" in Under 3 Minutes by Corrupting the RAM","stance":"reference","why":"Arbitrary code execution in Pokemon Yellow.","quote":"There's also a run where someone executes arbitrary code in Pokemon Yellow","text":"There's a Pokemon Yellow run which finishes the game in under a minute, without leaving the starting room: [LINK] There's also a run where someone executes arbitrary code in Pokemon Yellow: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wROQUZDCIMI",1]]},"HgWHeT_OwHc":{"wy":"HN cites it as evidence of Google's cloud commitment and for Jeff Dean's talk-and-code demo.","q":{"x":"I was on edge of my seat as I watched him talk and code things up at the same time.","i":19001296,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19001296,"author":"ceocoder","month":"2019-01","story":"Your live coding demo is boring me (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Jeff Dean talking and coding simultaneously in a demo","quote":"I was on edge of my seat as I watched him talk and code things up at the same time.","text":"As usual leave it up to Jeff Dean to be an outlier, this[0] is his talk/demo at GCP Next 2016 - I was on edge of my seat as I watched him talk and code things up at the same time. He is awesome. Oh and Gary Bernhardt's talk WAT[1] which happens to be one of my favorite talk of all time. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":11732541,"author":"hyperopt","month":"2016-05","story":"Google supercharges machine learning tasks with TPU custom chip","stance":"reference","why":"Jeff Dean demo of Cloud ML with 20-way parallelism","quote":"Jeff Dean demos Cloud ML on the GCP.","text":"In March at the GCP NEXT keynote [1], Jeff Dean demos Cloud ML on the GCP. He casually mentions passing in the argument \"replicas=20\" to get \"20 way parallelism in optimizing this particular model\". GCE does not currently offer GPU instances. I've never heard the term replicas in the GPU ML discourse. These devices may enable a type of parallelism that we have not seen before. Furthermore, his experiments are apparently using the Criteo dataset, which is a 10GB dataset. Now, I haven't looked into the complexity of the model or to what extent they train it to, but right now that sounds really impressive to me. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11389925,"author":"vgt","month":"2016-03","story":"Google launches Public Datasets program","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote cited as evidence of Google's cloud commitment","quote":"Just last week Sundar (CEO), Eric Schmidt (chairman), Jeff Dean, Urs Holzle, and Diane were on stage talking about how they spent $10 billion","text":"This narrative gets repeated time and time again, and it really doesn't hold up to even surface debate: - Google TI and Cloud merged. Same teams. Per Urs Holzle in Jan2014 - Just last week Sundar (CEO), Eric Schmidt (chairman), Jeff Dean, Urs Holzle, and Diane were on stage talking about how they spent $10 billion last year investing in infrastructure and how serious Google is about cloud ( [VIDEO] ) - In many cases, publically available technologies are actually replacing internal technologies (BigQuery and Dremel, Dataflow and FlumeJava/Millwheel for example). - Disney, Coca Cola, Snapchat, Spotify, Home Depot are just some of the customers running on Google Cloud. It'd be very foolish to abandon these. - If you watch the day two GCP NEXT keynote, you'll see how Google is the cloud leader in environmental responsibility and open source. We're the good guys :) ( [LINK] ) Happy to debate further!"}]},"HxSHIpEQRjs":{"wy":"It is the go-to explanation of how CPython's JIT works and why its design is so simple.","q":{"x":"I recommend that people watch Brandt Bucher's \"A JIT Compiler for CPython\" from last year's CPython Core Developer Sprint[0].","i":38925455,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":38925455,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2024-01","story":"Python 3.13 Gets a JIT","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows current CPython JIT implementation, limitations, and low complexity","quote":"I recommend that people watch Brandt Bucher's \"A JIT Compiler for CPython\" from last year's CPython Core Developer Sprint[0].","text":"You're right, and in this case \"foundational work\" even undersells how minimal this work really is compared to the results it already gets. I recommend that people watch Brandt Bucher's \"A JIT Compiler for CPython\" from last year's CPython Core Developer Sprint[0]. It gives a good impression of the current implementation and its limitations, and some hints at what may or may not work out. It also indirectly gives a glimpse into the process of getting this into Python through the exchanges during the Q&A discussion. One thing to especially highlight is that this copy-and-patch has a much, much lower implementation complexity for the maintainers, as a lot of the heavy lifting is offloaded to LLVM. Case in point: as of the talk this was all just Brandt Bucher's work. The implementation at the time was ~700 lines of \"complex\" Python, ~100 lines of \"complex\" C, plus of course the LLVM dependency. This produces ~3000 lines of \"simple\" generated C, requires an additional ~300 lines of \"simple\" hand-written C to come together, and no further dependencies (so no LLVM necessary to run the JIT. Also \"complex\" and \"simple\" qualifiers are Bucher's terms, not mine). Another thing to note is that these initial performance improvements are just from getting this first version of the copy-and-patch JIT to work at all , without really doing any further fine-tuning or optimization. This may have changed a bit in the months since, but the situation is probably still comparable. So if one person "},{"id":40554218,"author":"Qem","month":"2024-06","story":"Copy-and-Patch: Fast compilation for high-level languages and bytecode (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Copy-and-patch chosen for CPython JIT","quote":"This is the algorithm chosen for the CPython JIT under development","text":"Yes. This is the algorithm chosen for the CPython JIT under development, scheduled for inclusion from 3.13 onwards. See [VIDEO]"},{"id":38786019,"author":"acqq","month":"2023-12","story":"What's up Python? Epic CPython commit, Django 5 and 2FA for PyPI","stance":"reference","why":"Cites JIT performance versus LuaJIT from the talk","quote":"Only 35% slower overall than LuaJIT\" as per video from a month ago.","text":"\"Only 35% slower overall than LuaJIT\" as per video from a month ago. No idea which kind of tests are these \"overall\" though. [VIDEO] Slides (pg. 31 performance): [LINK]"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["4bM3Gut1hIk",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1]]},"ycPr5-27vSI":{"wy":"Recommended as a long, wide-ranging look into Musk's thinking, especially on AI and the future of humanity.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend listening to the entire 2.5 hour Joe Rogan podcast[1]. It really does give a nice glimpse into his mind","i":17939218,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17939218,"author":"nodesocket","month":"2018-09","story":"Company Update","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long Rogan conversation with Musk covering AI, simulation, climate and his mindset.","quote":"Highly recommend listening to the entire 2.5 hour Joe Rogan podcast[1]. It really does give a nice glimpse into his mind","text":"Highly recommend listening to the entire 2.5 hour Joe Rogan podcast[1]. It really does give a nice glimpse into his mind, and at times he appears to almost start choking up talking about fossil fuels and climate change. Once they get into the whiskey it actually gets more interesting, from evils of AI, to life being a simulation, to thinking positive and asserting as a whole judges, police, and military are some of the finest people we have. You can tell Elon is fed up with the constant negativity toward people and pervasive pessimism fueled by social media. I also honestly think he is probably dealing with depression, as you can almost see it in his face and speech. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18073416,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What podcasts do you listen to regularly?","stance":"rec","why":"Musk discusses his ideas, AI and humanity's future with Rogan.","quote":"They basically discuss a bunch of his ideas along with things such as AI and the future of humanity.","text":"Joe Rogan Experience [0]. I love that he sits down with people and actually has long in-depth conversations with them. Here's a few interesting ones: * Let's start with his Elon Musk discussion [1]. It came out 2 weeks ago and it already has 14m views. They basically discuss a bunch of his ideas along with things such as AI and the future of humanity. It's fascinating to get a peek into the kind of person he is. * His discussion with Matthew Walker has been one of the most enlightening ones for me [2]. He's a sleep expert, and in the podcast they go over different myths relating to that. There was recently a HN post [3] where some people were quoting him and his book. * Another big favorite of mine was his discussion with Paul Stamets [4], a mycologist (i.e. a mushroom expert). They discuss some of the effects of different kinds of mushrooms, including psilocybin mushrooms (colloquially known as magic mushrooms). * I loved his discussion with Gary Johnson [5], the Libertarian Party's presidential candidate for 2016. How many other presidential candidates would you imagine would be willing to sit down and answer hard-hitting questions for +2 hours? He also had a discussion with Larry Sharpe [6], the Libertarian Party's candidate for governor of New York in 2018. Full disclosure: I recently joined the Libertarian Party. Listening to these podcasts pushed me to learn more about their movement. * He had a discussion with Mikhaila Peterson [7], the daughter of Jordan Peterson, whi"},{"id":29945090,"author":"neillyons","month":"2022-01","story":"It’s not still the early days of blockchain","stance":"reference","why":"Musk describes internet content as a projection of our limbic system.","quote":"A lot of the content on the internet is a \"projection of our limbic system\".","text":"Reminds me of something Elon Musk said. A lot of the content on the internet is a \"projection of our limbic system\". (Elon saying this [VIDEO] ) I see the craze around crypto the same way. It is a representation of something in our limbic system. Perhaps greed?"},{"id":18745331,"author":"unixhero","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: Best talks of 2018?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Joe Rogan has had an incredible year. Joe Rogan interviews Dr. Andrew Weil [LINK] Joe Rogan interviews Elon Musk [VIDEO] Joe Rogan interviews James Hetfield [LINK]"},{"id":17946197,"author":"apo","month":"2018-09","story":"Isaac Asimov on The David Letterman Show (1980) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Musk discusses the future of AI and cybernetics with Rogan.","quote":"The middle segment of Musk's interview with Rogan was about the future of AI and cybernetics.","text":"Strange as this may sound, this reminds me of Joe Rogan's interview with Elon Musk: [VIDEO] Some of Asimov's predictions about the future were... off. The middle segment of Musk's interview with Rogan was about the future of AI and cybernetics. It will be very interesting to watch that interview in 40 years."},{"id":18018676,"author":"noetic_techy","month":"2018-09","story":"How Puerto Rico Became a Tax Haven for the Super Rich","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This was talked about at length on Joe Rogans show by Peter Schiff: [VIDEO] I think its a net benefit to Puerto Rico to be in this position."}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["SAc0vQCC6UQ",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["EWX5B6cD4_4",1],["zz4YVJ4aRfg",1],["64cEmjtwRgw",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["Cyya23MPoAI",1],["mPqWstVnRjQ",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1]]},"03p2CADwGF8":{"wy":"HN cites it as primary-source history of AI hype and skepticism, and for the vintage Freddy robot footage.","q":{"x":"Anyone interested in the History of AI can also watch the report being presented by Lighthill","i":21721294,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21721294,"author":"AndrewOMartin","month":"2019-12","story":"Review of “Artificial Intelligence: A General Survey” (1993)","stance":"rec","why":"Lighthill report presented and debated by McCarthy, Michie, Gregory","quote":"Anyone interested in the History of AI can also watch the report being presented by Lighthill","text":"Anyone interested in the History of AI can also watch the report being presented by Lighthill, and subsequently debated by John McCarthy, Donald Michie, and Richard Gregory. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43627113,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2025-04","story":"DeepMind program finds diamonds in Minecraft without being taught","stance":"reference","why":"1970s robot assembling puzzle with machine vision","quote":"A robot assembling a puzzle with machine vision! And it's only from the 1970's.","text":"Nice robot demo. Here's another one: [VIDEO] A robot assembling a puzzle with machine vision! And it's only from the 1970's."},{"id":38982551,"author":"huppeldepup","month":"2024-01","story":"The Global Project to Make a General Robotic Brain","stance":"reference","why":"1974 demo showing state of AI research then","quote":"I'd like you to view this demo from 1974 that showcases the then current state of research in AI.","text":"I'd like you to view this demo from 1974 that showcases the then current state of research in AI. Then reflect that the research presented in the link is 50 years later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33046840,"author":"masswerk","month":"2022-10","story":"AI Hyperopia","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the public Lighthill debate","quote":"Video of the public debate","text":"Ah, the AI versus advanced automation debate and the Lighthill Report (UK, 1973). [1] Report: [LINK] [2] Video of the public debate: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32263567,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2022-07","story":"Translating a Visual Lego Manual to a Machine-Executable Plan","stance":"reference","why":"Shows 1970s Freddy robot assembling toys with vision","quote":"Donald Michie presenting the Edinburgh Research Purpose General Assembly Robot, a.k.a. \"Freddy","text":"To be honest, 89-92% sounds too optimistic for a real-world application. But I was talking about applications of the lego assembly model. My intuition is that lego is a special case: the pieces are made to interlock with each other and there are only a small number of ways in which two pieces can lock together. Compare this with building a prefab house, for example, or a car, where elements must be bound together with nails, mortar, soldering, or glue etc, in essentially unpredictable ways. I say that the approach described is pointless because I can't see any real-world applications of extracting assembly plans from a visualisation of an already-known assembly plan. Even less so when the object assembled is just a toy. And it's been possible to assemble toys using machine vision, given a pre-determined set of instructions, from the 1970's already: (Donald Michie presenting the Edinburgh Research Purpose General Assembly Robot, a.k.a. \"Freddy\"): [VIDEO] More info of Freddy here [LINK] Freddy was tediously hand-programmed with a full plan but it used vision and touch to find the pieces of the toy it assembles in the video."},{"id":28749355,"author":"bingohbangoh","month":"2021-10","story":"AI Is No Match for the Quirks of Human Intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for scientists saying game-playing proves little","quote":"","text":"> ... \"AI can't do X\" until we actually achieve X and then the goalpost is moved. This gets said often but I don't think anybody whose credible in the field actually makes statements like this. Take Chess for instance. In the IBM \"Big Blue\" documentary from ~2004, they quote journalists as saying \"AI can't play Chess as well as humans but if it could, then AI would be solved.\" Why did the techniques from Big Blue not seem to go anywhere? I know for a fact that scientists _were not saying this_. The Lighthill Debates on AI specifically talk about why playing games well doesn't really prove anything. [0] I do agree that OCR has improved greatly by AI but this feels very niche. Somebody mentioned defect anomaly detection in another comment and I was not aware of this. All useful for sure. Still, this doesn't amount to anywhere near the hype that was announced earlier last decade. Moreover, the economics in AI are mostly awful despite everybody's seemingly best efforts. [1] Even if its useful in some vague sense, it's not necessarily economically useful. Amazon has ~10,000 people working on Alexa. [2] Have they turned a profit on these endeavours? I understand they can absorb the costs but its not clear to me how the economics will work out here. ML models haven't even been useful in places where statistical methods have reined supreme such as Renaissance Technologies and other hedge funds. No large companies are using neural networks in a significant capacity to my knowledge. An"}],"r":[["9KABcmczPdg",1],["q0RX59G2jaA",1],["vtWp45Eewtw",1],["s_fkpZSnz2I",1],["p33CVV29OG8",1],["yReDbeY7ZMU",1],["dpw9EHDh2bM",1],["mPn2AdMH7UQ",1],["VscdPA6sUkc",1]]},"1cUUfMeOijg":{"wy":"The go-to short explanation whenever lava lamps, entropy sources, or Cloudflare randomness come up.","q":{"x":"There's a good Tom Scott video on this too.","i":16041579,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16041579,"author":"ceph_","month":"2017-12","story":"A wall of lava lamps helps encrypt the internet","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on Cloudflare's lava lamp entropy wall","quote":"There's a good Tom Scott video on this too.","text":"There's a good Tom Scott video on this too. Like most of his stuff, it's well worth the watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":39776697,"author":"Karellen","month":"2024-03","story":"The London office where swinging pendulums keep cyber threats at bay","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott on lava lamps for security, mentions London pendulums","quote":"(Mention of the London pendulums at 2:39)","text":"ObTomScott: The Lava Lamps That Help Keep The Internet Secure [VIDEO] (Mention of the London pendulums at 2:39)"},{"id":31416129,"author":"Natfan","month":"2022-05","story":"Riot Games: Artificial Latency for Remote Competitors","stance":"reference","why":"Cloudflare uses cameras on lava lamps for randomness","quote":"have some cameras pointed at lava lamps[0]","text":"Alternatively, just do what Cloudflare does (did?), and have some cameras pointed at lava lamps[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"}]},"1m0TQjBRcFo":{"wy":"HN points to BigClive as a source for how cheap UVC lamps actually work and what they emit.","q":{"x":"BigClive has some interesting videos on these kinds of lights","i":38161457,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38161457,"author":"dwroberts","month":"2023-11","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"BigClive has interesting videos on germicidal UV lights","quote":"BigClive has some interesting videos on these kinds of lights","text":"BigClive has some interesting videos on these kinds of lights [VIDEO] He also works in events/lighting and has mentioned the risk of events inadvertently sourcing 'cool-looking' lights without understanding what they actually are (germicidal UV lights)"},{"id":39038072,"author":"byb","month":"2024-01","story":"Can ultraviolet lightbulbs end the next pandemic before it starts?","stance":"reference","why":"BigClive has made several videos on UVC lamps","quote":"YouTuber Big Clive has had several videos on UVC lamps","text":"Personally I feel the use of the term \"far uv\" is very misleading because the 220 nm wavelength is within UVC, and not too far away from 254 nm germicidal UVC. It is always helpful to consult a chart: [LINK] So, from a public health/public education standpoint, I find it troubling that scientific research, which the article points out, is limited in this area. For decades, general advice has been that all UVC is dangerous to all life. We don't know below which wavelength does UVC no longer become harmful to animal (human) cells. Nor do we know if 220 nm wavelength light damage/weaken other materials. I'm not a chemist/engineer so I don't know if theoretical calculations can be made or if a massive scientific effort needs to be done to verify the impact of 220 nm UV light on lots of materials/life. If there is an animal/human cell safety region, perhaps a rebranding or subdivision of UVC into a \"UV-D\" is better than calling this \"far uv\". This has the added benefit of the \"D\" meaning \"Death to germs/viruses\". YouTuber Big Clive has had several videos on UVC lamps, I'd like to see someone get a 220 nm light into his hands to see if he smells like burnt meat. [VIDEO] [LINK] Edit: maybe I am a bit alarmist, some comments did share additional information about products offering this technology... I still hold that the branding/messaging is a problem by lumping it all together"},{"id":18255113,"author":"namibj","month":"2018-10","story":"Sunshine Helps Kill Germs Indoors","stance":"reference","why":"BigClive on E27 UVC lamps popular in China","quote":"See biglive about it","text":"E27 UVC lamps, basically in this case a long CFL with proper UV quartz glass, but without the fluorescent coating, seem to be popular in china to be screwed into the ceiling. See biglive about it: [VIDEO]"}]},"4G_1uy_7B5w":{"wy":"HN shares it as a fun explanation of analog floppy-disk cameras.","q":{"x":"A fun video to learn more about the tech","i":18073615,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18073615,"author":"einr","month":"2018-09","story":"Street View of 1980s New York","stance":"rec","why":"Fun video explaining analog floppy-disk still cameras","quote":"A fun video to learn more about the tech","text":"Yes, probably -- before digital cameras, there were still video cameras. They used a CCD to record still video images onto magnetic media such as a video floppy disk. So a lot like a digital camera, but fully analog. These were pretty cutting-edge tech in the mid 80's and would have made a lot of sense for a project like this. [LINK] [LINK] A fun video to learn more about the tech: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46259626,"author":"qingcharles","month":"2025-12","story":"A 'toaster with a lens': The story behind the first handheld digital camera","stance":"reference","why":"Related to the commenter's analog floppy Canon camera","quote":"","text":"It's interesting that you differentiate between analog and digital electronic cameras. My first \"digital\" camera was one of the Canon Ion ones that use floppy discs but actually record the photos in analog, so technically just an \"electronic camera\", but was marketed as a \"Still Video Camera\" (!). You had to use a video capture card to get the images into the PC. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24397340,"author":"gambiting","month":"2020-09","story":"A floppy-disk Walkman using a Raspberry Pi","stance":"reference","why":"Example of cameras storing analog NTSC photos on floppies","quote":"I'm talking cameras which just stored photos as analog signal directly on the floppy, in NTSC format!","text":"Similarly, there have been photo cameras that used floppies for image storage, and I'm not talking about sony's Cybershoot cameras which stored photos as actual files - I'm talking cameras which just stored photos as analog signal directly on the floppy, in NTSC format! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Jph0gxzL3UI",1]]},"BxV14h0kFs0":{"wy":"The go-to citation for why free, open APIs get shut down and for the fate of services like Yahoo Pipes.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott made a wonderful video a while back about open APIs, and about how they (mostly) died out[0].","i":30799945,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30799945,"author":"Sophira","month":"2022-03","story":"Should you make an open API?","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on how open APIs mostly died out","quote":"Tom Scott made a wonderful video a while back about open APIs, and about how they (mostly) died out[0].","text":"Tom Scott made a wonderful video a while back about open APIs, and about how they (mostly) died out[0]. It's currently called \"This Video Has 51,973,714 Views\", and I personally believe it's a great watch. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":30808921,"author":"iamahackerguy","month":"2022-03","story":"Should you make an open API?","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on how open APIs mostly died out","quote":"Tom Scott made a wonderful video a while back about open APIs, and about how they (mostly) died out[0].","text":"Tom Scott made a wonderful video a while back about open APIs, and about how they (mostly) died out[0]. It's currently called \"This Video Has 51,973,714 Views\", and I personally believe it's a great watch. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":48604857,"author":"allwashedup","month":"2026-06","story":"There Are No Instances in ATProto","stance":"reference","why":"Open APIs get closed as platforms lock in users","quote":"twitter, etc once had a much more open API. so did reddit.","text":"How is it any different than a centralized social media service then? Merely the fact that you can get a firehose from the website in a standard format? This was already the case with HTTP. The problem is that websites can leverage their power to make a locked-in ecosystem. see [VIDEO] twitter, etc once had a much more open API. so did reddit. This was just the first stage of their penetration strategy. The decentralization is totally irrelevant due to the way it's implemented. The problem is that Apps are sticky, so app developers have undue power to control their users. The \"content hosts\" \"moderators\" etc need to be completely cut out of the picture to be at the same level of censorship resistance as an RSS-shared blog. I can host a blog on my own computer, and you can subscribe to it without that many intermediaries."},{"id":42610894,"author":"bewal416","month":"2025-01","story":"Show HN: Mashups – Resurrecting Yahoo Pipes, my side project","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions Yahoo Pipes","quote":"I remember a Tom Scott video that mentioned it.","text":"Cool project! Yahoo Pipes was before my time, but I remember a Tom Scott video that mentioned it. Imagine having a service like that for free today… This video has … views by Tom Scott: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38653830,"author":"ksec","month":"2023-12","story":"Pipe Dreams: The life and times of Yahoo Pipes","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott video about programming and APIs","quote":"Everytime I see Yahoo Pipes I remember the video [1] from Tom Scott about programming.","text":"Everytime I see Yahoo Pipes I remember the video [1] from Tom Scott about programming. ( The video title view count is updated in as close to real time as possible ) [1] This Video Has 69,187,812 Views [VIDEO] A lot of what I thought were good, accessible, productive, entry to middle level technology ( HyperCards. Yahoo Pipes. Flash, Delphi etc ) never gained enough market. They were all the sort of middle ground good enough options. Instead technology is often filtered from the top end extreme. So everyone tries to use what Google, Facebook, Amazon, Microsoft are using. Because those tools are deemed as what Professional should use. The only rare exception that is powering most of the word is Microsoft Excel."},{"id":33719237,"author":"dpz","month":"2022-11","story":"Yahoo Pipes","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to Yahoo Pipes discussion","quote":"Any mention of Yahoo Pipes always makes me think of Tom Scott's \"This video has x views\".","text":"Any mention of Yahoo Pipes always makes me think of Tom Scott's \"This video has x views\". Feels quite relevant to other comments people have been making here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28191757,"author":"dwrodri","month":"2021-08","story":"Yahoo Pipes","stance":"reference","why":"Essay on why open APIs became unprofitable and abused","quote":"Tom Scott's essay here","text":"Check out IFTTT ( [LINK] ) or Huginn ( [LINK] ). They're similar but not quite. An underlying assumption with Yahoo Pipes was that interesting places on the Internet would offer APIs which have a developer UX that matches or exceeds the quality of their official UI. A mixture of unprofitability and abuse lead to this no longer being the case. Tom Scott's essay here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28245175,"author":"willis936","month":"2021-08","story":"A collection of free/public APIs you can use to build stuff","stance":"reference","why":"Discussion of free APIs getting turned off","quote":"Obligatory link to the Tom Scott video on free APIs.","text":"Obligatory link to the Tom Scott video on free APIs. There's a lot of common knowledge there, but his discussion on free APIs seems relevant. I've been burned by having hobbyist projects break from free APIs getting turned off. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24194400,"author":"badRNG","month":"2020-08","story":"Dependency","stance":"reference","why":"Relevant to software not lasting forever","quote":"Relevant Tom Scott vid","text":"Where I work, anything that is outside of the standard library and some certain security-approved dependencies requires a miserable approval process where an authorization package has to be submitted to security who will require upgrades anytime a CVE with a high enough assessed criticality is posted for the dependency (or other concerns regarding the source arise.) This approval is revisited on a regular period (or added to the standard list of approved dependencies.) The net effect is that there isn't dependency creep where any team can just continuously add dependencies to a project without their ongoing justification and visibility. For personal projects, I'm very liberal with using dependencies that can get something in my mind functioning quickly before my motivation dries up. I think I shared the OP's anxiety regarding at least the longevity of what I write until I came to peace with the fact that my software will not function forever, and likely not for the rest of the decade. Relevant Tom Scott vid: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23674449,"author":"cryptonector","month":"2020-06","story":"Roy Fielding's Misappropriated REST Dissertation","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why open APIs disappeared","quote":"Just watch Tom Scott explain why the APIs went away","text":"Where to start... First, nowadays altering the DOM is way more responsive than loading a new page, so no one really wants HATEOAS -- not even users, but especially not the sites we like to visit because they don't want to put out public APIs anymore. For non-browser app APIs (public or private), well, they can be the same, just driven by JS when in-browser. This... is sad. But this is a fact of life now. Just watch Tom Scott explain why the APIs went away, and when the APIs went away, so did HATEOAS ( [VIDEO] ). Second, there's a lot of APIs that needn't have anything more than one end-point -- no links to traverse, no HATEOAS. For example, a certification authority can take a CSR and authentication and return you a certificate -- one end-point is all you need, and other than that, the API can be RESTful in every sense. And then there's APIs where links aren't necessarily abundant or interesting. Third, HTTP kinda sucks anyways if you're dealing with databases. There's no nice RESTful way to construct transactions unless you put the RDBMS smarts into the client (JS again), which you can't do if it means trusting the client (it does). So right there, REST is broken anyways. Sure, if you're distributing your database, you need to deconstruct your transactions anyways, but you're not going to trust a user-agent to get that right, and anyways, you can't really do that with a HATEOAS interface. Put another way: show us how to build a secure application with REST (including HATEOAS"}]},"CeDOQpfaUc8":{"wy":"Pointed to in healthcare cost threads as an entertaining primer on how the US system inflates prices.","q":{"x":"Adam made an episode explaining medical costs and why it is so high.","i":15350342,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15350342,"author":"vinothgopi","month":"2017-09","story":"Ask HN: Xray cost – USA $200 (13000 INR), India 1000 INR – what causes 10x diff?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why medical costs are so high in the US.","quote":"Adam made an episode explaining medical costs and why it is so high.","text":"Adam made an episode explaining medical costs and why it is so high. It will at least give you a different perspective into the system. [VIDEO] So adding on to the labor/fixed cost increase, prices are just inflated since insurance pays for them anyway. In India, people don’t really consider insurance for such routine work due to the hassle of processing the claims too."},{"id":44282903,"author":"nandhinianand","month":"2025-06","story":"The Gentle Singularity","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as counterpoint on American healthcare system.","quote":"","text":"I won't attempt to make the counter-argument on account of being a stranger to America and it's healthcare system, but would point to the existence of this, just in case you haven't seen it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25656062,"author":"29athrowaway","month":"2021-01","story":"US doctor forgives $650k in medical bills for cancer patients","stance":"reference","why":"Episode covering health insurance providers and uninsured penalties.","quote":"My takeaway is that the system is calibrated to aggresively punish people without insurance.","text":"This episode of \"Adam ruins everything\" covers healthcare insurance providers: [VIDEO] My takeaway is that the system is calibrated to aggresively punish people without insurance."},{"id":18895191,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2019-01","story":"Hospitals release lists of retail prices for services","stance":"reference","why":"Comedic skit on hospital price lists and surprise bills.","quote":"Adam Ruins Everything has a skit on this for people who haven't heard of it","text":"Adam Ruins Everything has a skit on this for people who haven't heard of it: [VIDEO] On one of my non-insured years, I did get a five-digit bill like one in the video. Then some more from a bunch of other companies. It shouldn't have been legal. Especially given I couldn't exactly shop around for better prices with them all faking them."}]},"K8kua5B5K3I":{"wy":"HN links it as a rare look inside a large Chinese mining operation, with its deafening machine noise and workers' conditions.","q":{"x":"The noise of these machines is incredibly loud and annoying even in the edited video","i":14753012,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14753012,"author":"teknologist","month":"2017-07","story":"Photos: Life inside of China’s massive and remote Bitcoin mines","stance":"rec","why":"Eerie audio-visual look at loud mining machines and workers' living conditions.","quote":"The noise of these machines is incredibly loud and annoying even in the edited video","text":"Vice produced a video called \"Life Inside a Chinese Bitcoin Mine\" which complements these photos with a bit of an eerie audio/visual experience. The noise of these machines is incredibly loud and annoying even in the edited video and the people running these things essentially live in squalor. [LINK] Non-Flash: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13890452,"author":"Animats","month":"2017-03","story":"Bitcoin, blockchains, and national security","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a Bitcoin mine in northeast China.","quote":"Northeast China mine","text":"No, they're not outside of China. Locating a Bitcoin farm in China allows paying for power, equipment, and staff in yuan, while generating revenue in dollars or euros, legally. Northeast China mine: [1] Another China mine: [2] Mine in Kangding: [3] BW Pool and Antpool, in Inner Mongolia: [4] OxBTC farm (possibly in Ordos): [5] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":13609015,"author":"brilliantcode","month":"2017-02","story":"Two of China's Biggest Exchanges Stop Bitcoin Withdrawals","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Chinese bitcoin mining operations.","quote":"","text":"Won't guys like this [VIDEO] get affected? If all they can do is mine but not cash out, what happens to the network transaction speed and the overall difficulty?"}]},"MeMPCSqQ-34":{"wy":"HN commenters link it when people ask about cross-platform game development and for details like SDL's licensing change.","q":{"x":"Here's a talk he did about porting games to Linux with SDL 2.0 during a Steam Dev Days event","i":12260954,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":9362663,"author":"EliRivers","month":"2015-04","story":"Ask HN: What are you using for cross platform games?","stance":"rec","why":"Steam Dev Days talk about game development with SDL","quote":"Here's someone talking about it at a steam dev day","text":"SDL. It is solidly cross-platform (supports Haiku, which makes me smile, as well as Linux, Win, iOS, Android and so on and so on) and zLib licensed (pay nothing; use it, distribute it, static link, dynamic link, whatever you want - the zLib licence fits on a postcard). Here's someone talking about it at a steam dev day: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22156836,"author":"tony","month":"2020-01","story":"Pygame Zero: Creating games without boilerplate","stance":"reference","why":"Shows SDL moving away from LGPL","quote":"SDL moved away from LGPL here","text":"Incredible! The sphinx docs look great. Also, the logo! Not to mention, the translations: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] One reason of why I wouldn't use it yet: LGPL. This is the issue pygame had when I tried it. The license made it very difficult to incorporate and use in practice, and I ended up playing with kivy instead ( [LINK] ). The reasons why: 1. Writing anything commercial would be a gamble. I've wrote at length on why this license isn't meant for scripting languages, but even if it's a compiled language, SDL moved away from LGPL here: [VIDEO] . 2. LGPL and scripting languages just don't work from an open source perspective. It's not copy-paste and vendorize friendly. Functions can't be copied a la carte. I'd rather write something from scratch than link an LGPL library in one of my MIT licensed projects. I'm just not willing to open downstream users to the risk. What ends up happening is 5/10 years on, someone ends up writing a new MIT/BSD library to get around a viral one (e.g. GCC -> Clang/LLVM)."},{"id":18886905,"author":"git-pull","month":"2019-01","story":"Retro game engine for developers that enjoy creating games like it's 1997","stance":"reference","why":"Segment on SDL's permissive license","quote":"SDL 2 which prides itself on being on a permissive license.","text":"This is a cool project! - CMake, yay! These make generating build files for a variety of platforms (even Windows) a breeze. It also works with code completion stuff (CLion IDE, KDevelop both have CMake-based projects) - I like how it splits stuff up as well ( [LINK] ). - As for vendorizing SDL2, there is an opportunity for optimization: The shared libraries and header files can be scanned through CMake scripts. Example: [LINK] . - I see the .clang-format file, nice! (This is for clang-format: [LINK] ) Unfortunately, license is going to be a real barrier to most game developers. The ironic thing is this engine includes SDL 2 which prides itself on being on a permissive license. ( [VIDEO] , 6:40 to 7:30) If it's the intention to create an open source library to be helpful as possible to developers. If it's the intention to have a library just for use in the GPL ecosystem of games (which isn't bad: OpenTTD is GPL!) then this is okay. The reason why is a private game developer want to retain control of their intellectual property, even throughout the prototyping phase. As for open sourcing, I'd approach it in a different way: I'd release reusable components under a permissive license (e.g. MIT, BSD, ISC, zlib, etc.) and keep any creative work product code/source files private. Similar SDL2 framework for 2D games (I made some CMake contributions a while back): [LINK]"},{"id":12260954,"author":"ingenieros","month":"2016-08","story":"Playability Test of 100 Humble Bundle Games on Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Ryan Gordon on porting games to Linux with SDL 2.0","quote":"Here's a talk he did about porting games to Linux with SDL 2.0 during a Steam Dev Days event","text":"Most of those ports are being done by one lone developer named Ryan C. Gordon who runs icculus.org Here's a talk he did about porting games to Linux with SDL 2.0 during a Steam Dev Days event: [VIDEO] One interesting thing to note is that most of those Linux ports can only be purchased through a Humble Bundle. (Playdead's Limbo is a case in point.) It goes without saying that the grand majority of those ports would have never happened if it weren't for Humble Bundle in the first place."}]},"Q3oItpVa9fs":{"wy":"Mentioned as a striking example of sound producing physical effects and a favorite among his videos.","m":[{"id":34355785,"author":"bigiain","month":"2023-01","story":"Engineer has built and programmed a terrifying all-robot band","stance":"rec","why":"Another Nigel Stanford video the commenter likes.","quote":"another one of his I like is tghis","text":"I quite like this one: [LINK] AUTOMATICA - Robots Vs. Music - Nigel Stanford More industrial robot arm choreography and creative film making, but with some fun mindless destruction too. (And if you like that, another one of his I like is tghis: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":28618704,"author":"motohagiography","month":"2021-09","story":"The Analog Thing: An open source, educational, low-cost modern analog computer","stance":"reference","why":"Example of sound waves producing physical effects in ferrofluid.","quote":"When you look at what you can do with magnets hooked to a synth with a ferrofluid, e.g.","text":"Thanks! With the caveat that I profoundly lack depth on both, and am a bit artsy craftsy in my intuitions, I did buy the Maths module precisely because it was an analog computer. The intent was to create some generative music, and seeing if I could use it to create 1/f noise, and exploring self-similarity using a musical ear instead of in code. Still working on it. When you look at what you can do with magnets hooked to a synth with a ferrofluid, e.g. [VIDEO] , the sound and your ear helps to develop an sense for what kinds of waves can be used to yield physical effects. If you can functionally suspend and maipulate a ferrofluid with waves, there are likely analogies to laser pulses and electrons for quantum computing. Waves gonna wave, etc. The other piece that grabbed me recently was \"analog fractals,\" ( [LINK] + that rabbit hole) where if you can get those artifacts instantaneously as the effect of physical feedback instead of rendering them computationally, there are likely faster functional approaches to a lot of other problems, only a subset of which we use ASICs and FPGAs to implement today. It's speculative based on laughably incomplete understandings on my part, but that's hacking."},{"id":20387148,"author":"peter_d_sherman","month":"2019-07","story":"How to Draw Mushrooms on an Oscilloscope with Sound","stance":"reference","why":"Related to oscilloscope music and cymatics.","quote":"Intuitively, there seems to be a relationship between Oscilloscope Music and Cymatics","text":"Related: What is Oscilloscope Music and how do you display it on your oscilloscope? [LINK] Jerobeam Fenderson's Oscilloscope Music YouTube Channel [LINK] Intuitively, there seems to be a relationship between Oscilloscope Music and Cymatics: CYMATICS: Science Vs. Music - Nigel Stanford [VIDEO] (Also, there's an implied 2D to 3D mapping from the Oscilloscope experiments...)"}],"r":[["lix-vr_AF38",1],["5MxH1sfJLBQ",1]]},"R5Gppi-O3a8":{"wy":"HN uses it as the canonical illustration of decentralized production and global cooperation, often cited well outside economics threads.","q":{"x":"There's not a single person in the world who could make this pencil.","i":19428616,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":14783863,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2017-07","story":"Remacs – A community-driven port of Emacs to Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Friedman makes a good point about decentralized production despite disagreements.","quote":"I don't agree with many of his views, but I think he makes a good point in this video","text":"> \"Do you mean that Boeing is built by independent people who work on whatever they want to and then a plane is assembled of that independently built parts and no one takes the responsibility of the result?\" Somewhere in the middle. If you consider the work that goes into making a plane, you'll see quite a bit of it happens outside Boeing. To give one example, think of the aluminium that goes into the plane. Boeing doesn't have to mine bauxite, extract the aluminium from the bauxite, transport it to the place where its sold, etc... A classic example of this ground-up view was this example given by Milton Friedman about the pencil. I don't agree with many of his views, but I think he makes a good point in this video: [VIDEO] EDIT: Turns out the source of this example may have been Leonard Read: [LINK]"},{"id":19428616,"author":"doomjunky","month":"2019-03","story":"Nvidia's $99 Jetson Nano Is an AI Computer for DIY Enthusiasts","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman's pencil quote.","quote":"There's not a single person in the world who could make this pencil.","text":"Look at this lead pencil. There's not a single person in the world who could make this pencil. [0] [0]: Milton Friedman [VIDEO]"},{"id":18338701,"author":"Namari","month":"2018-10","story":"The backlash against overtourism","stance":"reference","why":"Shows everything is interlinked and consumption scales with population.","quote":"Watch this video: and you'll see that all is linked","text":"Maybe but if you reduce the population from whatever group, there is less needs for everything and because everything is related it will an worldwide impact. Watch this video: [VIDEO] and you'll see that all is linked and the more we are the more we will consume this planet."},{"id":15226548,"author":"0x27081990","month":"2017-09","story":"A New Way to Learn Economics","stance":"reference","why":"Pencil lesson cited for decentralized markets versus central planning.","quote":"The lesson of the pencil comes to mind","text":">You can't control for every variable in a market. It just isn't possible. Even economic \"laws\" like supply and demand have counter examples like Giffen goods. That's the whole point. Markets are decentralized brains, while Socialism advocates that a government as a central planner will be more efficient (it could be more efficient in specific cases like total war or natural disasters) and lead to Communism where we won't even need a government because society will behave as one being. The lesson of the pencil comes to mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14571292,"author":"grondilu","month":"2017-06","story":"Elon Musk Publishes Plans for Colonizing Mars","stance":"reference","why":"Building a pencil requires global collaboration.","quote":"even to build something as simple as a pencil, you need lots of people collaborating from all over the planet [1].","text":"> there is no self-sustaining city on earth Indeed. That's an other concept that I'm not sure Musk is fully aware of. Economies, industrial production systems are highly inter-dependent on Earth. In our \"technological civilization\", even to build something as simple as a pencil, you need lots of people collaborating from all over the planet [1]. So to me the idea of building a \"self-sustaining technological civilization\" on mars sounds quite ludicrous. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10632531,"author":"huxley","month":"2015-11","story":"Making a pencil from scratch (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"Friedman's pencil allegory from Free to Choose.","quote":"Milton Friedman's Pencil Allegory from the 1980s PBS series \"Free to Choose\"","text":"While Leonard Read's essay was reasonably well known among Chicago School economists, the concepts it described were made more famous by Milton Friedman's Pencil Allegory from the 1980s PBS series \"Free to Choose\": [VIDEO] You can read a critical article on the back story to Friedman and Read's writings on pencils here: [LINK]"},{"id":10480944,"author":"oberstein","month":"2015-10","story":"All my trash for a year fit into two plastic bags","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for true cost of a pencil being reflected in price.","quote":"I think the true cost of a single pencil is well reflected.","text":"I too would be interested in studies. But I do think environmental cost is factored in to the cost of most things, especially if due to environmental damage it is no longer possible to get a Thing at any price. I think the true cost of a single pencil is well reflected. [VIDEO] The problem with the profiler analogy is that economic costs can open up economic gains, whereas wasting billions of cycles in a dumb loop opens up nothing. If you analyzed the human activities that produce the most environmental cost, the individual contributions would be nothing. Only when you look at economies of scale driven by collections of people do you get to anything remotely resembling significant numbers. And you might think \"Let's ban all cars\", since that's a somewhat noticeable effect in aggregate, but by doing so you would kill off many other parts of the economy. Depending on the reaction, it could be good or bad for the environment. It could be good if humanity went extinct and the costs we directly produce went to 0 (leaving only the 'natural' costs), but that's not a world I want to live in."},{"id":3294291,"author":"bfrs","month":"2011-11","story":"House votes to end country limits for skilled workers seeking green cards","stance":"reference","why":"Pencil example of nobody knowing how to make a pencil.","quote":"A pencil pusher doesn't even know what it takes to make the pencil [1] he is pushing","text":"A GC grants economic freedom to indentured servants (H1B visa holders is the politically correct term). The longer freedom is denied the more profitable it is for the corporate masters. One of the tricks of this modern day slave trade is to lobby Congress to set a quota (about 30% of the demand) on the number of people who are set free each year. For the groups with the largest numbers of indentured servants, additional quotas are lobbied for. As long as the dollar was strong and life was shit in the third world hells these people came from, this system worked. The corporate masters recognize that the game is now up. The usual argument given for the lack of freedom for H1B visa holders is that the bureaucrats need to ensure that a H1B doesn't cause a job loss for a citizen. A pencil pusher doesn't even know what it takes to make the pencil [1] he is pushing, and yet somehow he can ensure that a citizen doesn't lose a job! The H1B visa is indentured servitude by the back door, plain and simple. Anyone who has given some thought to the idea of protecting jobs knows it is an exercise in futility (For a start, Congress must pass an Amish decree [2]: ban all technology invented since 1830, and declare Thomas Edison as the worst job destroyer [3] the world has ever seen. Also it will have to order the arrest and lock up of all entrepreneurs aka wannabe job killers, which is most HN readers). Therefore, I suggest to do away with all the current H1B bullshit, and adopt a point based "}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1]]},"VplMHWSZf5c":{"wy":"Pointed to in Busy Beaver threads as an accessible expert talk on the topic.","q":{"x":"I learned about it in this Scott Aaronson talk,","i":45277519,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45277519,"author":"ks2048","month":"2025-09","story":"Determination of the fifth Busy Beaver value","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Busy Beaver program's Collatz-like behavior around 31:40.","quote":"I learned about it in this Scott Aaronson talk,","text":"An interesting thing about this is that they figured out a high-level description of what this Busy Beaver program is doing - it's computing a Collatz-like sequence until it terminates. I'm not sure if that is described in this paper, but I learned about it in this Scott Aaronson talk, [VIDEO] e.g see the slide at 31:40."},{"id":44410584,"author":"vismit2000","month":"2025-06","story":"BusyBeaver(6) Is Quite Large","stance":"reference","why":"Aaronson talk related to Busy Beaver.","quote":"","text":"Scott Aaronson | How Much Math Is Knowable? [Harward CMSA]: [VIDEO] Recently on HN (couple of months ago): [LINK]"},{"id":43775239,"author":"nthingtohide","month":"2025-04","story":"AI Horseless Carriages","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The video slides could be converted into a dark mode for night viewing.","text":"Feature Request: Can we have dark mode for videos? An AI OS should be able to understand and satisfy such a usecases. E.g. Scott Aaronson | How Much Math Is Knowable? [VIDEO] The video slides could be converted into a dark mode for night viewing."}]},"XkDEzfpdcSg":{"wy":"Recommended as the condensed tl;dr of the C++ Core Guidelines and as a route into modern C++.","q":{"x":"I recommend the tl;dr version from Kate Gregory at CppCon 2017","i":16550005,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16536336,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2018-03","story":"Ask HN: Best way to learn modern C++?","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"","text":"- Tour of C++ ( [LINK] ) - Principles and Practice Using C++ ( [LINK] ) - From Mathematics to Generic Programming ( [LINK] ) - The Scott Meyers books Some of the Bjarne Stroustrup videos, \"Learning and Teaching Modern C++\" - [LINK] Some of the Herb Sutter videos, \"Writing Good C++14... By Default\" - [LINK] \"Back to the Basics! Essentials of Modern C++ Style\" - [LINK] Some of the Kate Gregory videos, \"Stop Teaching C\" - [LINK] \"10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now\" - [VIDEO] \"It's Complicated\" - [LINK]"},{"id":16550005,"author":"boltzmannbrain","month":"2018-03","story":"C++ Core Guidelines","stance":"rec","why":"Condensed tl;dr of the C++ Core Guidelines.","quote":"I recommend the tl;dr version from Kate Gregory at CppCon 2017","text":"I recommend the tl;dr version from Kate Gregory at CppCon 2017, “10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20405702,"author":"wyldfire","month":"2019-07","story":"Catching use-after-move C++ bugs with Clang's consumed annotations","stance":"reference","why":"Video on the Guidelines Support Library.","quote":"it seemed like a really interesting concept","text":"In fact, Sutter (et al) are working on lifetimes in CppCoreGuidelines [1]. I built their clang tree and tried it out without bothering to RTFM and tried out the warning on a pile of C++ code. I naively assumed that it might be a generally-useful warning (\"-Wlifetime\") that's not ready to be introduced upstream. That's not the case AFAICT. What I suppose I would've learned from RTFM is that the profile specified by the guidelines is sorta like an opt-in 'dialect' to annotate/specify lifetime information. Without it, there's lots of spurious findings. Either that, or the codebase I tried it on isn't as good as I thought it was. Here's a couple of interesting examples of failure modes -Wlifetime can detect on godbolt[2][3]. I watched a video [5] a while back on the Guidelines Support Library (GSL) [4] and it seemed like a really interesting concept. I think it's a valuable idea and I'd love to see popular C++ projects leveraging it. I'm a card-carrying RESF member† (but have a day job w/mostly C++). Don't RIIR for one thing, RIIR to get all the things. Cargo is the sleeper hit of Rust. Hygienic macros and more! [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] CppCon 2017: Kate Gregory “10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now” [VIDEO] † Rust Evangelism Strike Force -- maybe we really should have cards"},{"id":15780995,"author":"pjmlp","month":"2017-11","story":"Carp, a compiled Lisp with type inference and a borrow checker","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, GSL is a kind of stop-gap solution, until standard catches on. For example std::string_view and ongoing design on std::array_view are based on gsl::span . The gsl::byte is also no longer needed on C++17 thanks to std::byte as yet another example. The GSL asserts are there until code contracts[0] get into the standard. The magical types like gsl::owner allow clang-tidy and VC++ checkers to apply a Rust-like memory tracking usage. Kate Gregory did a presentation at CppCon 2017. \"10 Core Guidelines You Need to Start Using Now\" [VIDEO] As for not taking off, being initially a Microsoft proposal, it is surely used by Office and Windows teams, specially given they already use SDL (Security Definition Language) macros as well. [0] [LINK]"}],"r":[["NH1Tta7purM",1],["xnqTKD8uD64",1],["YnWhqhNdYyk",1],["DRq2NgeFcO0",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["JfmTagWcqoE",1],["D7Sd8A6_fYU",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["YX3iRjKj7C0",1],["hEx5DNLWGgA",1],["NqKyHEJe9_w",1],["gV5obrYaogU",1],["uknEhXyZgsg",1],["AnrlSqtpOkw",1],["lgivCGdmFrw",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"ZvmMzI0X7fE":{"wy":"Recommended as a talk from SQLite's main author, and cited for the project's focus on backwards compatibility and for deployment anecdotes.","q":{"x":"You might also enjoy this talk by the main author of SQLite","i":15034849,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15034849,"author":"felixge","month":"2017-08","story":"How Postgres Makes Transactions Atomic","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by SQLite's main author comparing it to PostgreSQL.","quote":"You might also enjoy this talk by the main author of SQLite","text":"AFAIK SQLite doesn't achieve atomicity by virtue of its global write lock. It does so by using a rollback journal (default) or WAL. So it's not really all that different from PostgreSQL in that regard and the authors point remains valid :). You might also enjoy this talk by the main author of SQLite: [VIDEO] (SQLite: Protégé of PostgreSQL)"},{"id":13569145,"author":"justinclift","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: Is sqlite good for long-term persistence?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of SQLite community's focus on backwards compatibility.","quote":"the SQLite development Community are very very big on backwards compatibility.","text":"Not sure about a 30 year timeframe. 10 years seems like it wouldn't be any kind of problem (personal opinion), but 30 years is an awful long time. That being said, the SQLite development Community are very very big on backwards compatibility. eg: [VIDEO] Hmmmm, there's a better talk by D. Richard Hipp around, but I'm not finding it atm. In the better talk he explains they (SQLite) are planning to be around for the 30 year timeframe you mention. Something to do with an airplane manufacturer looking for long term guarantees, but I don't remember the details."},{"id":8034331,"author":"keeperofdakeys","month":"2014-07","story":"SQLite: Small, Fast, Reliable – Choose any three","stance":"reference","why":"Talk mentions SQLite use in Adobe Lightroom for thumbnails.","quote":"it was this talk that mentions sqlite being used in adobe lightroom","text":"As a read-only database, sqlite doesn't have too many limits, since the storage mechanism is flexible enough to allow fast queries. The problem comes when you start writing, and reads have to wait for them. Also, (I believe) it was this talk [VIDEO] that mentions sqlite being used in adobe lightroom, and it being faster to access thumbnails from an sqlite database."}]},"dE-vOscpiNc":{"wy":"Linked as a visualization of how the famous Earthrise photo was taken.","q":{"x":"Wikipedia links to a nice visualization on youtube of the moments when the photo was taken","i":40615792,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40615792,"author":"akavel","month":"2024-06","story":"Apollo 8 astronaut William Anders ID'd in WA plane crash","stance":"rec","why":"Visualization synced with astronauts' actual recorded conversation","quote":"Wikipedia links to a nice visualization on youtube of the moments when the photo was taken","text":"Wikipedia links to a nice visualization on youtube of the moments when the photo was taken, synced with the recording of the actual conversation of the Astronauts as recorded by the Apollo 8 equipment: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26924274,"author":"matsemann","month":"2021-04","story":"Earth Restored – 50 restored images of earth released","stance":"reference","why":"The reenactment video of how the Earthrise photo was taken","quote":"How the earth appeared over the horizon and they scrambled to find a camera or so.","text":"I once saw a video (reenactment but original voices maybe?) of how one of these images was taken. How the earth appeared over the horizon and they scrambled to find a camera or so. Anyone knows which video I'm talking about and could help me find it? Edit: finding out the picture in question had a name, Earthrise, made it easier to find. Here's the video [VIDEO]"},{"id":16068938,"author":"ElCapitanMarkla","month":"2018-01","story":"Who Took the Legendary Earthrise Photo from Apollo 8?","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the Earthrise moment discussed in the article","quote":"Video of the Earthrise he's talking about in the article -","text":"Video of the Earthrise he's talking about in the article - [VIDEO] I just finished Andrew's A Man on the Moon book a couple of weeks ago and I throughly enjoyed it."}],"r":[["5BIP_N9qQm4",1]]},"f1DtY42xEOI":{"wy":"Shared as a charming historical look at children learning computing, including kids acting as register bits.","q":{"x":"Kids acting as bits in a machine register and performing binary arithmetic by raising their hands if set, or not raising when cleared.","i":1480173,"y":"2010"},"m":[{"id":1480173,"author":"mahmud","month":"2010-07","story":"Tomorrow's World - Home Computer Terminal (1967)","stance":"rec","why":"Kids act as register bits to do binary arithmetic.","quote":"Kids acting as bits in a machine register and performing binary arithmetic by raising their hands if set, or not raising when cleared.","text":"Wow, I have never seen this: human digital logic. [VIDEO] Kids acting as bits in a machine register and performing binary arithmetic by raising their hands if set, or not raising when cleared. Really cute."},{"id":47363622,"author":"zeristor","month":"2026-03","story":"\"This Is Not the Computer for You\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Is this close enough? it’s from 1969, I wonder what became of them: “Tomorrow's World: Nellie the School Computer 15 February 1969 - BBC” [VIDEO]"},{"id":14483029,"author":"DanBC","month":"2017-06","story":"Jean Sammet, co-creator of COBOL, has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"One of the children wrote a language - minigol - so the younger children didn't have to use assembler.","text":"Or was it a product of the computers at the time? Here's a 1959 computer, donated to a school in 1965. This was filmed in 1969. One of the children wrote a language - minigol - so the younger children didn't have to use assembler. [VIDEO] Just turning the machine on requires coordination between several people using intercom."}]},"ggdYts4muu0":{"wy":"Cited as a technical look at how Tesla's VPPs and Autobidder work as distributed systems.","q":{"x":"was an informative watch that covers the power problem space and their distributed software solution","i":40222255,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40222255,"author":"kulor","month":"2024-05","story":"Virtual Power Plants and Software","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the power problem space and Tesla's distributed software solution","quote":"was an informative watch that covers the power problem space and their distributed software solution","text":"The linked Tesla QCon presentation [VIDEO] was an informative watch that covers the power problem space and their distributed software solution, something Tesla is uniquely positioned to pull off with their end-to-end platform."},{"id":32953305,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2022-09","story":"Charging cars at home at night is not the way to go: study","stance":"reference","why":"Talk by Tesla software devs on virtual power plant","quote":"there's a talk by a couple of the software devs online somewhere. Found it","text":"Tesla calls this \"Virtual Power Plant\" and there's a talk by a couple of the software devs online somewhere. Found it: [VIDEO] Some other people have mentioned that you just need to vary the price, but the price is set by bids from providers. Code that can learn demand patterns and integrate weather forecasts will let Tesla bid lower and so earn more money in the market while also lowering energy costs for everyone."},{"id":32503400,"author":"cptcobalt","month":"2022-08","story":"Tesla Virtual Power Plant","stance":"reference","why":"Technical details of Tesla VPPs as distributed systems","quote":"Some technical details of how Tesla VPPs work, as organized distributed systems","text":"I'm not OP, but the timing context of this post is important: Today, California has issued a FlexAlert, and Powerwalls in PG&E and SCE will start discharging at 6pm to help reduce load on the grid. It's not a daily action, but it's emergency support when the grid needs it the most. And the time window when it happens is important: Powerwalls are contributing to to reduce load when the grid needs it the most . It's not meant to support the entire grid, just directly attack the problem at the time where every Watt and Watt-hour counts. You don't need to own Powerwall to benefit from this, just like how you don't own the peaker plants that typically spark up at these times. You benefit because there's a big chance that there won't be rolling blackouts in California this afternoon—distributed assets like this can be dispatched. Dispatch Info: [LINK] A VPP tracker in the Tesla community: [LINK] Some technical details of how Tesla VPPs work, as organized distributed systems: [VIDEO] CAISO Grid Outlook: [LINK]"},{"id":23736523,"author":"manicdee","month":"2020-07","story":"Tesla’s SA big battery just got 50% bigger after goals thanks to Autobidder","stance":"reference","why":"Technical internals of the Autobidder market bidding system","quote":"Technical innards of the Autobidder system in case you care to know more","text":"Technical innards of the Autobidder system in case you care to know more [VIDEO] It’s basically a market forecasting system which allows the operator to place bids on various energy markets such as supply, contingency and ancillary services. The battery has limited resources (power output and energy storage) so it needs to plan well ahead for making the most money for each watt-hour it buys or sells."}]},"ljPFZrRD3J8":{"wy":"Shared as a delightful example of Irish humor and as a joke reference.","q":{"x":"Here's a delightful example","i":33947583,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33947583,"author":"nkurz","month":"2022-12","story":"Be Wary of Imitating High-Status People Who Can Afford to Countersignal","stance":"rec","why":"Irish comic song about a horse outside.","quote":"Here's a delightful example","text":"Equally, it's possible to go even further in the \"bicycle\" direction to end up ahead of those with cars. Here's a delightful example: Rubberbandits \"Horse Outside\" [VIDEO] (NSFW at one point if you are listening closely and can understand the Irish accent, but unlikely to actually offend). The beginning might be boring, but it's short, so stick with it."},{"id":46525235,"author":"aebtebeten","month":"2026-01","story":"Trump aide says no-one would fight US over Greenland","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thanks to HN, a little time ago I learned that my original conception of the Monroe Doctrine (donroe mocktrine?) was overly simplistic. Yay HN! [LINK] (who'd want to invade IE anyway? They have no culture beyond a pair of shite gaelic rappers and they're so backward they still travel on horseback: [VIDEO] ) EDIT: and thank you HN for introducing me to Comrade Chuān Jiàn Guó. It is to laugh, slimy Terrie: [LINK]"},{"id":43047647,"author":"redacted","month":"2025-02","story":"Zed now predicts your next edit with Zeta, our new open model","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"one of Ireland's greatest cultural contributions","text":"Can't believe you're getting downvoted for one of Ireland's greatest cultural contributions. Behold The Rubberbandits, Horse Outside (helpfully timestamped to the lyric in question) [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["fbhPIK-oBvA",1],["FBaVwwuErmU",1],["TiXINuf5nbI",1],["HLNhPMQnWu4",1]]},"nuruzFqMgIw":{"wy":"HN uses it as the standard reference for why USB devices are dangerous beyond ordinary viruses.","q":{"x":"For anyone interested in the subject I recommend watching the presentation","i":8405544,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8405544,"author":"fla","month":"2014-10","story":"Phison USB Custom Firmware and Existing Firmware Patches","stance":"rec","why":"Black Hat 2014 talk on reprogramming USB device firmware to turn evil","quote":"For anyone interested in the subject I recommend watching the presentation","text":"For anyone interested in the subject I recommend watching the presentation [1] by by Karsten Nohl and Jakob Lell at Black Hat USA 2014. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19473085,"author":"Crosseye_Jack","month":"2019-03","story":"Ransomware Forces Two Chemical Companies to Order ‘Hundreds of New Computers’","stance":"reference","why":"Example of bad things done with USB memory sticks","quote":"we have already seen people do \"bad things\" with USB Memory sticks [1]","text":"The NSA have been using Hard Drive Firmware exploits for years. Such an attack could hide malware that also survives a format[0] (Which is why I brought up a drive replacement in my prev post). I wouldn't be surprised if the same can't be done with SSD Firmware too (we have already seen people do \"bad things\" with USB Memory sticks [1]) Also if a full BIOS flash has been performed you might be SOL as after a power cycle the modified BIOS is now the first thing loaded by your system (Or it might be the VBIOS, its been a while.) which could prevent future flashing of the BIOS or fake the flashing process but not actually flash anything. If you have a board that can recovery flash you might be able to recover but how do you trust the system afterwards? As the BIOS is usually stored on a SPI Flash you could use an external programmer to dump the content of the flash and do a diff on the firmware file. You have to think about who is your attacker. Are the Kiddies going to go to such lengths to stay persistent on a consumers laptop they use as a facebook machine? Prob not. But is it outside the scope of a determined attacker (or nation state) who managed to get a first stage attack malware inside a large company? IMO it would depend on how valuable they determine access to your network / data is. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] EDIT: I've not spoke about VBIOS infections as the GPU Vendors on at least modern cards have been really locking down their GPUS and as far as I've seen, I've yet to"},{"id":10377452,"author":"halosghost","month":"2015-10","story":"USB/IP Project","stance":"reference","why":"BadUSB as a reason to worry about USB over IP","quote":"With things like BadUSB[1] still out there, I would be worried about anything that gives USB devices more wide reach or higher privileges.","text":"On the one hand, this looks incredibly cool. On the other hand, I actually find it a bit worrying. With things like BadUSB[1] still out there, I would be worried about anything that gives USB devices more wide reach or higher privileges. That this project aims to offer \"full functionality\" of the device, afaik, means it is likely doing exactly the kind of thing that I find worrying. Can anyone talk about this a little more in-depth? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9171847,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2015-03","story":"Dead Drops: what to do if you see a USB stick sticking out of a wall","stance":"reference","why":"Shows USB attacks can be worse than plain viruses","quote":"And it can get much, much worse than plain old viruses too","text":"Since sharing USB flash drives is pretty much the equivalent of having digital unprotected sex anyway, I'm sure you'll get that soon enough. And it can get much, much worse than plain old viruses too: [VIDEO]"}]},"o_TH-Y78tt4":{"wy":"Listed among favorite tech talks and cited in debates over whether the database should drive application architecture.","q":{"x":"Architecture is more about deciding what direction your dependencies go and where your hard boundaries go","i":27883316,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":12641781,"author":"0xmohit","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite tech talks.","quote":"The Principles of Clean Architecture [1]","text":"How To Design A Good API and Why it Matters [0] The Principles of Clean Architecture [1] The State of the Art in Microservices by Adrian Cockcroft [2] \"The Mess We're In\" by Joe Armstrong [3] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":27883316,"author":"Rd6n6","month":"2021-07","story":"I had to give a wrong answer to get the job (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains dependency direction and boundaries in architecture, around 27 minutes in.","quote":"Architecture is more about deciding what direction your dependencies go and where your hard boundaries go","text":"Mvc isn’t an architecture at all. It’s just meant to be a tiny pattern for ui widgets from the 70s that people started to use as an architecture. The actual goal is to just keep a tiny piece of business logic from having a dependency on a specific input or on the view. It applied to a single button or a single input field back then. Architecture is more about deciding what direction your dependencies go and where your hard boundaries go [VIDEO] (approx 27 mins in)"},{"id":21813222,"author":"MaxBarraclough","month":"2019-12","story":"Message DB: Event Store and Message Store for PostgreSQL","stance":"reference","why":"Uncle Bob argues against database-oriented architectures.","quote":"what do you make of Uncle Bob railing against database-oriented architectures?","text":"For some perhaps-off-topic contrarianism, what do you make of Uncle Bob railing against database-oriented architectures? [0] (He takes a good few minutes to get his point across.) [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20562977,"author":"lugg","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the “best” codebases that you've encountered?","stance":"reference","why":"Uncle Bob on frameworks for frameworks' sake, from about 10:30.","quote":"a framework that forces its architecture onto your domain model is fundamentally broken by design","text":"I'm not fully on board with all of Roberts views but this point about a framework for frameworks sake is one I agree with. [VIDEO] starting at around 10:30 he gets into what I was getting at. > Whereas I'd say Laravel and it's ecosystem are far more productive and time saving than simply using just PHP. I'm not arguing a time thing. I'm arguing that a framework that forces its architecture onto your domain model is fundamentally broken by design. My domain should drive architecture choices and the framework should let me augment that with well worn libraries and modular patterns. Laravels architecture works for one thing: web sites. And for that, you're better off using WordPress. Laravel is better than older frameworks, like say, code igniter but it still fights you every step of the way if you want to step outside the box."},{"id":19592042,"author":"btschaegg","month":"2019-04","story":"SQL databases come up with algorithms you’d never have dreamed of (2017) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Uncle Bob's argument that the database should not be the center of an application.","quote":"The DB usually (rembember that there is an exception to every rule!) has no business of being the","text":"I don't think there's a one-size-fits-all solution to this, since different domains require different guarantees from your storage medium. For example, if you need to combine multiple actions with a certain level of transaction safety, defining an interface for that can get tricky. Overall, I tend to agree with Uncle Bob's \"Clean Architecture\" [1][2] approach when it comes to DBs, though: The DB usually (rembember that there is an exception to every rule!) has no business of being the \"center\" of an application. It should provide you with functionality, not dictate how your business logic works. If you can pull out your DB and give it a sensible interface, you still might end up having a lot of inline SQL queries, but they are not littered all over your codebase; they live inside a narrow scope (a plugin, say). Also, the SQL schema and queries in this case often are quite boring -- the tricky bit is coming up with a good interface that provides all the guarantees you need to uphold (complicated transactions maybe?). And remember that you need these guarantees because of how your business domain works, not because of your DB. Perhaps you can get rid of some of them if you organize your storage (i.e. DB) in a specific way? [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] Edit: Also note that there's no reason not to use a ORM in this way, too (if it suits you). The important part is the same: The ORM has no business dictating how your business logic should be implemented, and if you try to remove it f"},{"id":17567419,"author":"jppope","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the things keep in mind while giving/preparing for a tech talk?","stance":"reference","why":"Clean Architecture talk listed among fantastic technical speeches.","quote":"Here's a collection of some fantastic technical speeches","text":"1) Do not attempt to live code. change your code via version control, build small videos, copy and paste... whatever you do... do not try and live code 2) Read \"The presentation secrets of Steve Jobs\" ... its not really about Steve Jobs so much as its about how to be a great presenter. 3) building or writing a talk is significantly less important than the delivery... which means that more of your time should be spent on how to deliver the speech (recording I find is the best way to practice) 4) Pick a single thing and do it well, do not attempt to put too much into a talk or tackle something massively complex. 5) Make sure that everyone is walking away with something they can actually use. This doesn't require it be a hard skill... \"Soft\" or philosophical utilities are just as good 6) Have Fun and be funny... Speakers have a tendency to want to show off the size of their intellect, and the level their competency- having a speech be entertaining is way way more important. 7) DO NOT READ FROM YOUR SLIDES. know your shit. slides are supplementary Extra Credit. Here's a collection of some fantastic technical speeches: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":13520591,"author":"techdominator","month":"2017-01","story":"The Domain-Driven Design Paradox (2016)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Uncle Bob screaming \"DATABASE IS DETAIL!","text":"I don't think DDD is a silver bullet; building quality software will require DDD but it will also require more. But don't get me wrong, I always been a proponent of keeping Domain concerns in The Domain layer. It makes much more things easier in the long run, beginners should be exposed to DDD very soon in the learning process, in my opinion. DDD and the principle of separation of concerns always reminds of Uncle Bob screaming \"DATABASE IS DETAIL!\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["aAb7hSCtvGw",1]]},"tzr7hRXcwkw":{"wy":"HN debugger-versus-print-debugging threads point to it as Carmack's authoritative case for using a real debugger.","q":{"x":"This was one of the more interesting parts of John Carmack's interview on Fridman's podcast","i":39195948,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39195948,"author":"pengaru","month":"2024-01","story":"When I got started I debugged using printf() today I debug with print()","stance":"rec","why":"One of the more interesting parts of the interview.","quote":"This was one of the more interesting parts of John Carmack's interview on Fridman's podcast","text":"This was one of the more interesting parts of John Carmack's interview on Fridman's podcast: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45193453,"author":"troupo","month":"2025-09","story":"Things you can do with a debugger but not with print debugging","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack baffled by people allergic to debuggers.","quote":"He's baffled why people are so allergic to debuggers","text":"Don't show the discussion to John Carmack. He's baffled why people are so allergic to debuggers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42499910,"author":"troupo","month":"2024-12","story":"Demystifying Debuggers, Part 2: The Anatomy of a Running Program","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on hostility to debuggers and IDEs.","quote":"It still boggles my mind how hostile to debuggers and IDEs Silicon Valley VC- backed companies are","text":"John Carmack: It still boggles my mind how hostile to debuggers and IDEs Silicon Valley VC- backed companies are [VIDEO]"},{"id":42221090,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2024-11","story":"Don’t look down on print debugging","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack laments avoidance of graphical debuggers.","quote":"There's a section of an interview with John Carmack","text":"Print debugging is a disaster. If there's one widespread practice among non-beginner developers that wastes hours of time it's print debugging. I honestly can't tell how often I have seen people, even experienced programmers (usually because they insist on running some bespoke vim setup) refuse to use a graphical debugger to actually step through a program, and instead they spend hours hunting down bugs they could have found in ten minutes. There's a section of an interview with John Carmack ( [VIDEO] ) where he laments the same thing. It's what the Windows/game development corner of the programming world actually got right, people generally use effective tools for software development."},{"id":38300193,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2023-11","story":"Ask HN: Is (n)vim worth the trouble?","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on graphical debugging.","quote":"Carmack talked about it in an interview","text":"vim bindings are worth learning, vim/nvim itself is not. Two reasons. One, graphical debugging. Almost every hardcore vim/nvim user I've met has terminal (no pun intended) printf-debugging disease, or uses an IDE on a second monitor. Carmack talked about it in an interview ( [VIDEO] ) Secondly people, especially with neovim, seem to be hellbent on turning the thing into a GUI editor anyway, with bespoke search interfaces, file tree plugins, graphical plugin installers, and so on. At which point I don't understand why you're not using something made for that purpose rather than painfully try to reinvent it in a piece of software designed to output text"},{"id":37015481,"author":"simscitizen","month":"2023-08","story":"I do not use a debugger (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's differing opinion on debuggers.","quote":"John Carmack has a pretty different opinion about debuggers","text":"While we're appealing to authority, we should probably note that John Carmack has a pretty different opinion about debuggers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35096251,"author":"heinrichhartman","month":"2023-03","story":"What a good debugger can do","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack frequently uses a debugger to explore program state.","quote":"drops into a debugger all the time to explore state, understand program behavior, debug things, etc.","text":"Contrasting perspective from John Carmack [1] who drops into a debugger all the time to explore state, understand program behavior, debug things, etc. Looks like there is no universal golden path. Differing environments make different approaches effective. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33310127,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2022-10","story":"Tell HN: I was tired of being a perfectionist so I built an app within 24 hours","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack's suggestion that a debugger is the best way to program.","quote":"John Carmack's[1] suggestion that using a debugger is the best way to program?","text":"Thanks for your take. What do you think of John Carmack's[1] suggestion that using a debugger is the best way to program? I am by no means comparing myself to Carmack :-) I find debugger a good way to \"see\" what the program is doing. But that leads to a lot of time wasted. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JFaOYYSxSEA",1],["c2lgEyNaop4",1],["X29lF43mUlo",1],["Ja5Q75hf6QI",1],["7xTGNNLPyMI",1],["jK0BkTPUGQY",1],["VFFlO7yBIBM",1],["RKT-sKtR970",1]]},"yS_c2qqA-6Y":{"wy":"Mentioned as a recommended Curtis film and referenced in discussions of AI, Eliza and manufactured realities.","q":{"x":"a film I recommend.","i":21644296,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":21644296,"author":"TurkishPoptart","month":"2019-11","story":"The Real Class War","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Curtis film relevant to elite-manufactured progressivism discussion.","quote":"a film I recommend.","text":"You can see this beginning in roughly 2011 when the Occupy Wallstreet protests start to fracture. This was when Starbucks began advocating for anti-racist policy (why? they make money selling permutations of coffee and milk) and a bunch of other corporations chimed in. The 99% movement quickly splintered off into anti-racist memes and whirled into Third Wave Feminism + needs for \"inclusion\" and \"intersectionality\" and whatever terms the Wallstreet-backed Left felt best to appease the working poor and working lower-middle class of academics and journalists (who are basically working poor now, but would not like to admit it). So when you say: >Today's western progressivism is taken hostage by the elite. I agree. In fact, there is likely evidence that it was manufactured by the elite. This discussion is heading to the territory of Hypernormalisation[ [VIDEO] ], a film I recommend."},{"id":47933124,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2026-04","story":"OpenAI: Our Principles","stance":"reference","why":"Describes the film's argument about a simpler fake world.","quote":"HyperNormalisation (2016) is a documentary film by Adam Curtis","text":"HyperNormalisation (2016) is a documentary film by Adam Curtis; the word/concept is from a book, so maybe that's what you're referring to? I have only seen the film myself. As to your question about Soviet collapse, I don't think I could coalesce the views of the 166 minute documentary to this comment field while doing it justice. I'm not sure that there is a direct casual relationship or arrow of causality between the collapse and use/misuse of language, as much as there is a feedback loop between the two. [LINK] > HyperNormalisation is a 2016 BBC documentary by British filmmaker Adam Curtis. It argues that following the global economic crises of the 1970s, governments, financiers and technological utopians gave up on trying to shape the complex \"real world\" and instead established a simpler \"fake world\" for the benefit of multi-national corporations that is kept stable by neoliberal governments. > The word hypernormalisation was coined by Alexei Yurchak, a professor of anthropology who was born in Leningrad and later went to teach at the University of California, Berkeley. He introduced the word in his book Everything Was Forever, Until It Was No More: The Last Soviet Generation (2006), which describes paradoxes of Soviet life during the 1970s and 1980s. He says everyone in the Soviet Union knew the system was failing, but no one could imagine any alternative to the status quo, and politicians and citizens alike were resigned to maintaining the pretense of a functioning soc"},{"id":34628179,"author":"labrador","month":"2023-02","story":"GPT3 Is Just Spicy Autocomplete","stance":"reference","why":"Curtis's Eliza segment interpreted differently by the commenter.","quote":"I was watching \"HyperNormalisation\" by Adam Curtis for the second time.","text":"I was watching \"HyperNormalisation\" by Adam Curtis for the second time. In his segment on Eliza, an early example of a chat bot, I realized that Curtis makes a mistake in his interpretation of Eliza. For Curtis it's narcissism that makes Eliza attractive. Curtis levels the charge that Westerners are individualistic and self-centered often. But when an interview with the creator Joseph Weizenbaum is shown starting at 01hr:22min, he never says that. He relates how his secretary took to it, and even though she knew it was a primitive computer program, she wanted some privacy while she used it. Weizenbaum was puzzled by that, but then the secretary (or possibly another woman) says Eliza doesn't judge me and it doesn't try to have sex with me. What jumped out at me was that Weizenbaum's secretary was using Eliza as a thinking tool to clarify her thoughts. Most high school graduates in America don't learn critical thinking skills as far as I can tell. Eliza is a useful tool because it encourages critical thinking and second order thinking by asking questions and reflecting back answers and asking questions in another way. The secretary didn't want to use Eliza because she was a narcissist, she wanted to talk through some sensitive issues with what she knew was a dumb program so she could try and resolve them. That's how I feel about ChatGPT so far. It's a great thinking tool. Someone to bounce ideas around with. Of course, I know it's a dumb computer program and it makes mistakes, "},{"id":34629273,"author":"labrador","month":"2023-02","story":"Chat GPT is the birth of the real Web 3.0, and it's not going to be fun","stance":"reference","why":"Curtis's Eliza segment interpreted differently by the commenter.","quote":"I was watching \"HyperNormalisation\" by Adam Curtis for the second time.","text":"I made this comment on a post that got flagged for reasons I don't understand. I don't want to feel like I wasted my time so I'm re-commenting here: I was watching \"HyperNormalisation\" by Adam Curtis for the second time. In his segment on Eliza, an early example of a chat bot, I realized that Curtis makes a mistake in his interpretation of Eliza. For Curtis it's narcissism that makes Eliza attractive. Curtis levels the charge that Westerners are individualistic and self-centered often. But when an interview with the creator Joseph Weizenbaum is shown starting at 01hr:22min, he never says that. He relates how his secretary took to it, and even though she knew it was a primitive computer program, she wanted some privacy while she used it. Weizenbaum was puzzled by that, but then the secretary (or possibly another woman) says Eliza doesn't judge me and it doesn't try to have sex with me. What jumped out at me was that Weizenbaum's secretary was using Eliza as a thinking tool to clarify her thoughts. Most high school graduates in America don't learn critical thinking skills as far as I can tell. Eliza is a useful tool because it encourages critical thinking and second order thinking by asking questions and reflecting back answers and asking questions in another way. The secretary didn't want to use Eliza because she was a narcissist, she wanted to talk through some sensitive issues with what she knew was a dumb program so she could try and resolve them. That's how I feel about Ch"}]},"6eZ2yvWl9nQ":{"wy":"Cited as a historical reference that Halo was first announced at Macworld, with Steve Jobs involved, and nearly launched as a Mac game.","q":{"x":"You wouldn't think Steve Jobs would personally introduce a game that was multi-platform","i":30154068,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":45721281,"author":"alexjplant","month":"2025-10","story":"Microsoft needs to open up more about its OpenAI dealings","stance":"reference","why":"Halo nearly launched on the iMac.","quote":"We almost had Halo on the iMac before Microsoft acquired Bungie [1].","text":"> firing game devs by the thousands as they hand halo over to PlayStation They kind of took it from Apple to begin with. We almost had Halo on the iMac before Microsoft acquired Bungie [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38619663,"author":"jacobsimon","month":"2023-12","story":"E3 Is Officially Dead","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs kicks off the 1999 Halo demo.","quote":"Here’s Steve Jobs kicking off the demo in 1999","text":"Believe it or not - it was actually meant to be an exclusive for Mac before Microsoft acquired it. Here’s Steve Jobs kicking off the demo in 1999: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30153880,"author":"015a","month":"2022-01","story":"Sony to buy video game maker Bungie in $3.6B deal","stance":"reference","why":"1999 Macworld trailer showing early third-person Halo.","quote":"the 1999 MacWorld trailer suggests","text":"First of all; the original acquisition is not what I was referring to, but rather: Microsoft's \"maintenance\" of the Halo IP after Bungie itself left Microsoft's domain, and the formation of a near-entirely new studio (343) to develop it. Ok, they didn't buy the IP at that juncture; they bought a studio to develop an IP they already owned. Use some creativity and you can see the parallels are similar enough. Second: Do you recall the keynote where Bungie unveiled Halo for the first time... at MacWorld 1999? For reference, Microsoft acquired Bungie in 2000. I've literally never heard the assertion that Halo was intended to be an RTS; my understanding is that it was originally going to be a third person FPS, as the 1999 MacWorld trailer suggests, but was changed to a first person FPS when Microsoft purchased them. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30154068,"author":"AdmiralAsshat","month":"2022-01","story":"Sony is buying Bungie, the developer of Destiny and original creator of Halo","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs personally introduced Halo.","quote":"You wouldn't think Steve Jobs would personally introduce a game that was multi-platform","text":"You'd certainly be forgiven for thinking it was a Mac exclusive. You wouldn't think Steve Jobs would personally introduce a game that was multi-platform: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23595454,"author":"musicale","month":"2020-06","story":"Why Apple ditched PowerPC, and what it says about Apple ditching Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Jobs unveiling Halo at Macworld 1999.","quote":"","text":"> The G4 Powermac came out in '99 \"We are starting to see some great games come back to the Mac, but this is one of the coolest I've ever seen...this is the first time anybody has ever seen it, the first time they've debuted it...Halo is the name of this game, and we're going to see, for the first time: Halo.\" -Steve Jobs, MacWorld 1999 [VIDEO]"},{"id":14124207,"author":"jblow","month":"2017-04","story":"The new Mac Pro: The audacity to say “Yes” in a design culture of “No”","stance":"reference","why":"Halo was originally announced at Macworld.","quote":"Halo was originally announced at MacWorld","text":"It's very sad. Halo was originally announced at MacWorld: [VIDEO] but history did not go that way...."}]},"9ziGI0i9VbE":{"wy":"HN points to it as the skeptical debunking of SpinLaunch whenever the company or similar launch concepts come up.","m":[{"id":36371780,"author":"YourCompanyHere","month":"2023-06","story":"Starlink v2 reached the brightness reduction target recommended by astronomers","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"sadly spinlaunch is bogus and the whole concept doesn't really work out.","text":"sadly spinlaunch is bogus and the whole concept doesn't really work out. [0] [0] [VIDEO] (Spinlaunch: BUSTED! by Thunderf00t)"},{"id":33751069,"author":"nix23","month":"2022-11","story":"Project Babylon","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's as brilliant and a scam as Hyperloop.","text":"It's as brilliant and a scam as Hyperloop. Balloons with a small launcher would be cheaper...but that's lame and not cool (marketing)....like normal trains instead of Hyper ;) Spinlaunch: BUSTED! 1+2 [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31032052,"author":"MongooseMan","month":"2022-04","story":"NASA will test SpinLaunch's ability to fling satellites into orbit","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Thunderf00t has already done two debunking videos on SpinLaunch.","text":"Sorry to put a dampener on things, but Thunderf00t has already done two debunking videos on SpinLaunch. He's rarely wrong. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":31032074,"author":"belter","month":"2022-04","story":"NASA will test SpinLaunch's ability to fling satellites into orbit","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Hopefully NASA watched this video","text":"Hopefully NASA watched this video, before making the contacts public instead of first examining the claims privately. \"Spinlaunch: BUSTED!\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":29433144,"author":"curiousfab","month":"2021-12","story":"Scientists find a way to ‘catapult’ rockets into space like ‘slingshots’","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is.","text":"If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is. Spinlaunch: BUSTED!: [VIDEO] Spinlaunch: BUSTED (Part 2): [LINK]"}]},"AJQ3TM-p2QI":{"wy":"Used as a joke whenever automated systems deny people without explanation or recourse.","m":[{"id":24685305,"author":"romwell","month":"2020-10","story":"Predictive policing algorithms are racist. They need to be dismantled","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"which allows the user to pass the responsibility buck onto the mysterious","text":"This is just redlining, but with \"Computer Says NO\"[1], which allows the user to pass the responsibility buck onto the mysterious \"algorithms\". The best part is that they don't even have to run the algorithm , they can just claim they did so[2] to justify their biases. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":16792515,"author":"jimnotgym","month":"2018-04","story":"America should borrow from Europe’s data-privacy law","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"being refused a mortgage because 'computer says no'[0] with no recourse or reason is tough","text":"1) You write it once and put it in your privacy policy 2) Enquiries would be from people who are a) concerned that the decision you made was unfair and would like it reviewed by a human. b) would like an explanation of the decision. Personally I welcome this. Sitting in a bank and being refused a mortgage because 'computer says no'[0] with no recourse or reason is tough. If you are profiling for marketing reasons I wonder what kind of enquiries you are expecting? [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16582632,"author":"KKKKkkkk1","month":"2018-03","story":"How Your Returns Are Used Against You at Best Buy, Other Retailers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Retail Equation sounds like they came straight out of Little Britain","text":"Retail Equation sounds like they came straight out of Little Britain's \"Computer Says No\" sketch. [VIDEO]"}]},"ErwS24cBZPc":{"wy":"Not recommended as a watch; HN cites the ad as shorthand for orderly conformity and as an example of an ad that has aged badly.","m":[{"id":44128068,"author":"PaulHoule","month":"2025-05","story":"Ask HN: Is \"Vibe Coding\" a term people are using with pride?","stance":"reference","why":"Uses Apple's 1984 ad as an image of orderly conformity","quote":"When things are orderly they feel like they are in the Apple '1984' video","text":"I think the first people to use that phrase were advocates for it. You have to remember that a lot of people in the industry have ass-backwards values. A lot of people squee when they see chaos and the more chaos the better. When things are orderly they feel like they are in the Apple '1984' video [VIDEO] It's got a lot to do with why Docker is so popular. Instead of deciding that you're going to use a certain version of libc and a certain version of Java and a certain version of MUI widgets, they take joy in having many different partitions of the system slightly different from others in ways that will cause all sort of problem large and small. If you standardized things you could run multiple tenants with 1/10 the resources you'll waste with Docker, in a just world it would mean you crush the Docker-using competition but unfortunately the Docker-users are often backed by VC which can be irrational longer than you can stay solvent."},{"id":40971557,"author":"pimlottc","month":"2024-07","story":"Seiko Originals: The UC-2000, A Smartwatch from 1984","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Imagine a smart watch, but from 1984. That sounds like something straight out of a scifi film since the 80s is not exactly known for great advances in personal computing. Now that's a ridiculous statement, especially when you're specifically talking about the year 1984... [VIDEO]"},{"id":46889176,"author":"gowld","month":"2026-02","story":"Claude Is a Space to Think","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple's ad had a woman dressed like a Hooter's waitress to represent themselves.","text":"Apple's ad had a woman dressed like a Hooter's waitress to represent themselves. That makes themselves the focus of attention. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46906009,"author":"joriJordan","month":"2026-02","story":"CIA suddenly stops publishing, removes archives of The World Factbook","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Apple's 1984 commercial didn't age well","text":"Wild. Growing up through Reagan, I saw the world only act like this. Apple's 1984 commercial didn't age well: [VIDEO] Everyone ran towards this Brave New World based on media fueled populism. To me religion isn't Christianity or Islam. It's following orders of arbitrary leaders who give themselves titles via narrative. Priest, Minister, CEO, General... just words. Provenance such as \"this is what I want to do with my life\" are poor justification for enabling it."}]},"Ij-jlF98SzA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it to contrast Apple's stated privacy stance with its later practices or to show Jobs's view on user control of data.","q":{"x":"your laptop is your diary, and they have a responsibility to that.","i":43315481,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43315481,"author":"neom","month":"2025-03","story":"Apple Exclaves","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs's belief in protecting personal data","quote":"your laptop is your diary, and they have a responsibility to that.","text":"I think Steve truly believed at his core, very simply: your laptop is your diary, and they have a responsibility to that. I don't think Tim would be CEO if he didn't believe what Steve did. It's so weird, but I really miss Steve. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40642245,"author":"al_borland","month":"2024-06","story":"Private Cloud Compute: A new frontier for AI privacy in the cloud","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on user control over data","quote":"I think this goes back to what Steve said in 2010.","text":"Thank you for mentioning this. I thought I was going crazy, because I heard this too, but kept seeing comment after comment on other sites asking if a person could choose not to use OpenAI, or that it was happening magically in the background. The way I heard it, the user was in control. I think this goes back to what Steve said in 2010. [VIDEO] And yes, while the data might not be linked to the user and striped of sensitive data, I could see people not wanting something very personal things to go to OpenAI, even if there should be no link. For example, I wouldn’t want any of my pictures going to OpenAI unless I specifically say it is OK for a given image."},{"id":32522344,"author":"nojito","month":"2022-08","story":"Apple surpassed $3.5B in annual revenue from its ad network","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Jobs 2010 on asking users before tracking","quote":"","text":"I’m surprised that people still call Apples stance a “story” All they are saying is that tracking is ok as long as you just ask the end user first. Here’s Steve Jobs in 2010 [VIDEO] Are they perfect? No. But at least they haven’t changed their stance in 12 years unlike Google et al."}]},"J691aLfkWP0":{"wy":"Cited as a nostalgic example of a CLI that orders pizza whenever HN discusses terminal-based ordering.","q":{"x":"reminds me a little of the old-school Pizza Party terminal app from the '90s","i":16044802,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":40227889,"author":"hk1337","month":"2024-05","story":"New startup sells coffee through SSH","stance":"reference","why":"CLI app that orders Dominos pizza","quote":"Reminds me of the pizza cli app that would order Domino's Pizza.","text":"Reminds me of the pizza cli app that would order Domino's Pizza. EDIT Pizza Party is what I am thinking about. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16044802,"author":"wyclif","month":"2018-01","story":"Show HN: Order pizza with random toppings","stance":"reference","why":"Old-school 90s terminal pizza ordering app","quote":"reminds me a little of the old-school Pizza Party terminal app from the '90s","text":"Concept (but not implementation) reminds me a little of the old-school Pizza Party terminal app from the '90s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13747061,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2017-02","story":"I wrote a program that emails me when one of my friends orders Domino's","stance":"reference","why":"Command line program for ordering Dominos pizza","quote":"I thought this is rather cool - Pizza Party a CLI for Domino's","text":"That seems a rather big privacy issue on Domino's part. I wonder if it also opens the doors to some kind of social engineering attack, with someone pretending to be Domino's asking for money over the phone. As an aside I thought this is rather cool - Pizza Party a CLI for Domino's [VIDEO] (One day I'd like to be able to afford to cron job something like that ;)"}]},"N3zU7sV4bJE":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as a humorous or illustrative reference to an extreme COPPA penalty.","m":[{"id":45218397,"author":"mptest","month":"2025-09","story":"I didn't bring my son to a museum to look at screens","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as footnote on regulations around kids and tech.","quote":"","text":"The real answer is the same reason younger generations grew up learning how to use excel and word and windows, a rich company found yet another way to acclimate users to their ecosystem and bypass all those pesky regulations around tech and kids[1]. They give out dirt cheap tech to schools to get buy in, they get data, users, (mostly for life, how many non stem people do you all know who explore things like the software landscape?), in short, like everything else, money is the answer. they get marketshare. Schools get to boast about their modernity. only ones losing are us 99%'rs. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36148331,"author":"FreshStart","month":"2023-06","story":"FTC Says Ring Employees Illegally Surveilled Customers, Failed to Stop Hackers","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a lethal-scale COPPA penalty.","quote":"","text":"There are different punishment classes for company. Fixed fines are only punishing small companies and are the cost of buisness for big companies priced into that buisness. (the fines appear market wide in the prices as jumps) Percentage punishments (x% of illegitimate gains) punish all companies equally and reduce interest in that market sector. Then there is lethal punishment ala Copa. [VIDEO] If not deterrable via ablative company structures or other devices, these endavours kill, usually by scaling with the growth of crime. Are these classification groups correct? Did I miss one?"},{"id":45045861,"author":"thelastgallon","month":"2025-08","story":"Beginning 1 September, we will need to geoblock Mississippi IPs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of Silicon Valley.","text":">Unfortunately, the penalties for failing to comply with the Mississippi law are incredibly steep: fines of $10,000 per user from Mississippi who we don't have identity documents verifying age for, per incident -- which means every time someone from Mississippi loaded Dreamwidth, we'd potentially owe Mississippi $10,000. Even a single $10,000 fine would be rough for us, but the per-user, per-incident nature of the actual fine structure is an existential threat. Reminds me of Silicon Valley. PiperChat has grossly violated COPPA as there was no parental consent form on the app leading to a 21 billion dollar fine: [VIDEO]"}]},"PV4Oze9JEU0":{"wy":"HN uses it as shorthand for post-scarcity economics, typically as a contrast to money-driven projects like crypto.","q":{"x":"Let's just listen to the man himself for a second","i":29557549,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":36573455,"author":"tiffanyg","month":"2023-07","story":"The Cluetrain Manifesto","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Very true. Sadly, while \"companies\" play a very large role, ultimately, it's people. The dynamics are, I think, somewhat interesting. The essential pattern appears like \"theme and variations\", woven endlessly through human history that I'm familiar with. It has been possible, in brief periods, to imagine that perhaps we had moved past some of it ... grown a bit, on the whole. But, it's really difficult to imagine we might ever reach a sort of \"Star Trek: First Contact\" ... \"economic regime\"*. Circumstances continually evolve and even relatively subtle changes in policies and principles and such, at scale, can make a big difference. One key problem is simply that the cohesion and principled behavior and all of that, borne out of challenging times** and the desire to make things better for ones offspring, so easily leads to less purposeful more entitled people who can even end up resentful at what previous generations accomplished, the philosophies they espouse, the plaudits that they have engendered... and purposefully wish to go against that grain. But, to get a bit away from this unintentionally increasingly high-falutin' \"jibber jabber\" - the phases of commercial internet development adhere to fairly typical (modern) human patterns. First: optimism and practically \"free money\" ... an initial gold rush, real attempts at building real value ultimately drowning out the charlatans and general gold rush \"carnival\". Second: a sort of plateau, \"professionalization\", coalescence, b"},{"id":29557549,"author":"Barrin92","month":"2021-12","story":"Web3 is going just great","stance":"reference","why":"Picard on post-scarcity economics versus money.","quote":"Let's just listen to the man himself for a second","text":"Instrumentalizing a show whose essential message was that living in a post-scarcity society is possible and where people pursue their interests genuinely rather than in the name of economic gain to make a case for crypto is so on brand with the actual post we're commenting on you could put it directly on the website Let's just listen to the man himself for a second, while we're talking about star trek and money: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27072872,"author":"financialize","month":"2021-05","story":"Ask HN: What's a problem you wish someone else would solve already?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Capitalism. It should have disappeared by now and been replaced with Star Trek type economics ( [VIDEO] ) and yet here we are surrounded by idiots that seek to optimize numbers in databases at the expense of everything else."}]},"THNPmhBl-8I":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke or reference when threads touch on status, difficulty or jobs.","m":[{"id":48425201,"author":"netsharc","month":"2026-06","story":"Nvidia is proposing a beast of a CPU system for Windows PCs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I found his website, [LINK] , and the \"2%\" brag is the very first sentence, geez. Being the top x% is what OnlyFans girls brag about, professor... And it's not exactly brain surgery, is it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":44495249,"author":"pcthrowaway","month":"2025-07","story":"New sphere-packing record stems from an unexpected source","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"the Rocket Scientist vs Brain Surgeon sketch","text":"There is no problem with it, but I assume there are many people who will look upon you favourably if they think you do a highly skilled job. While many of us may not care to impress those people, there are certainly those who do (possibly people with similar attitudes who care more about validation from people who think like them) A somewhat ungenerous characterization of the attitude may be something like the Rocket Scientist vs Brain Surgeon sketch - [VIDEO] But we should also acknowledge that there's an entire culture built around valuing people and their time relative to one's perception of their \"importance\", that this culture can influence one's earning potential and acquisition of material possessions, and that many people do care about things like \"seeming important\" or moving upwards in this hierarchy as a result."},{"id":37036579,"author":"kowlo","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the coolest physical thing you've made?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I made a VESA mount for my iPad [1]. It wasn't exactly brain surgery [2], but I thought it was cool! [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37038218,"author":"batterylow","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: What's the coolest physical thing you've made?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I made a VESA mount for my iPad [1]. It wasn't exactly brain surgery [2], but I thought it was cool! [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35523596,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2023-04","story":"Stop Telling Everyone What You Do for a Living","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's a reference to a Mitchell and Webb sketch about the status games that people play around employment.","text":"It's a reference to a Mitchell and Webb sketch about the status games that people play around employment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28470637,"author":"effingwewt","month":"2021-09","story":"If You’re So Successful, Why Are You Still Working 70 Hours a Week?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"infamous That Mitchell and Webb Look sketch =)","text":"His point was he has the skills. I made the point about bootcamps, skills are easily obtained. Implying the janitor could not gain the skills is insane. Thinking a SE could begin a janitorial job tomorrow and do it as well as the janitor is laughable. SE skills are just a bit harder to come by, they aren't special. If your skills can be learned in a 6 week course you are not an astrophysicist nor a brain surgeon. SEs are paid well and so have an overinflated self-value. Again, now that this is crashing with WFH. We have code chop shops in India, yet we still try and pretend coding is some modern-day magic language. ninja edit- hit reply b4 finished 2nd edit- reminded me of this [1] infamous That Mitchell and Webb Look sketch =) [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21109505,"author":"kbenson","month":"2019-09","story":"Starship Prototype Unveiled","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Now I can't help remembering a sketch from Mitchell and Webb.","text":"That's the best description. \"Joe over there? Oh, he's a rocket surgeon.\" Now I can't help remembering a sketch from Mitchell and Webb.[1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19956993,"author":"neilv","month":"2019-05","story":"Tech Jobs Lead to the Middle Class, But Not for the Masses","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't think plumber vs. theoretical physics post-doc is a great example. Plumbing is critical skilled work, taking years to master, and is paid accordingly. I imagine plumbing is also very stressful, at times, such as when you're being careful not to flood a building, or avoiding breaking something that would require ripping open a floor to fix. Though plumbing guild training sounds not entirely different than academia. (And postdocs tend to get paid poo because the market, and their guild, permit it.) We should also be careful of to avoid the oopses of: \"Brain Surgeon - That Mitchell & Webb Look, Series 3 - BBC Two\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":16757836,"author":"jwbensley","month":"2018-04","story":"Half of European flights delayed due to system failure","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It was in reference to That Mitchell and Webb Look","text":"It's very common that someone hasn't heard of everything. It's why down votes without explanation are bad, like on StackOverflow. It was in reference to That Mitchell and Webb Look: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14033897,"author":"dom0","month":"2017-04","story":"Everything Is Broken (2014)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have a free laugh","text":"We already have people that perform the software equivalent of rocket brain surgery... ANFSCD: Have a free laugh: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13622609,"author":"AnkhMorporkian","month":"2017-02","story":"Takeover.sh – Wipe and reinstall a running Linux system via SSH without reboot","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I'm just going to leave this here for anyone who wants a good laugh for their morning.","text":"I don't tend to link youtube videos on HN, particularly of the comedic type, but I'm just going to leave this here for anyone who wants a good laugh for their morning. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8525529,"author":"louthy","month":"2014-10","story":"Orbital Sciences Antares rocket explodes after liftoff","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"My favourite of those.","text":"> We make jokes about how nearly nothing is \"rocket science\" or \"brain surgery\" for a reason [VIDEO] My favourite of those."},{"id":5616942,"author":"losethos","month":"2013-04","story":"Obsession","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] X_dot = A X + B U Y = C X + D U Rocket science I did simstructure -- rocketscience My master's degree is rocket science [LINK] Snort: you'll notice that little course called \"nonlinear control systems\" with the A after it? That's as far as you can go in math. Nonlinear differential equations. Ask any mathmetician. He'll tell you nonlinear differential equations is the end of the road."},{"id":659894,"author":"axod","month":"2009-06","story":"Are Silicon Valley Startups More Likely To Be Acquired?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Off topic, but very funny","text":"Yeah you'd have to be a rocket scientist to be able to look down the list and find the biggest numbers... Off topic, but very funny: [VIDEO]"}]},"TsNc4nEX3c4":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke about Tesla's overpromised robotics and AI claims.","m":[{"id":48005374,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-05","story":"First Tesla Semi Rolls Off High-Volume Production Line","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I've seen Tesla's fake robots too.","text":"I've seen Tesla's fake robots too. Maybe they can pay people who lost their jobs to DOGE to dress up in Spandex and dance like robots to replace the batteries. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46069368,"author":"rvnx","month":"2025-11","story":"AGI is not possible even in 10 years","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It's plausible, in 2026, we are going to send to Mars fully autonomous humanoid robots, so AGI is right around the corner. [VIDEO]"},{"id":43734902,"author":"spwa4","month":"2025-04","story":"Tesla brand damage is destroying used car value","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Long FSD delays are no secret, but can we at least get the historical timeline correct? On one hand I'm tempted to say: \"yes of course\". On the other hand I feel like saying: even a slight amount of accuracy would be very out of place for claims involving Tesla, wouldn't it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":40383983,"author":"belter","month":"2024-05","story":"Hertz sold a bunch of Teslas and it turns out they're all kinds of trouble","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"They are currently hiring man in spandex.","text":"> upcoming robotaxis? They are currently hiring man in spandex. Edit: My bad, they will not hire, they will reuse this one: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38312220,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-11","story":"OpenAI Announces Leadership Transition","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Silicon Valley is a comedy, and that was a joke, obviously. But you can't deny there's a striking resemblance between Ariel & Fiona, and Ben & Sophia! That's why Silicon Valley was such a great show: they did their research. The entire final storyline is about an AI trying to take over -- if you haven't watched it, you should! But many of my friends who live and work in Silicon Valley can't stand watching it, because it strikes too close to home, not because it isn't funny. I think it's much more likely that Elon Musk fucked a robot, after having mistaken it for a human being in a robot suit. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34260960,"author":"ceejayoz","month":"2023-01","story":"Investors conclude that Tesla is a carmaker, not a tech firm","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"see the video of the 2021 reveal (a human in a catsuit)","text":"In case the sarcasm above isn't clear, see the video of the 2021 reveal (a human in a catsuit): [VIDEO] And the 2022 re-reveal (in which humans have to push it onto the stage): [LINK]"}]},"VreG1iC65Lc":{"wy":"Used as a concrete example of modern car assembly, with short cycle times and heavy robotic automation, in manufacturing and automation threads.","q":{"x":"Here's the Mercedes A-class assembly line.[1] Cycle time is about 40 seconds.","i":11639686,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":14900507,"author":"bildung","month":"2017-08","story":"Mercedes kills their only electric car","stance":"reference","why":"Shows robotic car factory scale","quote":"Have a look at this video","text":"> Except that the 10 million number does not matter if their entire system is set up to make exploding-fuel cars. The only thing common is the external shell and the dash electronics. The real expertise and resource is needed to make the rest of the stuff. And Tesla is ahead in that. It's all about logistics, infrastructure and capital. Have a look at this video: [VIDEO] Switching to batteries essentially just means reprogramming those robots. > Please point me to a battery factory this big, set up exclusively to supply batteries for one EV model. Please point me to a factory that actually produces 35GWh/a ;)"},{"id":13234456,"author":"credit_guy","month":"2016-12","story":"The Long-Term Jobs Killer Is Not China, It’s Automation","stance":"reference","why":"Robots working in a car factory","quote":"Here's a youtube movie of some robots in a Mercedes factory","text":"I think it's likely that automation will be a huge creator of jobs. And I'm not talking about creation of high skill jobs at the expense of destruction of low skill jobs. I'm talking about a net creation of both low and high skill jobs. Allow me to explain. Here's a youtube movie of some robots in a Mercedes factory [VIDEO] There are thousands of movies like that. Lots of robots doing a lot of work, and apparently displacing lots of jobs. But there are some jobs in the background. Someone has programmed those robots. But aren't those programmers (super-)highly skilled? I don't know for sure, but it feels to me a robot is programed like an Excel macro: you record the moves, and then edit what you recorded. For an Excel macro, both parts are done (generally) by the same person, but for industrial robots, they are probably done by different people. The person who is being recorded needs only know how to manipulate a mechanical arm with a controller, that is probably not very different from a PS4 controller. The person who edits the macros, creates procedures, then combines them, optimizes them, creates tests, etc, etc, that person is a highly skilled individual. The other one not so much. The difference here is the capacity for abstraction. Now, one could say that both these jobs happen only once, and then the robot performs the jobs thousands of times, so you have 2 new jobs (one high skill, one low skill) displacing thousands others. Alas, the programming, maintenance, re-prog"},{"id":11639686,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-05","story":"Tesla announces new Model 3 production plans","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a car assembly line with fixtures and short cycle time","quote":"Here's the Mercedes A-class assembly line.[1] Cycle time is about 40 seconds.","text":"It's not unusual. Here's the Mercedes A-class assembly line.[1] Cycle time is about 40 seconds. That's 720 cars in an 8 hour shift. It's not unusual to run two shifts of production, and do maintenance on the third. In some highly automated plants, maintenance is in the daytime, and production is at night, with few people around. That's more for small parts than entire cars. Production line design and tuning is all about cycle time. The line runs at the speed of the slowest station. Any operation that takes too long has to be broken up into successive operations, even if one station could do it. You can fan out the line to multiple stations if you absolutely have to have a long cycle, but that adds cost, takes up floor space, and is really tough to retrofit. It's not unusual, though, to redesign the slowest station if that will speed up the whole line. Take a look at that Mercedes assembly line. Note all the custom fixtures required, many with sensor and actuators attached. All those have to be designed, built, tested, and run through enough cycles that they behave reliably. One failure stops the whole line. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"WWKG6ZmgAX4":{"wy":"Cited as an example of a joke about violence that could be stripped of context, or as a comparison when other politicians' jokes are criticized.","q":{"x":"here's Obama unambiguously joking about drone-murdering the Jonas Brothers","i":12258425,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":36431581,"author":"netsharc","month":"2023-06","story":"Elon Musk and Mark Zuckerberg agree to hold cage fight","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a joke that could be turned into a misleading headline.","quote":"because Obama joked about it","text":"Bate? Bait? It's not even accurate news, only after a few paragraphs (BuzzFeed style paragraphs where each of them contain 1 sentence) does it say \"He later tweeted short videos of walruses, perhaps suggesting his challenge to Mr Zuckerberg may not entirely be serious.\". I don't think they agreed to do anything. This is almost on the level of publishing the headline \"Obama threatens to kill Jonas brothers with predator drones\" because Obama joked about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12258425,"author":"pkinsky","month":"2016-08","story":"Trump’s assassination joke isn’t just a threat to Secretary Clinton","stance":"reference","why":"Obama joking about drone-killing the Jonas Brothers for comparison.","quote":"here's Obama unambiguously joking about drone-murdering the Jonas Brothers","text":"I've been reading the word unprecedented a lot today. For reference, here's Obama unambiguously joking about drone-murdering the Jonas Brothers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9473367,"author":"patzerhacker","month":"2015-05","story":"Ask HN: Do you think there's a possibility Obama will pardon Snowden?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"joking about droning more American citizens","text":">Obama's constitutional law idealism may have been severely corrupted by the pressures of his current role, but it's still there. Really? I have two words for you: Predator drones[1]. The sitting president of the United States, who has actually ordered drone strikes against American citizens who surely have Constitutional rights, joking about droning more American citizens. Whatever bit of constitutional law idealism was there was either an act or long since gone. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"XPGgTy5YJ-g":{"wy":"Posted as a joke or reference whenever a product starts showing more ads.","m":[{"id":41656049,"author":"ta1243","month":"2024-09","story":"LG TVs Start Showing Ads on Screensavers","stance":"reference","why":"Futurama commented on ad saturation a quarter century ago","quote":"of course Futurama had comentary on this a quarter of a century ago","text":"Double dipping with adverts is the way. Amazon, Spotify, dropped them all after they started pushing adverts as well as getting their monthly cost. It's not new -- cable/sky TV was exactly the same (although sky one was the other way round - started with adverts, then added a subscription fee in 1993 [0]) The far bigger problem is that the majority of people accept adverts. They don't consider them a cost. Last time I got a yellow cab in New York it was adverts on a TV screen in the back. I get ubers if I need a cab now. The future of adverts was accurately predicted in idiocracy [1], and of course Futurama had comentary on this a quarter of a century ago [2] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34919514,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2023-02","story":"The FBI now recommends using an ad blocker when searching the web","stance":"reference","why":"Futurama predicted ubiquitous advertising decades ago","quote":"It's only been predictable for the last 20 years...","text":"Google and Meta seem to have no idea what's going on within their own advertising networks. Similarly to all the stories (with two currently in the front page of HN, eBay and PayPal) about algorithms that are just insufficient for the range of realistic scenarios these companies must deal with on a regular basis. It's merely the equation of profit outweighing customer service. Admittedly, they're working on a scale that's difficult to comprehend, but that shouldn't absolve them of aiding and abetting criminal use of their systems. Google's and Meta's profit motives are the base cause of this continuing escalation of the ubiquity and user-hostility (to put it mildly) of internet advertising. It's only been predictable for the last 20 years... [LINK] (first aired in March 2000) [VIDEO] (April 1999)"},{"id":40535213,"author":"tialaramex","month":"2024-05","story":"Brave launches Search Ads in key markets after successful test phase","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Advertising is potentially useful, it's the scale that's a problem. [VIDEO] Like, \"Every inch of the new park is covered in sponsor branding\" is a problem, but \"I had no idea that a store selling towels opened in our town\" is also a problem. These are both advertising, at some point you cross from \"I didn't know about that, thanks\" to \"Just fuck off already\" and that's too much advertising. Dispepsi for example, the Negativland album, is premised on the idea that everybody on the entire planet already knows about Coca-Cola and Pepsi, so their continued advertising is entirely futile. Nobody who wants a brown fizzy beverage is unsure whether they could obtain such a thing or what it might be called. So all they're doing is annoying us."},{"id":19053607,"author":"dbg31415","month":"2019-02","story":"New Technology Uses Lasers to Transmit Audible Messages to Specific People","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"* Futurama - S01E06 - Didn't you have ads in the 20th century? - YouTube || [VIDEO] Leela: \"Didn't you have ads in the 20th century?\" Fry: \"Well sure, but not in our dreams! Only on tv and radio...and in magazines...and movies. And at ball games, on buses, and milk cartons, and t-shirts, and bananas, and written on the sky. But not in dreams! No sirree.\""},{"id":18072623,"author":"IvyMike","month":"2018-09","story":"Product Updates Based on Your Feedback","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of Futurama's commentary on \"Ads in the 20th century","text":"Reminds me of Futurama's commentary on \"Ads in the 20th century\": [VIDEO] \"Only on TV and radio, and in magazines, and movies, and at ball games... and on buses and milk cartons and t-shirts, and bananas and written on the sky.\" (And that was in 1999. The 2018 version would have to be extended: \"And in our email, and in the Windows start menu, and in our phones, and on shopping carts and subway turnstiles, and maps, and video games, and in photo albums, and even inside of other ads. And the ads watched you as much as you watched them. But not our dreams.\")"},{"id":15189756,"author":"ge0rg","month":"2017-09","story":"HTC 10 keyboard suddenly starts showing ads, fix is on the way","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Original source (with screenshot): [LINK] That absolutely reminded me of the \"Didn't you have ads in the 20th century?\" Futurama scene: [VIDEO]"}]},"ZVYqB0uTKlE":{"wy":"Shared as a joke or classic illustration of government process and strategy in unrelated political threads.","m":[{"id":45212581,"author":"noirscape","month":"2025-09","story":"Germany is not supporting ChatControl – blocking minority secured","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The goal behind the EU is to represent Europe as a single unified economic bloc capable of being a world power. It's not meant to make the European Union into a superstate. You can pretty directly tie this as a natural consequence of most of Europe's colonial empires falling; without the extra resources the colonies brought in, Europe would've risked being run under by both the US, Russia and nowadays China. The goal of the EU is to essentially find agreement between 27 member states to do things that all those states agree are things they want to do. Actually federalizing the EU wouldn't work simply because Europeans are too different from one another; it's a cooperation between countries that spend most of their history being in varying degrees of \"dislike\" to \"waging war\" on each other, and while most people agree war is bad these days, those cultural differences have never gone away[0]. Trying to create a mono-EU \"national identity\" wouldn't work, the same way that most Americans find a shared national identity in well, \"being American\". Probably the most topical example for HN would be tech antitrust legislation. If any one European country tried to pass tech antitrust laws with teeth, it'd be trivial for those companies to just... stop providing services to that country. Most European countries are too small to make a meaningful dent, and a few actions \"to prove a point\", will lead to a chilling effect. It'd lead to a copy of the US's current tech dystopia where you don"},{"id":45361602,"author":"testdelacc1","month":"2025-09","story":"Rights groups urge UK PM Starmer to abandon plans for mandatory digital ID","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don’t find what either side is saying here very convincing. Anti-ID “it will change our relationship with the state, cause irreversible damage to our civil liberties and fail to deter illegal immigration”. They say it will lead to “frequent identity checks as we navigate our daily lives”. That last part isn’t true, for sure. I’ve lived in a country with a digital ID and you had to verify your identity a few times a year at most - submitting taxes, opening bank accounts etc. They’re exaggerating here. The pro-ID group is even less convincing, if that can be imagined. Tony Blair, former PM has been batting for this for ages - Digital ID is the Disruption the UK Desperately Needs ( [LINK] ). And it all sounds like snake oil to someone who is even a little tech inclined. He’s promising all kinds of things, like the UK will see dramatically higher growth. I’ve seen India before and after Aadhaar. It helped, but it didn’t fundamentally remake the economy. UPI did do that, but it’s not dependent on Aadhaar. And the government wants to show they’re cracking down on illegal immigration so this is an easy win. But it will take more than 5 years to roll out and by then the PM would be out of a job. Even if it could be rolled out to 70 million people in 2 years (that’s 100k people per day ), it wouldn’t have all these incredible benefits. As for illegal immigration, it would catch a few but not all. The technical issue is that while you can issue everyone an ID, it would take much, muc"},{"id":41977253,"author":"alephnerd","month":"2024-10","story":"TI expands internal manufacturing for gallium nitride (GAN) semiconductors","stance":"reference","why":"Classic scene illustrating blunting strategy.","quote":"this classic scene from","text":"The impact of BRICS is really overstated, and is only really discussed on Reddit and HN (because Reddit). In action it's been blunted because of China and India's rivalry, with each country vetoing expansion of nations that are fully lopsided one way or the other. It's not different from the SCO or APEC in that sense. If you want to learn further about blunting in IR I highly recommend reading Rush Doshi and Oriana Skylar Mastro's research. Alternatively, this classic scene from \"Yes Minister\" also pretty much explains this strategy - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31151489,"author":"gizajob","month":"2022-04","story":"UK-Morocco 10GW Renewable Electricity Interconnector Planned","stance":"reference","why":"A humorous take on British government process.","quote":"","text":"I feel you're misreading what I said, and just seeing your own angle in it. \"They took our jobs\" isn't exactly true, but what has happened is that wages stayed low and conditions got worse due to capitalism's demand for lower costs and greater efficiency. So a source of cheap labour from Eastern Europe only served that process and made it such that companies didn't have to invest in people locally, because anyone could come in and do the job, and in the case of people from Eastern Europe, our minimum wages were higher than those provided by skilled professions. (I worked with a guy whose mother was the head nurse of a hospital in Hungary, and on a wage of £400 per month, so he was earning a small fortune in comparison even though that came at the cost of working to hours and conditions that UK workers would reject). So levelling all the blame for this phenomenon at UK politicians is moot because there is really little they could do about it, the EU comes with complete freedom of movement, which many in the UK saw as not really providing them with any benefit while coming with big downsides that affect them on a daily basis. > That is basically the failure of UK politicians, too. But, as Yes, Minister very rightly put it, perhaps the UK wasn't in there for any kind of unity to begin with: [VIDEO] I'm well aware of Yes, Minister. While it gives a humourous take on the eccentricity and backwardness of our process, my point nevertheless stands. The UK has long-since developed a s"},{"id":31151111,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-04","story":"UK-Morocco 10GW Renewable Electricity Interconnector Planned","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The UK wasn't a founding member of the EU Ah, my mistake. I was pretty sure the UK was a founding member. > I feel that many in the UK regard it's parliament as the highest authority in law- and decision-making. Any body resting higher than that is going to face difficulty when attempting to claim greater and greater control over laws, and running them from \"far away\" even if that far away place was Brussels. That is basically the failure of UK politicians, too. But, as Yes, Minister very rightly put it, perhaps the UK wasn't in there for any kind of unity to begin with: [VIDEO] :) > The UK also wasn't part of the Euro, and so one of the main benefits of EU membership and coherence was missing The UK isn't the only country without the Euro though. > amongst the working classes that the low-wage sector was being undermined by labour coming in freely from abroad, mainly eastern Europe, and this wasn't reciprocal, i.e. they couldn't go to eastern Europe, or basically anywhere else in Europe, and get the same benefit from doing so. \"They took our jobs\" is also a failure of the politicians. Though, for them it's not a failure, it's votes for the next election. --- In the end, \"the EU wants us to be subservient to undemocratic bureaucracy\" ends up being \"there was a general feeling perpetuated and encouraged by our own politicians and that doesn't really have much to do with reality\" ;)"}]},"dWAO3vUn7nw":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it as an example of sponsored content, with critics calling it FUD about battery electric cars, though one commenter noted it as a comparison.","m":[{"id":28842969,"author":"godelski","month":"2021-10","story":"Nuclear Power in France","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> You can claim that todays designs are 99.9999999% safe, but there is no such thing as 100%. I'm not sure how you wrote this and took it seriously. Should I never leave the house? How do I eat? Eating is more dangerous than that. We can't just live in a bubble. There is never perfect safety in anything you will do in life. You'll always have to make risk/reward trade offs. Historically nuclear shows the safest option. > Nuclear waste is difficult to deal with. Our current strategies include finding a secret underground cave, and hoping no one accidentally stumbles onto it. Not to mention we have no plan for dealing with widespread use of nuclear power and the waste that would produce. You seem to be uninformed of how these work. If you care to learn more I suggest reading here[0]. I bet you're also overestimating how much waste there is. Let's look at the total waste of decades worth of France[1] and Russian[2] waste. That's the entire amount of waste since the nuclear programs started till now. France also recycles most of its waste which greatly reduces the rate of waste. Storage also doesn't have to be as long as you think. We tend to have a few orders of safety in nuclear safety. You're also not being fair in your comparisons. You're acting like strip mining and dredging for rare earth materials isn't doing crazy damage to our environment too. Pretending that these also don't pose a significant waste problem as well. Like the previous response, there is a waste tradeoff."},{"id":28268245,"author":"nnutter","month":"2021-08","story":"Hydrogen lobbyist quits, slams oil companies’ “false claims” about blue hydrogen","stance":"reference","why":"Compares hydrogen fuel cells and batteries; both have a place","quote":"Physics Girl just made a video comparing hydrogen fuel cell and batteries.","text":"Physics Girl just made a video comparing hydrogen fuel cell and batteries. The weight to energy ratio makes it extremely clear to me that there is a place for both. As far as blue hydrogen vs. green hydrogen I don’t know the details. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31719451,"author":"paranoidrobot","month":"2022-06","story":"European pension funds attack Toyota for not going ‘all in’ on electric cars","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this Toyota-sponsored video series from Diana Cowern (Physics Girl) is full of FUD and half-truths about BEVs","text":"Concept vehicles are really nothing more than a marketing effort. They don't represent an actual plan to produce any of these vehicles. Toyota has a single production BEV, which is due to be released \"Real Soon Now\" in the US, and next year in Australia. But they're only going to make about 7,000 of them[1] this year. They are still heavily pushing their fuel-cell vehicles, along with FUD about how BEVs will cause the electric grid to melt down if everyone owns a BEV and all plug it in at once. For example, this Toyota-sponsored video series from Diana Cowern (Physics Girl) is full of FUD and half-truths about BEVs, while neatly side-stepping the reality that all the cheap sources of Hydrogen are from LNG. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] There's a whole bunch of videos in the series and it's too long for me to go through and pick out individual quotes, but these two videos are IIRC the main ones: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28975538,"author":"emsign","month":"2021-10","story":"SmarterEveryDay's 4privacy can, and cannot, meet its goals","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Physics Girl did a video in cooperation with Toyota praising Toyota's hydrogen powered test car","text":"> Where I get uneasy is how quickly SmarterEveryDay seems to have > switched from a YouTube channel about explaining things into a > YouTube channel about stoking \"Big Tech\" fears as a way to sell > apps. The latest video is title \"Is Your Privacy An Illusion? > (Taking on Big Tech) - Smarter Every Day 263\". This has been a trend with YouTube science communicators and educators for a while now. They're getting hired by companies to basically produce an ad in the style of their regular videos. Physics Girl did a video in cooperation with Toyota praising Toyota's hydrogen powered test car, telling her audience that hydrogen as a passenger car fuel has a future while ignoring the fact that every car manufacturer including Toyota has given up on it and favoring Lithium battery packs instead. [LINK] It was an odd move from her to hype Hydrpgen cars but her audience caught on to it quickly in the comments. Which prompted her to make a follow up video: [VIDEO] where she's trying to save what's left to save for the hydrogen cause. Veritasium did it too. In which Derek Muller created nothing short of a propaganda piece for driverless cars and not even just an ad for the Waymo service in particular. [LINK] This gives me the same vibe as those doctors promoting cigarettes smoking against coughs in the 1950s and 1960s: Here's a professional often someone who is highly regarded as being objective and impartial who is basically leaving out crucial information to push something either a pro"}]},"hWiBt-pqp0E":{"wy":"HN quotes it in threads about civil liberties and government overreach for its line about rights being privileges.","q":{"x":"rights aren’t rights if someone can take em away. They’re privileges.","i":3402155,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":8945538,"author":"sarciszewski","month":"2015-01","story":"I Was Arrested for Learning a Foreign Language. Today, I Have Some Closure","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> What you describe is doing everything you can in order to try to avoid a situation in which you might be required to flex your rights. I like to think of it as maintaining a preemptive strategy. I don't like this, but the world we live in is unfair and full of assholes who don't play by their own rules. > How can you describe it as a strategy for \"making sure your rights are respected\"? A man who faces no wars carries no battle scars. > It is on its face an attempt to make the question of whether you have rights or not a no-impact detail. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3402155,"author":"icebraining","month":"2011-12","story":"'TSA Arrests Me for Using the Fourth Amendment as a Weapon'","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin's routine arguing rights are only temporary privileges.","quote":"rights aren’t rights if someone can take em away. They’re privileges.","text":"To quote George Carlin (RIP): Now, if you think you do have rights, I have one last assignment for ya. Next time you’re at the computer get on the Internet, go to Wikipedia. When you get to Wikipedia, in the search field for Wikipedia, i want to type in, “Japanese-Americans 1942″ and you’ll find out all about your precious fucking rights. Alright. You know about it. In 1942 there were 110,000 Japanese-American citizens, in good standing, law abiding people, who were thrown into internment camps simply because their parents were born in the wrong country. That’s all they did wrong. They had no right to a lawyer, no right to a fair trial, no right to a jury of their peers, no right to due process of any kind. The only right they had was…right this way! Into the internment camps. Just when these American citizens needed their rights the most…their government took them away. and rights aren’t rights if someone can take em away. They’re privileges. That’s all we’ve ever had in this country is a bill of TEMPORARY privileges; and if you read the news, even badly, you know the list gets shorter, and shorter, and shorter. Yep, sooner or later the people in this country are going to realize the government doesn’t give a fuck about them. the government doesn’t care about you, or your children, or your rights, or your welfare or your safety. it simply doesn’t give a fuck about you. It’s interested in it’s own power. That’s the only thing…keeping it, and expanding wherever possible. The w"},{"id":1111868,"author":"scythe","month":"2010-02","story":"FIXME: zero ideas in the dept. of education's open government idea board","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Please stop mincing words; you know as well as I do what the phrase \"free and compulsory education\" refers to. Yes, it's tax-funded. No, taxes do not constitute enslavement . (And before you mention that you said \"payment or enslavement of others\", let's both agree to admit that you included the word \"enslavement\" solely for dramatic effect.) I'm not going to play the \"human rights\" game, because you have no rights [1]. I'm going to say that providing education to everyone has been proven time and again to be good for society [2]. The attitude of \"oh well it's not a right\" is perhaps the greatest piece of sophistry I've been subjected to in some time, in that the definition of a \"right\" is entirely subjective and consequently useless. It is a right as much as the freedom of speech, in that it's provision helps to provide a basic framework that is necessary for a free society. An uneducated populace cannot perform simple democratic tasks, like, say, voting. Speaking of which, why is voting a right? Doesn't counting several million ballots require \"significant effort\"? I certainly couldn't do it in an afternoon! Are you \"enslaved\" by having to pay for people to count ballots? [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}]},"ktE_3PrJZO0":{"wy":"HN cites it for its claims about NIS bribery of defectors and for defectors who say they want to go home.","m":[{"id":29830801,"author":"drekk","month":"2022-01","story":"Tips to grow your North Korean Startup","stance":"reference","why":"Locally produced documentary on defectors","quote":"There are also documentaries produced locally","text":"One thing I find more odd is how American audiences treat the DPRK while also supporting the continued military puppet state that is the ROK. There were also a \"lot of sources\" supporting WMDs in Iraq. When what you're saying is the state department narrative it's not going to receive pushback. The US killed 20% of Koreans on the peninsula and reduced it all to rubble. Forgive my skepticism at the \"humanitarian intentions\". Especially when there are financial incentives to become a defector [0] There are also documentaries produced locally [1] and you can very well go on Weibo yourself and speak to North Koreans. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27648433,"author":"kleton","month":"2021-06","story":"Teaching open-source software in North Korea","stance":"reference","why":"Accidental defectors who say they want to go home","quote":"There are a few of these people who say they want to go home.","text":"There are some people who \"defected\" accidentally. By happenstance they visited family in China expecting to return but got tricked by human traffickers and ended up in South Korea as defectors. There are a few of these people who say they want to go home. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26910227,"author":"mrobot","month":"2021-04","story":"The Rise of North Korea's Hacking Army","stance":"reference","why":"Alleged bribery of defectors by South Korean intelligence","quote":"contains some accusations of bribery of defectors and other shady business by NIS","text":"Here is a video that contains some accusations of bribery of defectors and other shady business by NIS (Korean CIA) [VIDEO]"}]},"zihTWh5i2C4":{"wy":"Linked in threads about Kurzweil joining Google, mostly as a source for his exponential progress charts and his ambitions there.","q":{"x":"intro by Peter Norvig to a Google Talks event with Ray from a month ago","i":5010394,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5869798,"author":"cadalac","month":"2013-06","story":"Google Chrome LOTR easter egg","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzweil presentation hinting at Google's ambitions.","quote":"I watched a presentation by Ray Kurzweil at Google","text":"Two rings to rule 'em all. (One for Larry, one for Sergey.) All joking aside, I watched a presentation by Ray Kurzweil at Google and it's pretty (what's the word?) what they're up to. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5004468,"author":"rpm4321","month":"2013-01","story":"Ray Kurzweil Says He’ll Get ‘Unlimited Resources’ to Work on AI at Google","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"God damn it... Not this again. =================MY PREVIOUS POSTS================= I don't think that's a very fair assessment of Kurzweil's role in technology. He was on the ground, getting his hands dirty with the first commercial applications of AI. He made quite a bit of money selling his various companies and technologies, and was awarded the presidential Medal of Technology from Clinton. As I was growing up, there was a series of \"Oh wow!\" moments I had, associated with computers and the seemingly sci-fi things they were now capable of. \"Oh wow, computers can read printed documents and recognize the characters!\" \"Oh wow, computers can read written text aloud!\" \"Oh wow, computers can recognize speech!\" \"Oh wow, computer synthesizers can sound just like pianos now!\" I didn't realize until much later that Kurzweil was heavily involved with all of those breakthroughs. =========================================== In addition, I'd rank Minsky, Larry Page, Bill Gates, Dean Kamen, Peter, Norvig, Rafael Reif, Tomaso Poggio, Dileep George, and Kurzweil's other supporters as much more qualified to judge the merits of his ideas, than Kurzweil's detractors like Hofstadter, Kevin Kelly, Mitch Kapor, and Gary Marcus. It seems that Hofstadter is the only one of that group who is really qualified to render a verdict. [LINK] ===================================== Here's Peter Norvig himself on Kurzweil: “Ray’s contributions to science and technology, through research in character and speec"},{"id":5010394,"author":"rpm4321","month":"2013-01","story":"Ask PG: Is Ray Kurzweil full of it?","stance":"reference","why":"Peter Norvig introduces Kurzweil's Google talk.","quote":"intro by Peter Norvig to a Google Talks event with Ray from a month ago","text":"Hey Paul, thanks for responding. Just in case you’re interested, this Time cover story on him from a couple of years back actually does a reasonably good job on the broad strokes: [LINK] as does this NY Times one: [LINK] - there's also this intro by Peter Norvig to a Google Talks event with Ray from a month ago: [VIDEO] The basic idea is that exponential growth in tech will result in strong AI, advanced nanotech, biotech, and neural interfaces that will lead to a near utopian civilization (if things go smoothly) in less than forty years. As outlandish as it seems, he actually makes a fairly convincing, well-researched case, and has had a pretty impressive career as an inventor and tech prognosticator. Needless to say, he’s a very polarizing figure."},{"id":4903459,"author":"mtgx","month":"2012-12","story":"Jimmy Wales: $50 Android smartphones are disrupting Africa","stance":"reference","why":"Graph showing technology's effect on wealth and living conditions.","quote":"Here's Ray Kurzweil showing a graph from 1800 till recently","text":"How can you call people getting access to technology for the first time \"stagnation\"? It's the exact opposite of that. Here's Ray Kurzweil showing a graph from 1800 till recently that demonstrates how technology has had a direct effect on the wealth of the nations and on the average \"conditions of living\" for everyone: [VIDEO] Also a relevant bit from Louis CK on how we take technology for granted at every step: [LINK] longer audio version: [LINK]"},{"id":4924879,"author":"nnq","month":"2012-12","story":"Ray Kurzweil joins Google","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"gotta love this guy","text":"gotta love this guy: > 1%... you're pretty much finished... try that with product submission schedules [1] ...so now we know who to blame for future Google product delays. [1]: [VIDEO] EDIT: added the source link"}]}});
